window.JOURNAL_INDEX = [{"id": "paper-2610-03143", "arxiv": "2610.03143", "title": "First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande", "authors": "K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration", "primary_category": "hep-ex", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos at approximately six sigma with gadolinium neutron tagging, enabling an internal comparison with its solar-neutrino measurements.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03143", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration 2610.03143 Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos at approximately six sigma with gadolinium neutron tagging, enabling an internal comparison with its solar-neutrino measurements. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. First observation of electron antineutrinos from nuclear reactors at Super-Kamiokande\n\n**K. Abe et al. — Super-Kamiokande Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.03143](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03143)  \n**New experimental result**\n\nUsing **411.52 live days** from two gadolinium-loaded operating phases, Super-Kamiokande observes reactor antineutrinos through inverse beta decay with delayed neutron tagging. The prompt-energy spectrum and time-dependent rates favor an oscillated reactor contribution over the no-reactor hypothesis at approximately **\\(6\\sigma\\)**.\n\nThe reactor-only fit gives\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2\\theta_{12}=0.500\\pm0.155,\\qquad\n\\Delta m_{21}^{2}\n=\\left(9.08^{+0.55}_{-0.54}\\right)\\times10^{-5}\\ {\\rm eV^2}.\n\\]\n\nA joint comparison with Super-K’s solar-neutrino measurement yields a parameter-goodness-of-fit discrepancy of **\\(1.9\\sigma\\)**—not significant evidence for different neutrino and antineutrino oscillation parameters. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03143v1))\n\n##### History\n\nThis is the first reactor-antineutrino observation **in Super-Kamiokande**, not the first in a water Cherenkov detector. [SNO+ previously reported \\(3.5\\sigma\\) evidence using pure water](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14154), with its nearest reactors approximately 240 km away. The new result establishes the practical reactor sensitivity of SK-Gd and enables a solar–reactor comparison within the same detector. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14154))\n\nThe principal advance is therefore experimental capability and an internally comparable dataset. The reactor mixing-angle uncertainty remains substantial, and the analysis depends on accidental-background estimation, neutron-tagging efficiency, and reactor-spectrum modeling. The quoted solar–reactor mismatch does not support a CPT-violation claim. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03143v1))"}, {"id": "paper-v4641-sgr", "arxiv": "2610.02966", "title": "XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr", "authors": "Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Gamma rays", "Compact objects", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "XMM-Newton resolves extended nonthermal X-ray structures around V4641 Sgr, favoring localized electron acceleration while leaving a hadronic gamma-ray component possible.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-v4641-sgr", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al. 2610.02966 XMM-Newton resolves extended nonthermal X-ray structures around V4641 Sgr, favoring localized electron acceleration while leaving a hadronic gamma-ray component possible. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 X rays Gamma rays Compact objects Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. V4641 Sgr: two complementary detections of extended X-ray emission\n\n**XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr**  \n**Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02966](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02966)\n\n**Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe**  \n**Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02962](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02962)  \n**New observations; emission mechanism remains non-unique**\n\nThe XMM-Newton study resolves northern and southern X-ray structures extending tens of parsecs from the binary, including knots approximately **20 pc** away. Their spectra are consistent with nonthermal emission, with a representative photon index\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_X=1.9\\pm0.1_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.5_{\\rm sys}.\n\\]\n\nA joint synchrotron/inverse-Compton interpretation favors magnetic fields around **\\(3\\!-\\!5\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)**. The compact X-ray structures suggest localized acceleration at jet termination or recollimation regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))\n\nEinstein Probe independently identifies diffuse knots approximately **20–40 pc** from the source. Their softer X-ray spectra, \\(\\Gamma_X\\sim2\\!-\\!3\\), and different extraction regions give approximately **\\(1\\!-\\!2\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)** in one-zone leptonic fits. These values should not be averaged with the XMM result: they come from different regions and modeling assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02962v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[HAWC’s 2024 discovery](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117) established exceptionally hard, extended gamma-ray emission reaching approximately 200 TeV, but could not determine whether electrons or nuclei dominated. A subsequent [hadronic propagation model by Neronov, Oikonomou and Semikoz](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.103025) explained the large-scale gamma-ray structure through cosmic rays escaping along Galactic magnetic-field lines and predicted a potential neutrino counterpart. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117))\n\nThe new X-ray measurements provide the missing spatially resolved evidence for energetic electrons and constrain their magnetic environment. They strengthen leptonic interpretations but **do not establish that all the gamma rays are leptonic**, particularly where gamma-ray emission extends beyond the X-ray knots. Conversely, calling the system a PeVatron does not by itself prove acceleration of PeV protons. Distinguishing localized synchrotron-emitting electrons from a more extended hadronic component now requires joint, spatially resolved modeling rather than only a broadband spectral fit. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))"}, {"id": "paper-v4641-sgr", "arxiv": "2610.02962", "title": "Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe", "authors": "Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["X rays", "Gamma rays", "Compact objects", "Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Einstein Probe independently finds diffuse X-ray knots around V4641 Sgr; its spatial regions and magnetic-field estimates complement the XMM-Newton study.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-v4641-sgr", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al. 2610.02962 Einstein Probe independently finds diffuse X-ray knots around V4641 Sgr; its spatial regions and magnetic-field estimates complement the XMM-Newton study. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 X rays Gamma rays Compact objects Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. V4641 Sgr: two complementary detections of extended X-ray emission\n\n**XMM-Newton Resolves Parsec-scale X-ray Jets in the PeVatron Microquasar V4641 Sgr**  \n**Naomi Tsuji, Caterina Tresoldi, Kaya Mori, Hiromasa Suzuki, Matthew Lundy, Dmitry Khangulyan, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02966](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02966)\n\n**Candidate X-ray Counterparts of Ultra-High-Energy Emission of the Galactic Microquasar V4641 Sgr Revealed by Einstein Probe**  \n**Yi-Heng Chi, Shao-Qiang Xi, Ping Zhou, Hua Feng, Ruo-Yu Liu, Songzhan Chen, et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.02962](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02962)  \n**New observations; emission mechanism remains non-unique**\n\nThe XMM-Newton study resolves northern and southern X-ray structures extending tens of parsecs from the binary, including knots approximately **20 pc** away. Their spectra are consistent with nonthermal emission, with a representative photon index\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_X=1.9\\pm0.1_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.5_{\\rm sys}.\n\\]\n\nA joint synchrotron/inverse-Compton interpretation favors magnetic fields around **\\(3\\!-\\!5\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)**. The compact X-ray structures suggest localized acceleration at jet termination or recollimation regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))\n\nEinstein Probe independently identifies diffuse knots approximately **20–40 pc** from the source. Their softer X-ray spectra, \\(\\Gamma_X\\sim2\\!-\\!3\\), and different extraction regions give approximately **\\(1\\!-\\!2\\,\\mu{\\rm G}\\)** in one-zone leptonic fits. These values should not be averaged with the XMM result: they come from different regions and modeling assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02962v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[HAWC’s 2024 discovery](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117) established exceptionally hard, extended gamma-ray emission reaching approximately 200 TeV, but could not determine whether electrons or nuclei dominated. A subsequent [hadronic propagation model by Neronov, Oikonomou and Semikoz](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.103025) explained the large-scale gamma-ray structure through cosmic rays escaping along Galactic magnetic-field lines and predicted a potential neutrino counterpart. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16117))\n\nThe new X-ray measurements provide the missing spatially resolved evidence for energetic electrons and constrain their magnetic environment. They strengthen leptonic interpretations but **do not establish that all the gamma rays are leptonic**, particularly where gamma-ray emission extends beyond the X-ray knots. Conversely, calling the system a PeVatron does not by itself prove acceleration of PeV protons. Distinguishing localized synchrotron-emitting electrons from a more extended hadronic component now requires joint, spatially resolved modeling rather than only a broadband spectral fit. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02966v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03427", "arxiv": "2610.03427", "title": "Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection", "authors": "Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Three-dimensional pair-plasma simulations show that a fast reconnection acceleration channel survives guide fields while particle participation and acceleration efficiency decline.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03427", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou 2610.03427 Three-dimensional pair-plasma simulations show that a fast reconnection acceleration channel survives guide fields while particle participation and acceleration efficiency decline. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Cosmic rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Guide field effects on particle acceleration in three-dimensional relativistic magnetic reconnection\n\n**Despina Karavola, Lorenzo Sironi, Maria Petropoulou**  \n[arXiv:2610.03427](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03427)  \n**New kinetic-simulation result**\n\nThree-dimensional particle-in-cell simulations examine relativistic pair-plasma reconnection at magnetization \\(\\sigma=10\\), varying the guide field from zero to a strength comparable to the reconnecting field:\n\n\\[\n0\\leq B_g/B_0\\leq1.\n\\]\n\nThe rapid acceleration channel unique to three dimensions survives even with a substantial guide field: particles escaping magnetic flux ropes can sample the large-scale upstream reconnection electric field. However, increasing \\(B_g\\) reduces the participating population, steepens the spectrum, lowers the cutoff, and increases directional anisotropy. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03427))\n\n##### History\n\n[Zhang, Sironi and Giannios (2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.00009) identified this three-dimensional escape-and-acceleration mechanism for weak guide fields. Their escaping particles gained energy approximately linearly with time, unlike the slower growth of particles trapped in two-dimensional plasmoids. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.00009))\n\nThe new paper establishes how robust that particular mechanism is when the magnetic geometry becomes less favorable. It does **not** newly establish the general fact that guide fields can soften reconnection spectra. Its more specific contribution is separating the survival of a fast acceleration channel from the declining efficiency with which particles enter it.\n\nThe simulations use a pair plasma, one principal magnetization, and no radiative cooling. Their results cannot yet be translated directly into an ion acceleration efficiency or a universal maximum energy for radiatively bright astrophysical jets. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03427v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02332", "arxiv": "2610.02332", "title": "Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast", "authors": "Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A linear-stability study maps the slow–fast flavor-instability crossover and shows why homogeneous modes alone can miss the fastest growth.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02332", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns 2610.02332 A linear-stability study maps the slow–fast flavor-instability crossover and shows why homogeneous modes alone can miss the fastest growth. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★☆ 4. Neutrino flavor instabilities intermediate between slow and fast\n\n**Nicolás Viaux, Lucas Johns**  \n[arXiv:2610.02332](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02332)  \n**New linear-stability analysis**\n\nThe usual distinction between “slow” vacuum-assisted and “fast” neutrino-self-interaction-driven flavor instabilities becomes less clear when neither limiting approximation is adequate.\n\nThis paper maps the transition between them while retaining finite vacuum frequency,\n\n\\[\n\\omega=\\frac{\\Delta m^2}{2E},\n\\]\n\nand varying the angular distributions. It tracks both the maximum growth rate and the wavevector of the fastest-growing mode. The dominant mode can move away from spatial homogeneity or switch between branches, so restricting the analysis to \\(k=0\\) can miss the physically fastest instability. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02332v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Fiorillo and Raffelt’s unified dispersion framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389) already connected fast, slow, and collisional instabilities by treating vacuum splittings and collision rates as perturbations and classifying the resulting branches. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389))\n\nThe present contribution is a more explicit map of the **slow–fast crossover**, including changes in which branch dominates. It reinforces the need to inspect the full dispersion relation rather than assign a mechanism from a growth-rate estimate or a homogeneous calculation alone.\n\nThese are linear instabilities of prescribed neutrino backgrounds. Growth rates do not determine the eventual flavor mixture, conversion depth, or observable supernova spectrum. Nonlinear saturation and coupling to transport remain separate problems. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02332v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02420", "arxiv": "2610.02420", "title": "High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition", "authors": "James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Filtered dark matter connects a cosmological phase transition with neutrinos and gravitational waves, but the KM3NeT-motivated benchmark has an inaccessible gravitational-wave counterpart.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02420", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu 2610.02420 Filtered dark matter connects a cosmological phase transition with neutrinos and gravitational waves, but the KM3NeT-motivated benchmark has an inaccessible gravitational-wave counterpart. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter Multimessenger BSM #### ★★☆ 5. High Energy Neutrinos, Gravitational Waves, and Dark Matter from a Cosmological First Order Phase Transition\n\n**James M. Cline, Savas Stoica, Yong Xu**  \n[arXiv:2610.02420](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02420)  \n**Multimessenger model and forecast**\n\nDark-matter particles acquire a large mass inside expanding phase-transition bubbles. Only the energetic fraction crosses the bubble wall, setting the relic abundance; subsequent dark-matter decay produces high-energy neutrinos, while the transition produces gravitational waves.\n\nFor\n\n\\[\nm_{\\rm DM}\\simeq4.4\\times10^8\\ {\\rm GeV},\n\\]\n\nthe authors obtain benchmarks compatible with the abundance and neutrino energies associated with KM3-230213A. **The corresponding gravitational-wave peak is near \\(10^4\\) Hz and below planned detector sensitivities.** Lower-mass benchmarks, \\(m_{\\rm DM}\\lesssim10^6\\) GeV, move the gravitational-wave signal into potentially accessible frequency ranges. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02420v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Baker, Kopp and Long](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02830) introduced filtered dark matter as a production mechanism allowing masses well above the conventional thermal-relic range. The basic filtering process and its ability to suppress the surviving abundance are therefore established ideas. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02830))\n\nThe new calculation determines the bubble-wall velocity using improved hydrodynamics rather than leaving it freely adjustable, strengthening the connection between relic abundance and the gravitational-wave prediction. That is the substantive advance.\n\nThe KM3NeT-motivated benchmark should not be presented as predicting an observable two-messenger coincidence: its gravitational-wave counterpart is inaccessible in the stated setup. The neutrino interpretation also remains a model-dependent explanation of an individual event, not evidence that the phase transition occurred. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02420v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03692", "arxiv": "2610.03692", "title": "Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints", "authors": "Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A Higgsino reinterpretation identifies the restrictive assumptions needed to reconcile an isolated LZ recoil with solar-neutrino limits and distinguishes capture from thermalization and equilibrium.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03692", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos 2610.03692 A Higgsino reinterpretation identifies the restrictive assumptions needed to reconcile an isolated LZ recoil with solar-neutrino limits and distinguishes capture from thermalization and equilibrium. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Higgsino dark matter compatible with the LUX-ZEPLIN high-energy nuclear-recoil event and IceCube constraints\n\n**Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos**  \n[arXiv:2610.03692](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03692)  \n**Reassessment of an existing exclusion**\n\nThe authors revisit whether inelastic Higgsino scattering can explain LZ’s isolated high-energy recoil while avoiding solar-neutrino limits. They vary the high-speed halo population, post-capture cooling, capture–annihilation equilibrium, and treatment of LZ’s empty high-energy sideband.\n\nThe **thermal \\(1.1\\)-TeV Higgsino remains only marginally compatible** when an LMC-induced high-speed tail is included **and both elastic solar cooling and the sideband constraint are neglected**. Heavier, nonthermally produced Higgsinos admit larger allowed regions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03692v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Pospelov and Ramani’s September analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775) obtained \\(\\delta>566\\) keV for the approximately 1.08-TeV Higgsino, excluding its interpretation of the recoil under their solar-capture assumptions. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775))\n\nThe new paper does not simply invalidate that result. It identifies which assumptions permit a surviving region and extends the mass range beyond the thermal benchmark. Its most important lesson is that **capture, thermalization, and capture–annihilation equilibrium are distinct conditions**.\n\nThe high-mass conclusions are less secure experimentally: above 10 TeV, the analysis holds IceCube’s reconstructed annihilation-rate limit fixed at its 10-TeV value. That is a benchmark extrapolation, not a published high-mass detector likelihood. Neither this reinterpretation nor the original recoil establishes dark matter. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03692v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03455", "arxiv": "2610.03455", "title": "Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on B-mesogenesis", "authors": "James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Hadron-driven neutron–proton conversion imposes an approximately 9.5–10 MeV reheating floor in the studied B-mesogenesis scenarios.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03455", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on B-mesogenesis James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu 2610.03455 Hadron-driven neutron–proton conversion imposes an approximately 9.5–10 MeV reheating floor in the studied B-mesogenesis scenarios. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 BSM Particle physics Dark matter #### ★★★ 7. Big Bang Nucleosynthesis Constraint on \\(B\\)-mesogenesis\n\n**James M. Cline, Jonas Frerick, Sebastian A. Naja, Yong Xu**  \n[arXiv:2610.03455](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03455)  \n**New quantitative cosmological constraint**\n\nIn \\(B\\)-mesogenesis, a late-decaying particle reheats the Universe and produces bottom quarks; meson oscillations and exotic decays then generate the baryon asymmetry. This study follows the altered expansion, neutrino heating, and injected hadrons through nucleosynthesis.\n\nThe dominant effect is **hadron-induced neutron–proton interconversion**, not destruction of already-formed nuclei. For parent masses \\(M_\\Phi\\lesssim100\\) GeV, the approximate three-sigma requirement is\n\n\\[\nT_{\\rm rh}\\gtrsim9.5\\!-\\!10\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to \\(\\tau_\\Phi\\lesssim0.02\\) s. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03455v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Elor, Escudero and Nelson’s original proposal](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00880) connected baryogenesis and dark matter through neutral-\\(B\\) oscillations and visible–dark-sector decays, making low reheating temperatures central to the mechanism. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00880))\n\nThe present paper supplies the missing process-specific answer to how low that temperature can be. A generic low-reheating bound is insufficient because the decay products directly modify \\(n/p\\), even when the expansion and neutrino temperatures alone would seem acceptable.\n\nThis closes the lowest-temperature portion of the parameter space rather than excluding \\(B\\)-mesogenesis. The bound depends most strongly on hadronic interconversion rates and the adopted decay history; the authors propagate estimated rate uncertainties, which weaken it modestly. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03455v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-03718", "arxiv": "2610.03718", "title": "Dark matter bound-states made easy", "authors": "Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Compact bound-state and Sommerfeld approximations reproduce tested Abelian relic-density calculations within a few percent, with limits on excited-state simplifications.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-03718", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Dark matter bound-states made easy Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl 2610.03718 Compact bound-state and Sommerfeld approximations reproduce tested Abelian relic-density calculations within a few percent, with limits on excited-state simplifications. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 8. Dark matter bound-states made easy\n\n**Simone Biondini, Stefan Vogl**  \n[arXiv:2610.03718](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03718)  \n**Computational-method improvement**\n\nThe paper develops compact approximations for freeze-out calculations including Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation, dissociation, annihilation, and selected excited-state transitions.\n\nIn the Abelian model tested, smooth approximations reproduce the full relic-density calculation at the **few-percent level**. Including excited-state transitions can shift the abundance by approximately **15%** relative to a ground-state-only treatment; the corresponding approximate calculation stays within about **5%** of the full result.\n\nA semi-analytic post-freeze-out integration further reduces the numerical workload. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03718v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Von Harling and Petraki (2014)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.7874) already demonstrated that bound-state formation can materially change thermal relic abundances and supplied a fit for the coupling needed to obtain the observed density. The physical importance of bound states is therefore not new. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.7874))\n\nThe advance is a more systematic, reusable approximation to the rates and thermal evolution, with explicit accuracy tests against the full calculation.\n\nA particularly useful caution is that the frequently invoked efficient-transition limit does **not** consistently simplify all excited-state processes in this setup. The prescription is not a universal replacement for coupled bound-state evolution, especially for non-Abelian models, complicated level networks, or percent-level precision targets. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.03718v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-02667", "arxiv": "2610.02667", "title": "Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry", "authors": "Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu", "primary_category": "astro-ph.CO", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The angular dependence of CMB polarization rotation offers a forecasted test of axion dark energy, axion dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry; no new detection is claimed.", "path": "2026/10/05/index.html#paper-2610-02667", "reportDate": "5 October 2026", "searchText": "Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu 2610.02667 The angular dependence of CMB polarization rotation offers a forecasted test of axion dark energy, axion dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry; no new detection is claimed. 2026-10-05 5 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 9. Distinguishing the origin of cosmic birefringence: dark energy, dark matter, and neutrino asymmetry\n\n**Lu Yin, Eiichiro Komatsu**  \n[arXiv:2610.02667](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02667)  \n**New discrimination strategy; no new detection**\n\nDifferent mechanisms can rotate CMB polarization by a similar mean angle while producing different angular-scale dependence in the \\(EB\\) spectrum.\n\nThe authors compare axionlike dark energy, axionlike dark matter, and a neutrino-asymmetry-induced interaction. In the models studied, neutrino asymmetry and axion dark matter suppress the large-angle signal at approximately\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\lesssim20,\n\\]\n\nwhereas the dark-energy case does not. Differences during recombination supply additional discrimination at smaller angular scales. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02667v1))\n\n##### History\n\n[Nakatsuka, Namikawa and Komatsu (2022)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.08560) already proposed using reionization- and recombination-era polarization as a tomographic test distinguishing dark energy from a more rapidly evolving axion field. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.08560))\n\nThe new contribution adds the neutrino-asymmetry mechanism and works out where its predicted spectrum differs from both axion scenarios. It extends an existing tomographic idea rather than introducing it.\n\nThese are model-discrimination forecasts, not evidence that any proposed source is responsible for the reported birefringence hints. Practical inference still requires foreground treatment, polarization calibration, and sufficiently precise large-angle measurements; similar mean rotation angles alone cannot identify the underlying physics. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02667v1))"}, {"id": "paper-2610-01877", "arxiv": "2610.01877", "title": "Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches", "authors": "Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy", "tags": ["Dark matter", "X rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Stacking low-background XRISM observations in the Galactic halo frame strengthens decay-line limits; a 3.53-keV residual remains below three-sigma global significance.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01877", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy 2610.01877 Stacking low-background XRISM observations in the Galactic halo frame strengthens decay-line limits; a 3.53-keV residual remains below three-sigma global significance. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter X rays BSM #### ★★★ 1. Recycling serendipitous observations of XRISM for dark matter searches\n\n**Lidiia Zadorozhna, Denys Malyshev, Oleg Ruchayskiy**  \n[arXiv:2610.01877](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01877)\n\nThe authors combine 60 low-background public XRISM/Resolve observations, totaling 2.917 Ms, to search for narrow dark-matter decay lines from the Milky Way halo. Each spectrum is corrected for the observer’s velocity relative to the Galactic halo before stacking.\n\nThe resulting limits are, on average, approximately 70% stronger than XRISM’s recent cluster-stack constraints. More intriguingly, a residual appears at\n\n\\[\nE=3.5311~{\\rm keV}.\n\\]\n\nIts significance rises from \\(2.5\\sigma\\) in an ordinary stack to \\(3.0\\sigma\\) locally after halo-frame correction, but falls to only \\(2.0\\!-\\!2.5\\sigma\\) after the look-elsewhere effect. The excess corresponds to approximately \\(26\\pm10\\) photons over roughly 52 background counts in a 5-eV bin. Interpreted as sterile-neutrino decay, its best-fit mixing is\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2(2\\theta)=(2.7\\pm1.0)\\times10^{-10}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\nBulbul et al. originally reported a cluster-stack line near 3.55–3.57 keV, while Boyarsky et al. found a compatible feature in Perseus and M31. ([Bulbul et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.2301); [Boyarsky et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.4119)) XRISM’s subsequent 3.75-Ms stack of ten clusters found no line and improved on Hitomi’s decay-rate bound by a factor of 3–4, although it was still insufficient to test the original Bulbul flux fully. ([XRISM cluster-stack search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24560))\n\nThe present work materially improves the experimental strategy: low-continuum blank-field observations and halo-frame alignment can outperform a longer cluster exposure. The 3.53-keV residual is nevertheless not evidence for sterile-neutrino dark matter. Its global significance is below \\(3\\sigma\\), it contains only a few dozen excess counts, and the inferred mixing is already in roughly \\(2\\sigma\\) tension with existing exclusions. An independent, pre-specified analysis of additional fields—and explicit tests of detector-line and field-selection systematics—is essential."}, {"id": "paper-2610-01308", "arxiv": "2610.01308", "title": "Search for charged-lepton-flavour violating eμtq interactions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "ATLAS finds no charged-lepton-flavour violation in top production or decay and sets improved limits on individual effective operators.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01308", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for charged-lepton-flavour violating eμtq interactions at √s = 13 TeV with the ATLAS detector ATLAS Collaboration 2610.01308 ATLAS finds no charged-lepton-flavour violation in top production or decay and sets improved limits on individual effective operators. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 2. Search for charged-lepton-flavour-violating \\(e\\mu tq\\) interactions with ATLAS\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.01308](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01308)\n\nATLAS searches for contact interactions containing an electron, muon, top quark and up- or charm-type quark, using the full \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 dataset. The three-lepton analysis covers both anomalous single-top production and flavour-violating top decay inside \\(t\\bar t\\) events.\n\nNo excess is observed. One-operator-at-a-time bounds on six EFT coefficients range from\n\n\\[\n0.018\\ {\\rm TeV^{-2}}\\quad{\\rm to}\\quad0.32\\ {\\rm TeV^{-2}},\n\\]\n\nwith corresponding branching-fraction limits spanning approximately\n\n\\[\n{\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow e\\mu u)<6\\times10^{-9}\n\\]\n\nto\n\n\\[\n{\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow e\\mu c)<2.7\\times10^{-7},\n\\]\n\ndepending on Lorentz and flavour structure.\n\n##### History\n\nATLAS’s earlier 79.8-\\({\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) decay-only search obtained a combined limit near \\({\\cal B}(t\\rightarrow\\ell\\ell' q)<1.86\\times10^{-5}\\). ([Earlier ATLAS search](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.09048)) The quoted branching-fraction limits are numerically smaller by factors ranging from roughly 70 to several thousand, although the comparison depends on the flavour and operator assumptions. That gain is not attributable merely to luminosity: the analysis adds the often more sensitive anomalous-production channel and resolves individual EFT structures.\n\nThe limits assume one nonzero coefficient at a time. Correlations, cancellations and mediator-induced relations between operators can weaken their direct ultraviolet interpretation, while the contact-interaction description must be checked if the underlying mediator is light enough to be produced at the LHC."}, {"id": "paper-2610-01825", "arxiv": "2610.01825", "title": "Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension", "authors": "Kevin Langhoff", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Near-threshold d-wave suppression limits graviton cascades in the minimal flat dark-dimension model, independently corroborating a related theoretical analysis.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-01825", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension Kevin Langhoff 2610.01825 Near-threshold d-wave suppression limits graviton cascades in the minimal flat dark-dimension model, independently corroborating a related theoretical analysis. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 3. Near Threshold Kaluza–Klein Graviton Decays and the Dark Dimension\n\n**Kevin Langhoff**  \n[arXiv:2610.01825](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01825)\n\nThe paper revisits decays within a nearly degenerate Kaluza–Klein graviton tower. Angular-momentum constraints make the near-threshold decay purely \\(d\\)-wave, yielding\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\propto p^5\n\\]\n\nrather than the \\(s\\)-wave-like \\(\\Gamma\\propto p\\) scaling used in some dark-dimension cosmologies.\n\nApplied to a flat dark dimension, the suppression prevents the proposed graviton cascade from efficiently creating a sufficiently cold, long-lived dark-matter population. Irreducible freeze-in also produces MeV gamma rays, while BBN and CMB requirements force the compactification radius to be about three orders of magnitude smaller than the nominal \\(R\\sim\\Lambda^{-1/4}\\) dark-dimension scale.\n\n##### History\n\nThe dark-dimension dark-matter proposal relied on cascades ending in approximately \\(1\\!-\\!100\\)-keV KK gravitons. ([Dark Dimension Gravitons as Dark Matter](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09249)) The new threshold suppression directly removes the decay efficiency needed for that construction. However, a near-contemporaneous independent analysis, [*A Universal KK Graviton Cascade Rate*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36234), derives the same \\(p^5\\) behavior and associated amplitude suppression. Thus the physical correction is important, but it should not be attributed to this paper alone.\n\nThe exclusion concerns the minimal flat-dimensional realization and its assumed production history. Warped geometries, additional light states or nonstandard cosmological histories are not generically ruled out."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00965", "arxiv": "2610.00965", "title": "High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically-bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events", "authors": "Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Radiatively compressed shocks in obscured tidal disruption events could supply part of the diffuse neutrino flux, with large uncertainties in event rates and cosmic-ray loading.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00965", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically-bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto 2610.00965 Radiatively compressed shocks in obscured tidal disruption events could supply part of the diffuse neutrino flux, with large uncertainties in event rates and cosmic-ray loading. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. High-energy neutrinos from shocked circumnuclear material around optically bright and infrared-only tidal disruption events\n\n**Mainak Mukhopadhyay, Shigeo S. Kimura, Tatsuya Matsumoto**  \n[arXiv:2610.00965](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00965)\n\nA semi-analytic calculation follows cosmic-ray acceleration and transport in shocks driven into circumnuclear gas by tidal-disruption-event outflows. Radiative cooling and post-shock compression are included in calculating the \\(pp\\) efficiency and neutrino output.\n\nOrdinary optically selected TDEs are generally inefficient neutrino emitters. Dust-obscured, infrared-only events can approach hadronic calorimetry and, given the still-uncertain event rate, may supply as much as roughly 10% of the diffuse neutrino intensity around 100 TeV. Detecting the predicted stacked signal would probably require a future detector with an effective volume near \\(30~{\\rm km^3}\\) and several years of observations of nearby events.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier source-specific outflow–cloud calculations for AT2019dsg predicted only about \\(4\\times10^{-3}\\) PeV events under already favorable assumptions. ([AT2019dsg outflow–cloud model](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01748)) IceCube’s recent search over 89 catalogued TDEs found neither a significant individual source nor a stacked excess. ([IceCube TDE search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.00657))\n\nThe present work does not contradict that null result: most IceCube targets were optically or X-ray selected, whereas the predicted efficient population is heavily obscured and potentially missing from those catalogues. The real advance is the self-consistent population treatment of infrared-only events, including radiative compression. The 10% contribution is an upper-end estimate controlled by poorly measured event rates, gas covering factors and cosmic-ray loading; individual-event detections remain unlikely."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00995", "arxiv": "2610.00995", "title": "Search for dark matter produced in association with a low-multiplicity jet in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV", "authors": "CMS Collaboration", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "CMS opens a search for missing momentum accompanied by a narrow low-multiplicity jet, setting model-dependent mediator limits without a signal excess.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00995", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Search for dark matter produced in association with a low-multiplicity jet in proton-proton collisions at √s = 13 TeV CMS Collaboration 2610.00995 CMS opens a search for missing momentum accompanied by a narrow low-multiplicity jet, setting model-dependent mediator limits without a signal excess. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Dark matter Particle physics BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Search for dark matter produced with a low-multiplicity jet using CMS\n\n**CMS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2610.00995](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00995)\n\nCMS searches for a dark-matter pair produced through a heavy vector or axial-vector mediator, with one dark particle radiating a light leptophobic \\(Z'\\). A \\(0.3\\!-\\!3\\)-GeV \\(Z'\\) decaying into two pions or kaons produces an unusually narrow, low-track-multiplicity “pencil jet,” rather than a conventional QCD jet.\n\nThe full \\(138~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 sample is analyzed using hadronic-\\(\\tau\\) reconstruction followed by machine-learning classification. No excess is found. For \\(m_\\chi=150\\) GeV, \\(m_{Z'}=1\\) GeV, \\(g_q=0.25\\) and \\(g_\\chi=1\\), heavy mediators below approximately 4.25 TeV are excluded; at \\(m_\\chi=550\\) GeV, the reach is about 3.5 TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nBai, Bourbeau and Lin proposed the mono-\\(Z'\\)-jet signature in 2015, but it had not previously received a dedicated collider search. ([Mono-Z′-jet proposal](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.01395)) This is therefore an experimental opening of a qualitatively new channel, not simply a stronger monojet bound.\n\nThe impressive mediator reach is conditional on a very light leptophobic boson, prompt hadronic decay and the specified benchmark couplings. It cannot be compared directly with conventional monojet exclusions, which test different radiation and acceptance assumptions. Modeling extremely narrow hadronic jets and calibrating a classifier inherited partly from \\(\\tau\\) reconstruction are important experimental systematics."}, {"id": "paper-2610-02107", "arxiv": "2610.02107", "title": "The Scalar MSW Effect: Compact analytical formulas for neutrino oscillations with large matter effects, including μ–μ and τ–τ dominant matter potentials", "authors": "Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Analytic diagonalization exposes scalar matter resonances and maps vacuum to effective mixing parameters, without adding a new experimental constraint.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-02107", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "The Scalar MSW Effect: Compact analytical formulas for neutrino oscillations with large matter effects, including μ–μ and τ–τ dominant matter potentials Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund 2610.02107 Analytic diagonalization exposes scalar matter resonances and maps vacuum to effective mixing parameters, without adding a new experimental constraint. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Scalar MSW resonance and approximate diagonalization with scalar nonstandard interactions\n\n**Sandhya Choubey, Andreas Lund**  \n[arXiv:2610.02107](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02107)\n\nThe authors extend Jacobi-style approximate diagonalization of the three-flavour neutrino Hamiltonian to scalar nonstandard interactions, which modify the mass matrix rather than adding an ordinary vector matter potential.\n\nThey identify an energy-independent scalar analogue of the MSW resonance for each diagonal scalar coupling and derive compact mappings between vacuum and in-matter mixing parameters. The electron-flavour case admits particularly accurate formulae; the \\(\\mu\\mu\\) and \\(\\tau\\tau\\) cases require an expansion in the charged-current matter potential plus controlled corrections.\n\n##### History\n\nScalar matter effects in neutrino oscillations were introduced explicitly by Ge and Parke and have since been constrained with solar and long-baseline data. ([Ge and Parke](https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08376)) The new paper does not introduce the interaction or improve an experimental constraint. Its contribution is to expose the resonance structure analytically and provide fast parameter mappings that can replace repeated numerical diagonalization in fits.\n\nThe “energy-independent resonance” is a property of the effective scalar mass contribution; observability still depends on density profiles, flavour structure and degeneracies with vacuum masses. Off-diagonal couplings and simultaneous multi-parameter fits remain more complicated than the presented single-coupling cases."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00455", "arxiv": "2610.00455", "title": "Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks", "authors": "Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Three candidate spider pulsar binaries include a system whose optical line kinematics favor an intrabinary shock wrapping around the neutron star.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00455", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart 2610.00455 Three candidate spider pulsar binaries include a system whose optical line kinematics favor an intrabinary shock wrapping around the neutron star. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Compact objects Gamma rays #### ★★☆ 7. Three New Likely Spider Millisecond Pulsar Binaries and Optical Kinematic Tracers of Intrabinary Shocks\n\n**Jay Strader, Rebecca Kyer, Laura Chomiuk, Elias Aydi, Peter Craig, Thomas M. Do, Isabella Molina, Teresa Panurach, Subhroja Roy, Kirill V. Sokolovsky, Samuel J. Swihart, Ryan Urquhart**  \n[arXiv:2610.00455](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00455)\n\nMultiwavelength follow-up of unassociated Fermi sources identifies two strong redback candidates with orbital periods of \\(0.15\\!-\\!0.30\\) days and companion mass functions around \\(0.6\\!-\\!0.9\\,M_\\odot\\), plus a less secure 30.5-hour candidate.\n\nOne system, J1020, shows persistent broad H\\(\\alpha\\) emission with FWHM near \\(2100~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\). Its phase-dependent velocity is offset from the companion but consistent with material near the neutron star, providing the first kinematic evidence for an intrabinary shock wrapping around the pulsar rather than the secondary.\n\n##### History\n\nPhase-resolved H\\(\\alpha\\) tomography of PSR J1048+2339 previously located emission near the companion and inner Lagrange point. ([PSR J1048+2339 shock tomography](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.06317)) J1020 therefore supplies a qualitatively different geometry and observationally supports the theoretically expected reversal of shock orientation when the companion wind dominates.\n\nNone of the three systems yet has detected radio or gamma-ray pulsations, so their neutron-star identifications remain probabilistic. The third association is particularly tentative, and interpreting H\\(\\alpha\\) velocities as direct spatial tracers requires assumptions about acceleration and radiative transfer in the shock."}, {"id": "paper-2610-00508", "arxiv": "2610.00508", "title": "X-ray Through Radio Observations and Modeling of the Soft X-ray Flash GRB 250419A", "authors": "Genevieve Schroeder et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Broadband observations favor a low-energy relativistic jet for a soft X-ray flash, with degeneracy between energy stratification and viewing geometry.", "path": "2026/10/02/index.html#paper-2610-00508", "reportDate": "2 October 2026", "searchText": "X-ray Through Radio Observations and Modeling of the Soft X-ray Flash GRB 250419A Genevieve Schroeder et al. 2610.00508 Broadband observations favor a low-energy relativistic jet for a soft X-ray flash, with degeneracy between energy stratification and viewing geometry. 2026-10-02 2 October 2026 Gamma rays X rays Compact objects #### ★☆☆ 8. X-ray through radio observations and modeling of the soft X-ray flash GRB 250419A\n\n**Genevieve Schroeder et al.**  \n[arXiv:2610.00508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00508)\n\nSVOM measured a prompt spectral peak below 4 keV. Broadband follow-up found an achromatic X-ray/optical break and evidence for two radio components. The authors model the event as a low-energy relativistic jet with\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm jet}\\sim10^{49}-10^{50}\\ {\\rm erg},\n\\]\n\nsupplemented by radial energy stratification, modest off-axis viewing, or both. The result favors at least some X-ray flashes being the low-energy tail of the classical GRB population.\n\n##### History\n\nNearby soft events such as GRB 060218 and GRB 100316D have long admitted competing relativistic-jet and shock-breakout interpretations. The new event adds an unusually broad radio-to-X-ray dataset supporting a genuine jet, but does not settle the population question. Radial energy injection and off-axis geometry remain partly interchangeable in the fit, and a single burst cannot establish whether all X-ray flashes share the same origin.\n\nThere is **no new UHECR spectrum, composition or anisotropy measurement** and no new precision laboratory oscillation dataset in the 2 October listings."}, {"id": "paper-2609-40237", "arxiv": "2609.40237", "title": "First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless νμ charged-current interactions on argon using the MicroBooNE detector", "authors": "MicroBooNE Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MicroBooNE resolves argon interaction cross sections through the fourth outgoing proton, exposing correlated generator discrepancies relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-40237", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless νμ charged-current interactions on argon using the MicroBooNE detector MicroBooNE Collaboration 2609.40237 MicroBooNE resolves argon interaction cross sections through the fourth outgoing proton, exposing correlated generator discrepancies relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. First double-differential cross-section measurements in proton multiplicity and kinematics for mesonless \\(\\nu_\\mu\\) charged-current interactions on argon\n\n**MicroBooNE Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.40237](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40237)\n\nUsing MicroBooNE’s complete \\(1.30\\times10^{21}\\)-proton-on-target Booster Neutrino Beam exposure, the collaboration measures mesonless charged-current cross sections jointly as functions of proton multiplicity and kinematics. Observables include the momenta and muon–proton opening angles of the first through fourth protons and the total available hadronic kinetic energy.\n\nNo tested event generator adequately describes the complete correlated dataset. The largest discrepancies appear in proton multiplicities, backward muon–proton configurations and subleading-proton kinematics—precisely the regions most sensitive to intranuclear rescattering and nucleon ejection.\n\n##### History\n\nMicroBooNE’s earlier CC\\(0\\pi\\) analyses established argon cross sections with final-state protons, including a 359-bin double-differential dataset, but primarily constrained inclusive or leading-particle kinematics ([arXiv:2403.19574](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19574)). The new result resolves the hadronic cascade by proton rank through the fourth proton and exposes generator failures that leading-proton observables can conceal.\n\nThis is therefore a genuinely new constraint on final-state-interaction modeling, important for neutrino-energy reconstruction in liquid-argon oscillation experiments. It does not isolate a unique microscopic defect: nuclear ground-state modeling, multinucleon interactions and intranuclear transport contribute correlated uncertainties, and generator tuning to these data will need validation against independent targets and beam energies."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38682", "arxiv": "2609.38682", "title": "New Constraints on r-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra", "authors": "Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Late spectra of GW170817 constrain several heavy-element abundances in its inner ejecta, with lanthanum and cerium estimates sensitive to atomic and radiation-field uncertainties.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38682", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "New Constraints on r-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka 2609.38682 Late spectra of GW170817 constrain several heavy-element abundances in its inner ejecta, with lanthanum and cerium estimates sensitive to atomic and radiation-field uncertainties. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★★ 2. New Constraints on \\(r\\)-process Nucleosynthesis in Neutron Star Mergers from GW170817 Late-Phase Spectra\n\n**Salma Rahmouni, Masaomi Tanaka, Daiji Kato, Gediminas Gaigalas, Kenta Hotokezaka**  \n[arXiv:2609.38682](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38682)\n\nAn analytic nebular model follows allowed and forbidden emission from collisionally excited ions in AT2017gfo. It identifies La III and Ce III as the principal contributors near \\(1.4\\) and \\(1.6\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\), respectively, and confirms Te III at \\(2.1\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\).\n\nThe inferred mass fractions are\n\n\\[\nX({\\rm La})\\simeq0.025-0.05,\\quad\nX({\\rm Ce})\\simeq0.05-0.10,\\quad\nX({\\rm Te})\\simeq0.04-0.08,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(X({\\rm Kr})\\lesssim0.03\\) and \\(X({\\rm Sb})\\lesssim0.003\\). The implied inner ejecta have a suppressed first \\(r\\)-process peak, enhanced heavy elements relative to the solar pattern and a lanthanide fraction \\(X_{\\rm LN}\\simeq0.03-0.06\\).\n\n##### History\n\nThe \\(2.1\\,\\mu{\\rm m}\\) feature had already been attributed to [Te III] using a solar heavy-\\(r\\)-process abundance pattern ([Hotokezaka et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00988)); JWST subsequently detected a similar feature in the GRB 230307A kilonova. ([JWST kilonova spectroscopy](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02098)) The new advance is the simultaneous identification and abundance inference for several heavy species, including constraints from absent lines, rather than another qualitative Te identification.\n\nThe La and Ce numbers remain tentative because they depend strongly on the poorly known radiation field, ionization balance and atomic collision data. The analytic treatment also cannot reproduce the full geometric and time-dependent radiative-transfer problem. The heavy-element enhancement is consequently evidence about the inner ejecta, not yet a model-independent total yield for GW170817."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38590", "arxiv": "2609.38590", "title": "Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of the Gravitational lens MG B2016+112 at z=3.273 with 175 pc separation", "authors": "Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Separate absorbed X-ray spectra support two active nuclei at a projected separation of 175 pc in a strongly lensed high-redshift system.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38590", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of the Gravitational lens MG B2016+112 at z=3.273 with 175 pc separation Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka 2609.38590 Separate absorbed X-ray spectra support two active nuclei at a projected separation of 175 pc in a strongly lensed high-redshift system. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Chandra X-ray Spectral Evidence of the Dual AGN Nature of MG B2016+112 at \\(z=3.273\\) with 175-pc separation\n\n**Daniel A. Schwartz, Julia M. Sisk-Reynes, Anna Barnacka**  \n[arXiv:2609.38590](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38590)\n\nA new 175-ks Chandra observation uses extreme gravitational magnification to obtain separate spectra for two compact X-ray emitters in MG B2016+112. The highly magnified component inside the lens caustic shows intrinsic absorption\n\n\\[\nN_{\\rm H}>4\\times10^{22}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}}\n\\]\n\nat \\(3\\sigma\\), identifying it as an obscured AGN alongside the previously known type-II nucleus. The projected source-plane separation is approximately 175 pc, making this the tightest confirmed high-redshift AGN pair reported so far.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier VLBI proper-motion measurements and archival Chandra imaging had resolved two emitters and favored a dual-AGN interpretation, but could not exclude one AGN plus a highly magnified parsec-scale jet. ([VLBI study](https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.11756); [archival Chandra analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08537)) The distinct absorbed X-ray spectrum is the decisive new discriminator; this is more than a deeper-image confirmation.\n\nThe system is nevertheless not a dynamically verified binary: the 175-pc distance is projected and lens-model-dependent, and this X-ray analysis does not measure an orbit or dynamically determine the two black-hole masses. Its immediate significance is as a rare compact merger phase and potential precursor population for massive-black-hole gravitational-wave sources."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38373", "arxiv": "2609.38373", "title": "NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at z=5", "authors": "Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST captures a luminous young transient in an unlensed field at redshift five; an unusual core-collapse supernova is favored but its subtype remains unconfirmed.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38373", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at z=5 Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang 2609.38373 JWST captures a luminous young transient in an unlensed field at redshift five; an unusual core-collapse supernova is favored but its subtype remains unconfirmed. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. NEXUS: Discovery of a Luminous Young Transient at \\(z=5\\)\n\n**Ming-Yang Zhuang, Yue Shen, Lei Hu, Justin D. R. Pierel, Zhiwei Pan, Zachary Stone, Adam J. Burgasser, Xiaohui Fan, Jenny E. Greene, Junyao Li, Feige Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.38373](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38373)\n\nJWST/NEXUS discovered AT 2026abck in a galaxy at \\(z=5.017\\pm0.001\\). It brightened by more than 1.4 magnitudes in at most 12 rest-frame days and was probably caught only 4–10 days after explosion.\n\nA blackbody fit gives \\(T\\simeq1.7\\times10^4\\) K, \\(R\\simeq7\\times10^{14}\\) cm and \\(L_{\\rm bol}\\simeq3\\times10^{43}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}}\\). Its off-nuclear position and blue continuum favor a core-collapse supernova, although it is hotter and more luminous than ordinary early Type-II events. It is the first robust \\(z\\gtrsim5\\) transient found in an unlensed field.\n\n##### History\n\nSN Eos at \\(z=5.133\\) remains the stronger object-level classification because it was multiply imaged, strongly lensed and spectroscopically confirmed as a metal-poor Type-II supernova ([arXiv:2601.04156](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04156)). AT 2026abck changes the survey context: no lens magnification was required, and the high cadence captured the UV-bright rise in a host roughly \\(10^{3.3}\\) times more massive than that of Eos.\n\nThe event itself lacks a spectroscopic subtype and a mature light curve. A superluminous supernova, interaction-powered event or other unusual core-collapse transient is not yet excluded, so “core-collapse supernova” should be read as the favored photometric interpretation."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38316", "arxiv": "2609.38316", "title": "Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture", "authors": "Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Solar capture and IceCube limits constrain inelastic electroweak multiplets, with implications for the LZ recoil candidate that depend on relic abundance and the halo velocity distribution.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38316", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani 2609.38316 Solar capture and IceCube limits constrain inelastic electroweak multiplets, with implications for the LZ recoil candidate that depend on relic abundance and the halo velocity distribution. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Constraints on Complex Electroweak WIMPs from Solar Capture\n\n**Juan P. Luengas, Maxim Pospelov, Harikrishnan Ramani**  \n[arXiv:2609.38316](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38316)\n\nThe authors calculate capture, energy loss and annihilation in the Sun for scalar and fermionic electroweak dark-matter multiplets with sub-MeV inelastic splittings. For higher representations, IceCube’s non-observation of solar annihilation neutrinos requires approximately\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\gtrsim350~{\\rm keV}\n\\]\n\nover masses extending to \\(100\\) TeV. Under thermal freeze-out and the Standard Halo Model, this excludes most such multiplets as explanations of LZ’s single 248-keV recoil candidate. An LMC-induced high-velocity tail can, however, move the LZ-preferred splitting above the solar limit for several representations.\n\n##### History\n\nA study published in September 2026 applied the same solar-acceleration mechanism specifically to the 1.08-TeV Higgsino and obtained the stronger doublet-specific bound \\(\\delta>566\\) keV ([arXiv:2609.02775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02775)). The present paper’s substantive advance is the generalization to scalar and fermionic higher multiplets, revealing an approximately representation-independent \\(350\\)-keV floor.\n\nThe conclusion about the LZ event is conditional. LZ reported one candidate, not a dark-matter discovery, and the mapping to a preferred splitting depends on thermal abundance and the poorly known high-speed halo tail. Nonstandard production, a subdominant WIMP abundance or modified local phase space can weaken the stated exclusion."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38233", "arxiv": "2609.38233", "title": "XENONnT pp-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the ν₁ Pseudo-Dirac Splitting", "authors": "Gio Leone", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An independent reanalysis of XENONnT solar-neutrino recoils limits a pseudo-Dirac mass splitting without finding a preference for active–sterile oscillations.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38233", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "XENONnT pp-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the ν₁ Pseudo-Dirac Splitting Gio Leone 2609.38233 An independent reanalysis of XENONnT solar-neutrino recoils limits a pseudo-Dirac mass splitting without finding a preference for active–sterile oscillations. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 6. XENONnT \\(pp\\)-Dominated Solar Neutrinos Constrain the \\(\\nu_1\\) Pseudo-Dirac Splitting\n\n**Gio Leone**  \n[arXiv:2609.38233](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38233)\n\nThe published XENONnT low-energy electron-recoil spectrum is reanalyzed for active–sterile oscillations caused by a pseudo-Dirac splitting of the first neutrino mass eigenstate. The calculation includes the \\(pp\\) and \\(^{7}\\)Be recoil spectra and finds no preference for a nonzero splitting.\n\nThe resulting two-sigma limit is\n\n\\[\n\\delta m_1^2 \\lesssim 5.9\\times10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}.\n\\]\n\nThis reaches the regime previously associated mainly with future low-energy solar-neutrino detectors.\n\n##### History\n\nSolar data had generally limited pseudo-Dirac splittings at roughly the \\(10^{-11}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\) scale. Dedicated forecasts found that a 300-ton-year DARWIN exposure could reach \\(10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\), while a \\(pp\\)-inclusive JUNO analysis might reach \\(2.9\\times10^{-13}\\ {\\rm eV^2}\\). ([DARWIN forecast](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02421); [JUNO forecast](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.05418)) The new paper is the first actual xenon-\\(pp\\) constraint in this range, although it does not yet equal those full-exposure projections.\n\nThis is an independent phenomenological likelihood built from published spectra, not an official XENONnT oscillation analysis. Correlated background, detector-response and solar-flux nuisance parameters may therefore be treated less completely than in a collaboration likelihood."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38305", "arxiv": "2609.38305", "title": "Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background", "authors": "Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Multimessenger", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Differencing nearby pulsars could suppress the common gravitational-wave background and reveal dark substructure, subject to untested cluster and timing assumptions.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38305", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek 2609.38305 Differencing nearby pulsars could suppress the common gravitational-wave background and reveal dark substructure, subject to untested cluster and timing assumptions. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Dark matter Multimessenger Compact objects #### ★★☆ 7. Close Pulsar Pairs See Dark Matter Substructure through the Gravitational-Wave Background\n\n**Abhiram Cherukupalli, Vincent S. H. Lee, Kim V. Berghaus, Kathryn M. Zurek**  \n[arXiv:2609.38305](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38305)\n\nThe proposal uses sub-parsec pulsar pairs as a differential detector. A nanohertz gravitational-wave background produces nearly common timing residuals and cancels when the pair is differenced, whereas the gravitational perturbation from a nearby dark subhalo remains differential.\n\nFor separations near \\(0.1\\) pc, the projected sensitivity to approximately \\(10^{-6}M_\\odot\\) subhalos improves by more than an order of magnitude relative to an otherwise identical array of widely separated pulsars. Globular clusters naturally supply candidate pairs.\n\n##### History\n\nThe same authors recently showed that the measured stochastic gravitational-wave background degrades ordinary PTA sensitivity to substructure by one to three orders of magnitude ([arXiv:2607.03533](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03533)). The new paper supplies a geometrical cancellation strategy for that specific limitation rather than merely updating a forecast.\n\nIts practicality remains unproven. Globular clusters may have lost much of their primordial dark-matter content, and their stellar accelerations, encounters, intracluster plasma and timing-model correlations can all generate differential signals. The forecast also assumes suitable, precisely timed pulsar pairs that do not yet constitute a demonstrated observing array."}, {"id": "paper-2609-38314", "arxiv": "2609.38314", "title": "How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas", "authors": "Damiano F. G. Fiorillo", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A linear-dispersion analysis separates symmetry-dependent endpoints of collective neutrino modes from generic branch-cut termination when velocity dispersion is included.", "path": "2026/10/01/index.html#paper-2609-38314", "reportDate": "1 October 2026", "searchText": "How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas Damiano F. G. Fiorillo 2609.38314 A linear-dispersion analysis separates symmetry-dependent endpoints of collective neutrino modes from generic branch-cut termination when velocity dispersion is included. 2026-10-01 1 October 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 8. How collective modes terminate in the dispersion relation of neutrino plasmas\n\n**Damiano F. G. Fiorillo**  \n[arXiv:2609.38314](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38314)\n\nThe paper studies how nonzero neutrino masses and the associated velocity dispersion modify singular endpoints of collective flavor-wave branches. Masses remove the frequently seen symmetry-axis degeneracy of fast-limit modes, although degeneracy lines may remain in the full three-dimensional wavevector space. Abrupt termination at a branch cut of the flavor dielectric function is instead generic.\n\nA useful analytic result is that a Lorentzian neutrino-frequency distribution is exactly equivalent to a monochromatic distribution with an effective collision rate, allowing more general spectra to be approximated by sums of simpler components.\n\n##### History\n\nRecent work unified fast, slow and collisionally driven instabilities within a common neutrino-plasma dispersion framework but generally treated mass or collision effects perturbatively around simpler distributions ([arXiv:2505.20389](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20389)). The present result clarifies which apparent mode endpoints are symmetry artifacts and which survive realistic velocity dispersion.\n\nThis is a rigorous linear-dispersion result, not a calculation of nonlinear flavor conversion in a supernova or merger. Inhomogeneity, time-dependent transport, angular structure and backreaction may determine whether any of these modes significantly alter an observable neutrino signal."}, {"id": "paper-2609-35974", "arxiv": "2609.35974", "title": "JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars", "authors": "D. Gilman et al.", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Flux ratios in 29 lensed quasars favor tested populations containing core-collapsed dark-matter halos, with evidence sensitive to globular-cluster and subhalo assumptions.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35974", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars D. Gilman et al. 2609.35974 Flux ratios in 29 lensed quasars favor tested populations containing core-collapsed dark-matter halos, with evidence sensitive to globular-cluster and subhalo assumptions. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★★ 1. JWST lensed quasar dark matter survey V: Hints of self-interacting dark matter from 29 quadruply imaged quasars\n\n**D. Gilman et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.35974](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35974)\n\nThe authors use flux-ratio perturbations in 29 quadruply imaged quasars to infer the abundance of core-collapsed dark-matter halos and subhalos between \\(10^6\\) and \\(10^{10.7}\\,M_\\odot\\). Such deeply collapsed objects are a characteristic late-time outcome of some velocity-dependent self-interacting-dark-matter models.\n\nAfter marginalizing over globular clusters, subhalo abundance, lens macromodels and collapsed-halo structure, the data prefer models containing core-collapsed halos. The reported Bayes factors disfavor collisionless CDM relative to the tested SIDM populations by between \\(6{:}1\\) and \\(24{:}1\\). A CDM-only fit requires both more globular clusters than expected and a subhalo abundance above standard \\(N\\)-body predictions.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier simulations showed that quasar flux ratios should be unusually sensitive to SIDM core collapse, forecasting roughly \\(20{:}1\\) odds against CDM for a favorable 50-lens sample. ([SIDM lensing forecasts](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.05259)) The JWST survey has already used essentially the same sample to set competitive warm-dark-matter limits—\\(m_{\\rm WDM}\\gtrsim5.6\\!-\\!6.4\\) keV depending on the substructure prior—but those analyses tested suppression of halo abundance, not the enhanced central densities generated by collapse. ([JWST warm-dark-matter analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07765))\n\nThis is therefore the first substantial population-level observational hint for SIDM core collapse from strong lenses, rather than another forecast. It is not yet evidence for a unique particle interaction: the odds vary by a factor of four under plausible assumptions about globular clusters and SIDM subhalos, and the inference depends on extrapolating collapsed-halo profiles below scales directly resolved by simulations. Independent lens samples and the promised particle-physics interpretation are essential before treating this as a challenge to CDM rather than a lens-population modeling tension.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35968", "arxiv": "2609.35968", "title": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter", "authors": "Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque", "tags": ["Dark matter", "X rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint spectral and spatial analysis of twenty years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data strengthens sub-GeV dark-matter limits while retaining dependence on astrophysical background and propagation models.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35968", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque 2609.35968 A joint spectral and spatial analysis of twenty years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data strengthens sub-GeV dark-matter limits while retaining dependence on astrophysical background and propagation models. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Dark matter X rays BSM #### ★★★ 2. Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations II: Sub-GeV dark matter\n\n**Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.35968](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35968)\n\nTwenty years of diffuse INTEGRAL/IBIS hard-X-ray data are analyzed jointly in energy and Galactic longitude. The background model combines inverse-Compton emission from cosmic-ray electrons with unresolved accreting white dwarfs; secondary inverse-Compton photons from dark-matter-produced electrons and positrons are propagated consistently.\n\nFor \\(1\\) MeV–\\(5\\) GeV dark matter annihilating or decaying to \\(e^+e^-\\), \\(\\mu^+\\mu^-\\), or \\(\\pi^+\\pi^-\\), the analysis obtains limits of approximately\n\n\\[\n10^{-28}\\lesssim \\langle\\sigma v\\rangle/\n({\\rm cm^3\\,s^{-1}})\\lesssim10^{-26},\n\\qquad\n\\tau\\gtrsim10^{25}\\ {\\rm s}\n\\]\n\nat masses of a few MeV, with improvements reaching two orders of magnitude over previous X-ray bounds. The results are also translated into dark-photon and scalar-Higgs portal parameters.\n\n##### History\n\nThe foundational INTEGRAL analysis already showed that secondary inverse-Compton emission greatly strengthens limits above roughly 20 MeV. ([Earlier INTEGRAL analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11493)) A subsequent combination of INTEGRAL, XMM-Newton, NuSTAR and Suzaku produced leading bounds over parts of the \\(0.1\\!-\\!1\\) GeV range. ([Combined X-ray constraints](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08854)) The present gain comes from twenty years of IBIS exposure and, more importantly, fitting spectral and longitudinal morphology simultaneously instead of treating the diffuse intensity as a one-dimensional bound.\n\nThe improvement is substantial, but not purely exposure-driven or detector-systematics-limited. It relies on the adopted cosmic-ray propagation model, unresolved-white-dwarf template, Galactic radiation fields and dark-matter density profile. These astrophysical nuisance choices should be compared directly with the earlier multi-instrument analysis before the strongest limits are used in global portal-model fits.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-36632", "arxiv": "2609.36632", "title": "Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between 10¹⁶ and 10²⁶ eV", "authors": "S. V. Troitsky", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A common exposure and likelihood framework combines diffuse ultrahigh-energy neutrino searches across ten decades in energy, with machine-readable tables for testing model spectra.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-36632", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between 10¹⁶ and 10²⁶ eV S. V. Troitsky 2609.36632 A common exposure and likelihood framework combines diffuse ultrahigh-energy neutrino searches across ten decades in energy, with machine-readable tables for testing model spectra. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 3. Combined constraints on the diffuse flux of cosmic neutrinos between \\(10^{16}\\) and \\(10^{26}\\) eV\n\n**S. V. Troitsky**  \n[arXiv:2609.36632](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36632)\n\nPublished searches from optical Cherenkov telescopes, air-shower arrays, in-ice and balloon radio detectors, and lunar radio experiments use mutually incompatible flavor, binning and confidence-limit conventions. Troitsky reconstructs their energy-dependent exposures, converts them to a common all-flavor \\(\\nu+\\bar\\nu\\) convention, and combines event counts and backgrounds in a Poisson likelihood.\n\nThe output is a reproducible set of 90%-confidence, one-decade quasi-differential limits over ten orders of magnitude in energy. Model spectra—including cosmogenic neutrinos and selected exotic scenarios—can be folded directly through the combined exposure; machine-readable exposure and limit tables are supplied.\n\n##### History\n\nIceCube and Auger previously published strong quasi-differential constraints, but as experiment-specific results with different energy intervals and statistical definitions. ([IceCube limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01820); [Auger limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.07422)) Broader “unified neutrino spectrum” reviews compiled fluxes through the PeV scale rather than constructing a likelihood combination of ultrahigh-energy null searches. ([Grand Unified Neutrino Spectrum review](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11878))\n\nThe advance is consequently methodological and highly useful, not a new neutrino event or detector result. It appears to be the first reproducible exposure-level combination spanning the full \\(10^{16}\\!-\\!10^{26}\\) eV range. It is nevertheless an external reconstruction from published summaries, not a collaboration-sanctioned joint likelihood; incomplete covariance information, changing cross-section assumptions and detector-specific systematics cannot all be propagated exactly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35956", "arxiv": "2609.35956", "title": "Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST", "authors": "Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty", "tags": ["Compact objects", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST identifies the Rapid Burster's infrared counterpart through strong active-state variability, enabling follow-up studies of its donor and accretion flow.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-35956", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty 2609.35956 JWST identifies the Rapid Burster's infrared counterpart through strong active-state variability, enabling follow-up studies of its donor and accretion flow. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Compact objects X rays #### ★★★ 4. Discovery of the Infrared Counterpart of the Rapid Burster with JWST\n\n**Malina M. Desai, Kevin B. Burdge, Cristina Pallanca, Saul Rappaport, Francesco R. Ferraro, Barbara Lanzoni, Mason Ng, Paul Draghis, Emma T. Chickles, Ilaria Caiazzo, Deepto Chakrabarty**  \n[arXiv:2609.35956](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35956)\n\nJWST/NIRCam observations obtained while MXB 1730−335 was X-ray active identify an unambiguous infrared counterpart to the Rapid Burster—the only known neutron star producing both thermonuclear Type-I and accretion-driven Type-II bursts.\n\nThe source fades by approximately 2.5 magnitudes in F200W and shows strong variability on minute timescales. The identification provides a practical target for infrared spectroscopy and coordinated X-ray/IR observations of the donor and accretion flow.\n\n##### History\n\nChandra, HST and ground-based infrared imaging had narrowed the field but could not isolate a uniquely variable object in Liller 1’s extremely crowded and extinguished core. ([Earlier counterpart search](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0106140)) A 2025 HST/Gemini study proposed a variable optical candidate, explicitly leaving its association unconfirmed. ([2025 optical candidate](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10640)) JWST’s active-state variability converts that candidate-level situation into a compelling identification.\n\nThe result is an observational discovery rather than yet a determination of the binary parameters. The donor’s spectral type, orbital period and the relation between infrared variability and individual Type-II bursts remain unmeasured.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37928", "arxiv": "2609.37928", "title": "Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620", "authors": "Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen", "tags": ["Compact objects", "X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Magnetically motivated pulse-profile models yield a radius consistent with previous NICER results while exposing an unresolved problem in polar-cap heating.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37928", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620 Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen 2609.37928 Magnetically motivated pulse-profile models yield a radius consistent with previous NICER results while exposing an unresolved problem in polar-cap heating. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Compact objects X rays #### ★★★ 5. Joint Mass-Radius and Magnetic-Geometry Inference of PSR J0740+6620\n\n**Chun Huang, Tuomo Salmi, Alexander Y. Chen**  \n[arXiv:2609.37928](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37928)\n\nInstead of fitting phenomenological emitting spots, the authors connect the NICER/XMM pulse profile of the massive pulsar J0740+6620 to two low-dimensional magnetospheric geometries: a centered dipole–quadrupole and a shifted dipole.\n\nThe shifted dipole is favored by\n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\ln Z=5.65,\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a Bayes factor near 285 under the chosen priors. It gives\n\n\\[\nM=2.059^{+0.068}_{-0.069}M_\\odot,\\qquad\nR=13.28^{+1.47}_{-1.07}\\ {\\rm km}.\n\\]\n\nBoth tested configurations require an X-ray footprint considerably smaller than a standard force-free polar cap; exploratory photon-conversion calculations do not reproduce that compactness.\n\n##### History\n\nThe two independent 2024 NICER analyses obtained \\(R=12.49^{+1.28}_{-0.88}\\) km and \\(12.92^{+2.09}_{-1.13}\\) km using flexible hotspot parameterizations. ([Salmi et al. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.14466); [Dittmann et al. (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.14467)) The new radius is fully consistent with both, so this does not materially alter neutron-star equation-of-state constraints. The important result is that a restricted, magnetically motivated hotspot model preserves the radius inference while making a testable statement about polar-cap heating.\n\nOnly two idealized field families were compared, and the preferred model itself fails to explain the required footprint through the simple heating prescription. The large Bayes factor should therefore be read as preference within this narrow model space, not as a definitive magnetic-field reconstruction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37335", "arxiv": "2609.37335", "title": "First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering", "authors": "MINERvA Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MINERvA measures a visible-kinematics superscaling variable that tests nuclear missing-energy biases relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37335", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering MINERvA Collaboration 2609.37335 MINERvA measures a visible-kinematics superscaling variable that tests nuclear missing-energy biases relevant to neutrino-energy reconstruction. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★☆ 6. First Measurement of the Superscaling Variable as a Function of Visible Kinematics in Charged-Current Quasi-Elastic Neutrino–Nucleus Scattering\n\n**MINERvA Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.37335](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37335)\n\nMINERvA measures a visible-kinematics version of the quasielastic superscaling variable, \\(\\psi'_{\\rm vis}\\), in \\(\\nu_\\mu\\)-carbon CCQE-like events. Because its peak is externally calibrated, shifts directly expose nuclear missing energy that would bias reconstructed neutrino energy.\n\nFive nuclear-interaction models are compared through forward folding. Their predictions diverge most strongly—and generally fit least well—at low proton recoil energy and low muon transverse momentum. The relativistic mean-field model gives the best peak position among those tested.\n\n##### History\n\nSuperscaling and RMF/SuSAv2 models were developed using electron scattering and had already been confronted with inclusive neutrino cross sections. ([SuSAv2 model](https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.8346)) The new contribution is an experimentally unfolded, visible final-state variable designed specifically to diagnose missing-energy biases rather than another inclusive-rate comparison.\n\nThat makes it directly useful for oscillation-energy systematics, although extrapolation from carbon and MINERvA’s few-GeV flux to argon experiments still requires generator-level validation. The conclusion that RMF performs best is restricted to the measured projections and the five tested implementations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37422", "arxiv": "2609.37422", "title": "Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset", "authors": "ANTARES Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The full ANTARES dataset yields no solar dark-matter neutrino excess and improves earlier WIMP–proton limits by factors of about two to three.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37422", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset ANTARES Collaboration 2609.37422 The full ANTARES dataset yields no solar dark-matter neutrino excess and improves earlier WIMP–proton limits by factors of about two to three. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter #### ★★☆ 7. Search for dark matter annihilation in the Sun using the full ANTARES dataset\n\n**ANTARES Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.37422](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37422)\n\nThe final 2007–2022 ANTARES exposure is searched for neutrinos from WIMP annihilation in the Sun. No excess is observed. Spin-dependent and spin-independent WIMP–proton limits are reported for masses from 35 GeV to 10 TeV, assuming 100% branching to \\(b\\bar b\\), \\(\\tau^+\\tau^-\\), or \\(W^+W^-\\).\n\nThe limits improve previous ANTARES results by factors of roughly two to three.\n\n##### History\n\nThe earlier analysis used only 2007–2012 data and covered 50 GeV–5 TeV. ([Earlier ANTARES analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02228)) The expanded mass range and factor-of-few sensitivity gain make this the definitive ANTARES solar-WIMP result, but it is an exposure-driven legacy improvement rather than a qualitatively new search technique or globally new WIMP regime.\n\nTranslation from a neutrino-flux limit to a scattering cross section assumes capture–annihilation equilibrium in the Sun, a Galactic velocity distribution and single-channel annihilation. Those assumptions can fail in models with suppressed annihilation or mixed final states.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-37087", "arxiv": "2609.37087", "title": "Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon", "authors": "Yasser Maghrbi", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Trace argon isotopes dominate neutron opacity near a 40Ar interference minimum, affecting simulated neutron-capture calibration geometry in liquid-argon detectors.", "path": "2026/09/30/index.html#paper-2609-37087", "reportDate": "30 September 2026", "searchText": "Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon Yasser Maghrbi 2609.37087 Trace argon isotopes dominate neutron opacity near a 40Ar interference minimum, affecting simulated neutron-capture calibration geometry in liquid-argon detectors. 2026-09-30 30 September 2026 Neutrinos Particle physics #### ★★☆ 8. Trace isotopes set the neutron horizon of liquid argon\n\n**Yasser Maghrbi**  \n[arXiv:2609.37087](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37087)\n\nNear 57 keV, destructive nuclear interference makes \\(^{40}\\)Ar nearly transparent to neutrons. The \\(0.4\\%\\) natural abundance of \\(^{36}\\)Ar and \\(^{38}\\)Ar consequently supplies more than 90% of the total opacity, amplifying their effect by a factor of about 230.\n\nPublished transmission data exclude the \\(^{36}\\)Ar evaluation used by default in FLUKA and instead constrain its cross section to approximately \\(2\\!-\\!11\\) b. In a DUNE-scale simulation, adopting data-consistent evaluations shifts the fiducial fraction of \\(^{41}\\)Ar calibration captures by 9–13%, although the total yield changes by only 2%.\n\n##### History\n\nNeutron-transport studies normally treat natural argon as effectively \\(^{40}\\)Ar; no earlier work appears to have isolated this trace-isotope dominance in the interference window and propagated it to DUNE calibration geometry. The result is novel and potentially operationally important, but rests on existing transmission datasets rather than a new dedicated isotope measurement. A modern \\(^{36}\\)Ar measurement around 57 keV is the clean way to resolve the discrepancy among nuclear-data libraries.\n\nThe 30 September selection includes **no new UHECR spectrum, composition or anisotropy measurement and no new precision oscillation-parameter result**."}, {"id": "paper-2609-32649", "arxiv": "2609.32649", "title": "Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies", "authors": "Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A unified spline fit combines cosmic-ray spectrum and composition measurements from GeV energies to above 100 EeV, with updated all-nucleon fluxes relevant to atmospheric backgrounds.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-32649", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel 2609.32649 A unified spline fit combines cosmic-ray spectrum and composition measurements from GeV energies to above 100 EeV, with updated all-nucleon fluxes relevant to atmospheric backgrounds. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Cosmic rays Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. Global Spline Fit: A unified data-driven view of the cosmic-ray spectrum and mass composition from GeV to the highest energies\n\n**Anatoli Fedynitch, Kozo Fujisue, Hans Dembinski, Ralph Engel**  \n[arXiv:2609.32649](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32649)\n\nThe authors combine elemental AMS-02, CALET and DAMPE spectra, LHAASO knee-region measurements, and Auger fluorescence composition into a single nonparametric fit from GeV energies to above \\(10^{20}\\) eV. Four leading mass groups are represented by cubic splines; experimental energy scales, solar modulation and correlated uncertainties are fitted simultaneously.\n\nApproximately one thousand measurements are described with \\(\\chi^2/{\\rm ndf}\\simeq1.3\\), falling to 0.83 after localized dataset tensions are down-weighted. The derived all-nucleon flux differs from widely used parametrizations by \\(20\\!-\\!50\\%\\) over four decades in energy—larger than the remaining fit uncertainty and therefore potentially important for atmospheric-neutrino and muon calculations.\n\n##### History\n\nThe authors’ [2017 Global Spline Fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.11432) pioneered the same data-driven combination and explicit energy-scale alignment. The present work is a major modernization: it incorporates the precision spaceborne spectra that exposed multiple spectral breaks, LHAASO’s element-separated knee measurements, Auger’s updated composition, solar modulation, and a more useful covariance/reduced representation.\n\nThe practical advance is stronger than merely updating a plot: the large change in the inferred nucleon flux means atmospheric-background calculations based on older H3a/GST-like parameterizations should be revisited. It is still a phenomenological world average, not a source or transport model. Air-shower composition retains hadronic-interaction dependence, four broad mass groups lose elemental information, and the improvement obtained by de-weighting discrepant data shows that full experimental consistency has not literally been achieved.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-34155", "arxiv": "2609.34155", "title": "Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy”", "authors": "Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A correction to simulated dark-matter annihilation templates challenges a claimed morphological degeneracy with the Galactic stellar bulge, without establishing the origin of the GeV excess.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-34155", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy” Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias 2609.34155 A correction to simulated dark-matter annihilation templates challenges a claimed morphological degeneracy with the Galactic stellar bulge, without establishing the origin of the GeV excess. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Dark matter Gamma rays #### ★★★ 2. Comment on “Fermi-LAT Galactic Center Excess Morphology of Dark Matter in Simulations of the Milky Way Galaxy”\n\n**Kevork N. Abazajian, Jason Kumar, Oscar Macias**  \n[arXiv:2609.34155](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34155)\n\nThis Comment identifies a consequential error in the dark-matter templates of Muru et al.: their “quadratic dark matter” map used\n\n\\[\n\\left[\\int \\rho\\,s^2\\,ds\\right]^2\n\\]\n\nrather than the annihilation \\(J\\)-factor,\n\n\\[\nJ=\\int \\rho^2\\,ds.\n\\]\n\nBecause squaring an already projected positive map preserves its isophote geometry, the published similarity between dark-matter and old-stellar-population contours was largely built into the erroneous construction. Recalculation with the proper line-of-sight integral does not support the claimed morphological degeneracy.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original [Muru et al. PRL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06314) argued that realistic, merger-disturbed Milky-Way dark-matter halos could look essentially indistinguishable from the boxy stellar bulge, weakening morphology as a discriminator for the GeV excess. The Comment attacks the central observable rather than a secondary numerical detail; unless the original authors supply a different corrected analysis, their main conclusion should not be used.\n\nThe correction does not establish that the excess is dark matter, nor does it resolve diffuse-emission and point-source systematics. It restores only the narrower conclusion that a correctly calculated annihilation morphology remains meaningfully distinguishable from a projected stellar template in these simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-35697", "arxiv": "2609.35697", "title": "JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst", "authors": "B. Schneider et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "JWST spectroscopy reveals a dust-obscured supernova associated with GRB 240825A, identifying a massive-star progenitor despite earlier optical nondetections.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-35697", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst B. Schneider et al. 2609.35697 JWST spectroscopy reveals a dust-obscured supernova associated with GRB 240825A, identifying a massive-star progenitor despite earlier optical nondetections. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Gamma rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. JWST unveils a dust-obscured supernova associated with a gamma-ray burst\n\n**B. Schneider et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.35697](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35697)\n\nGRB 240825A, at \\(z=0.659\\), had conflicting prompt-emission classifications and no supernova in deep ground-based observations. JWST/NIRSpec spectroscopy obtained 40.1 rest-frame days after the burst is decomposed pixel by pixel into host and transient components.\n\nA point source at the burst position shows broad features resembling SN 1998bw. The sightline has \\(A_V=1.37\\pm0.08\\) mag; the supernova supplies \\(10\\!-\\!15\\%\\) of the NIRSpec flux but would have contributed only about 1% of a contemporaneous ground-based optical image. After extinction correction its luminosity is comparable to SN 1998bw, securely identifying a massive-star progenitor.\n\n##### History\n\nA previous multiwavelength study found hybrid merger/collapsar prompt properties and only an optical limit, \\(m_r>26.1\\), while already suggesting that dust could conceal an ordinary broad-lined Type Ic supernova. JWST converts that inference into a spectroscopic detection. Relative to the foundational GRB 980425/SN 1998bw association, the novelty is not another bright nearby GRB-SN but recovery of one beneath a bright, dusty and unusually metal-rich host.\n\nThis strengthens the warning that some apparently “supernova-less” or duration-ambiguous bursts are obscured collapsars. The inferred luminosity remains sensitive to host subtraction, extinction law and the adopted SN template; a single event cannot establish how common this failure mode is.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-32306", "arxiv": "2609.32306", "title": "Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset", "authors": "NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Combining the NANOGrav background with the absence of resolved continuous-wave sources favors more numerous, lower-mass supermassive-black-hole binaries within the tested population models.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-32306", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al. 2609.32306 Combining the NANOGrav background with the absence of resolved continuous-wave sources favors more numerous, lower-mass supermassive-black-hole binaries within the tested population models. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. Constraints on Supermassive Black Hole Binaries from the Lack of Resolvable Sources in the NANOGrav 15-yr Dataset\n\n**NANOGrav Collaboration; Emiko C. Gardiner et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.32306](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32306)\n\nSemi-analytic supermassive-black-hole-binary populations are confronted jointly with NANOGrav’s stochastic-background spectrum and its nondetection of a convincing continuous wave. The absence of resolved systems disfavors backgrounds dominated by a few ultramassive nearby binaries and instead favors many lower-mass contributors.\n\nCompared with the background-only inference, the joint posterior moves toward higher galaxy-merger rates, smaller scatter in the black-hole–bulge relation, shorter binary lifetimes and steeper environmental hardening. The highest-S/N expected sources cluster around chirp mass \\(10^{9.6}M_\\odot\\), luminosity distance \\(1.3\\) Gpc and frequency \\(6\\) nHz.\n\n##### History\n\nNANOGrav’s 15-year analyses established evidence for a nanohertz background and separately found only weak individual-source candidates near 4 and 170 nHz. The 4-nHz feature lost much of its significance when the correlated background was modeled, while the higher-frequency candidate lacked a plausible host. ([Continuous-wave search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16222))\n\nThis is the first population analysis to use that *absence* quantitatively alongside the detected background, breaking part of the number-versus-mass degeneracy. The result is conditional on semi-analytic prescriptions for galaxy pairing, black-hole scaling relations and sub-parsec hardening. It is not yet a measurement of any one environmental parameter, and the low-frequency candidate should not be interpreted as a resolved binary.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-33990", "arxiv": "2609.33990", "title": "First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium", "authors": "GBAR Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "GBAR measures antihydrogen production cross sections through antiproton–positronium charge exchange at two beam energies, calibrating a reaction needed for future experiments.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-33990", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium GBAR Collaboration 2609.33990 GBAR measures antihydrogen production cross sections through antiproton–positronium charge exchange at two beam energies, calibrating a reaction needed for future experiments. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 5. First measurement of the antihydrogen production cross section through charge exchange of low-energy antiprotons with orthopositronium\n\n**GBAR Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.33990](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33990)\n\nGBAR measures \\(\\bar p+\\mathrm{Ps}\\rightarrow\\bar H+e^-\\) at two antiproton energies:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(6.2\\,{\\rm keV})\n =\\left(14.1\\pm1.3_{\\rm stat}^{+2.2}_{-1.4}{}_{\\rm sys}\\right)\n \\times10^{-16}\\ {\\rm cm^2},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(4.15\\,{\\rm keV})\n =\\left(8.7\\pm2.4_{\\rm stat}^{+1.5}_{-0.09}{}_{\\rm sys}\\right)\n \\times10^{-16}\\ {\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth agree with recent three-body calculations. This is the first direct charge-exchange cross-section measurement made with an antiproton beam.\n\n##### History\n\nGBAR previously reported the first antihydrogen beam produced through this channel, at only \\(3\\!-\\!4\\sigma\\), without extracting a cross section. ([2023 production result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.15801)) The new result converts proof of production into a calibrated atomic-collision measurement and validates the reaction-rate calculations used to design GBAR’s two-step \\(\\bar H^+\\) source.\n\nIt is enabling metrology rather than a gravity or CPT test: only two kinetic energies are measured, the second positron-capture step remains rate-limiting, and no conclusion about antihydrogen free fall follows directly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31833", "arxiv": "2609.31833", "title": "Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics", "authors": "Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The neutrino DSMC framework couples decaying relics, nonthermal neutrino spectra and Big Bang nucleosynthesis, with validation against independent transport solvers.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-31833", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov 2609.31833 The neutrino DSMC framework couples decaying relics, nonthermal neutrino spectra and Big Bang nucleosynthesis, with validation against independent transport solvers. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Neutrino Direct Simulation Monte Carlo: Accurate modeling of out-of-equilibrium decaying cosmological relics\n\n**Kensuke Akita, Miguel Escudero, Oleksii Ihnatenko, Maksym Ovchynnikov**  \n[arXiv:2609.31833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31833)\n\nThe \\(\\nu\\)DSMC framework is extended to evolve a decaying relic, nonthermal neutrino spectra, the electromagnetic plasma, cosmic expansion and Big Bang nucleosynthesis together. It includes inverse decays, flavor conversion, quantum statistics and pion production by energetic neutrinos.\n\nBenchmarking against independent quantum-kinetic and Boltzmann solvers shows excellent agreement, with a computational advantage when spectra become strongly nonthermal. Momentum-averaged treatments can predict even the wrong sign of \\(\\Delta N_{\\rm eff}\\) and substantially incorrect light-element yields.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original \\(\\nu\\)DSMC work introduced Monte Carlo neutrino transport for new-physics cosmologies, while the subsequent precision implementation reproduced standard decoupling at \\(N_{\\rm eff}=3.0439\\pm0.0006\\). ([Method paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.07378); [precision validation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.08379))\n\nThe present paper supplies the missing end-to-end relic-decay and BBN machinery, making it a useful computational advance. It does not yet provide a universal revised exclusion plot: constraints remain model-dependent, and nuclear-rate, hadronic-decay and flavor-coherence uncertainties must still be propagated separately.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-33364", "arxiv": "2609.33364", "title": "Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee", "authors": "Jieshuang Wang", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Fits across the cosmic-ray spectrum favor a Galactic proton contribution beyond 100 PeV, while source-population and transport interpretations remain degenerate.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-33364", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee Jieshuang Wang 2609.33364 Fits across the cosmic-ray spectrum favor a Galactic proton contribution beyond 100 PeV, while source-population and transport interpretations remain degenerate. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 7. Evidence for an extended Galactic proton component beyond the cosmic-ray knee\n\n**Jieshuang Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.33364](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33364)\n\nFits to proton and helium spectra from \\(0.1\\) to \\(1000\\) PeV disfavor interpreting the few-PeV knee as a single terminal proton cutoff. When the fit is anchored down to tens of GeV, a proton contribution extending beyond \\(100\\) PeV is required, either as a continuous component or as a distinct component with a break near the knee.\n\nA transport break can produce the latter behavior without requiring ordinary supernova remnants to accelerate protons continuously to \\(100\\) PeV; extreme Galactic accelerators such as super-Eddington X-ray binaries are discussed as possible contributors.\n\n##### History\n\nLHAASO directly established a proton spectrum that hardens toward 3 PeV and then softens sharply, while its helium spectrum hardens near 1.1 PeV and softens around 7 PeV. Those measurements already suggested multiple Galactic components rather than a featureless rigidity cutoff. ([Protons](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14447); [helium](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.05013))\n\nThe new paper extends this into a full-spectrum consistency argument and makes the \\(>100\\)-PeV proton tail explicit. It is not a new composition measurement, however. Its significance depends on combining experiments with different energy scales and hadronic models, and the data do not distinguish a new source population from energy-dependent transport.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31836", "arxiv": "2609.31836", "title": "Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet", "authors": "Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Minute-scale optical polarization rotation in PKS 1510−089 coincides with a new radio-jet component, supporting a possible interaction between moving and standing shocks.", "path": "2026/09/29/index.html#paper-2609-31836", "reportDate": "29 September 2026", "searchText": "Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall 2609.31836 Minute-scale optical polarization rotation in PKS 1510−089 coincides with a new radio-jet component, supporting a possible interaction between moving and standing shocks. 2026-09-29 29 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 8. Discovery of minute-scale polarization-angle rotation in a supermassive-black-hole jet\n\n**Aristeidis Polychronakis, Ioannis Liodakis, Dmitry Blinov, Anastasia Glykopoulou, Sebastian Kiehlmann, Karan Pal, Georgios F. Paraschos, Lena Debbrecht, Swati Ravi, Herman L. Marshall**  \n[arXiv:2609.31836](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31836)\n\nOptical monitoring of PKS 1510−089 finds a coherent \\(136^\\circ\\) polarization-angle rotation in about 80 minutes, corresponding to roughly \\(2450^\\circ\\) per day. Contemporaneous 43-GHz VLBA imaging shows a new component leaving the radio core with apparent speed \\(17.8c\\) and inferred Doppler factor near 42.\n\nThe authors favor interaction between a moving shock and a standing-shock complex. The stable, low polarization degree and absence of detected periodicity or a strong broadband flare are argued against simple turbulent-walk, kink and reconnection interpretations.\n\n##### History\n\nPreviously catalogued coherent rotations normally lasted days; the earlier speed record was approximately \\(360^\\circ\\) per day. A 2014 rotation in 3C 454.3 had similar structure and a component ejection but lasted about twelve hours. Correcting for the different Doppler factors brings its intrinsic timescale within roughly \\(2\\sigma\\) of the present event.\n\nThe new observation is the first resolved intra-night rotation, not an entirely new physical mechanism. The shock interpretation is plausible but circumstantial: the VLBA cadence does not time the interaction to 80-minute precision, and Fermi-LAT’s unfavorable viewing geometry prevents a meaningful test for a simultaneous short gamma-ray flare."}, {"id": "paper-2609-31457", "arxiv": "2609.31457", "title": "4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources", "authors": "Fermi-LAT Collaboration", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The 4FHL catalog uses 16 years of Fermi-LAT observations to identify 673 sources between 50 GeV and 2 TeV, improving sensitivity and localization over 2FHL.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31457", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources Fermi-LAT Collaboration 2609.31457 The 4FHL catalog uses 16 years of Fermi-LAT observations to identify 673 sources between 50 GeV and 2 TeV, improving sensitivity and localization over 2FHL. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★★ 1. 4FHL: The Fourth Catalog of Hard Fermi-LAT Sources\n\n**Fermi-LAT Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.31457](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31457)\n\nUsing 16 years of LAT data, the collaboration constructs the deepest all-sky catalog yet in the \\(50~{\\rm GeV}-2~{\\rm TeV}\\) band. The catalog contains **673 sources**:\n\n- 84.7% have extragalactic associations, predominantly BL Lac objects;\n- 36 associated sources lie at \\(z>1\\);\n- approximately 10% are Galactic, chiefly pulsar-wind nebulae and supernova remnants;\n- about 5% remain unassociated or uncertain;\n- 20 have no counterparts in previous LAT catalogs.\n\nSensitivity and localization are each improved by approximately a factor of two relative to the previous catalog covering the same energy range.\n\n##### History\n\nThe 2015 [2FHL catalog](https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.04449) used 80 months of Pass-8 data and contained 360 sources above 50 GeV. 4FHL nearly doubles that sample while preserving the same energy threshold, making this a major catalog-level advance rather than a routine duration update. The intervening 3FHL catalog contained more objects but extended down to 10 GeV, so it is not the appropriate direct sensitivity comparison.\n\nThe larger sample should materially improve blazar luminosity-function, extragalactic-background and CTA-target studies. Its principal selection limitation is the 16-year time average: steady hard-spectrum sources are favored over brief flares. At high redshift, EBL absorption also makes the 36 \\(z>1\\) objects a spectrally selected subset rather than an unbiased blazar population.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30366", "arxiv": "2609.30366", "title": "Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures", "authors": "Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Hybrid Partial Dressing combines all-temperature scalar-potential resummation with two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction in its controlled regime.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30366", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy 2609.30366 Hybrid Partial Dressing combines all-temperature scalar-potential resummation with two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction in its controlled regime. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 2. Hybrid Partial Dressing: Correct Effective Potentials at All Temperatures\n\n**Raphaël Berthiaume, David Curtin, Michael Luke, Andrija Rasovic, Jyotirmoy Roy**  \n[arXiv:2609.30366](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30366)\n\nThe authors introduce Hybrid Partial Dressing, a resummation prescription for finite-temperature scalar effective potentials. It is designed to remain valid outside the high-temperature expansion while being exactly correct through two loops and avoiding the overlapping-momentum problem of conventional partial dressing.\n\nIn multi-scalar \\(\\mathbb Z_2\\)-breaking examples, HPD reproduces two-loop dimensional reduction in the high-temperature regime and continues smoothly into temperatures where dimensional reduction becomes unreliable. Renormalization-group improvement is incorporated, and the resulting potentials are used to calculate first-order-transition gravitational-wave spectra.\n\n##### History\n\nConventional Parwani and Arnold–Espinosa daisy prescriptions are easy to use but can miscount diagrams or fail when thermal and zero-temperature masses are comparable. The authors’ earlier [Partial Dressing framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00466) removed the high-temperature restriction, while [Optimized Partial Dressing](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08218) improved numerical convergence and substantially altered some gravitational-wave forecasts. HPD’s advance is to combine all-temperature applicability with explicit two-loop exactness and agreement with dimensional reduction where the latter is controlled.\n\nThis potentially removes an important theoretical systematic from BSM phase-transition scans. The present demonstrations are nevertheless scalar theories. Gauge dependence, gauge-boson resummation and realistic multi-field tunneling calculations remain to be established before HPD can be treated as a universal replacement for existing electroweak-transition pipelines.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30378", "arxiv": "2609.30378", "title": "Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples", "authors": "Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Population synthesis of hierarchical stellar triples reproduces several observed black-hole merger mass and spin distributions, while formation-channel degeneracies remain.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30378", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak 2609.30378 Population synthesis of hierarchical stellar triples reproduces several observed black-hole merger mass and spin distributions, while formation-channel degeneracies remain. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 3. Mass and spin properties of black-hole mergers reveal formation in triples\n\n**Jakob Stegmann, Aleksandra Olejak**  \n[arXiv:2609.30378](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30378)\n\nPopulation-synthesis and secular-dynamics calculations follow massive hierarchical triples through stellar evolution and tertiary-induced black-hole mergers. The resulting population simultaneously reproduces several structures inferred from current gravitational-wave data:\n\n\\[\nm_1\\simeq10\\,M_\\odot\n\\]\n\nas the dominant primary-mass peak, a preference for nearly equal masses, and broad, asymmetric distributions of the effective and precessing-spin parameters \\(\\chi_{\\rm eff}\\) and \\(\\chi_p\\). The model predicts a sharp decline near \\(m_1\\simeq30\\,M_\\odot\\), above which another formation channel may dominate.\n\n##### History\n\nField-triple calculations previously established that Lidov–Kozai excitation can produce mergers with misaligned spins and \\(\\chi_{\\rm eff}\\) concentrated near zero ([Antonini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06614); [Fragione and Kocsis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.00407)). Separate work found that triples can produce distinctive mass-ratio distributions, but did not claim agreement with the complete observed population. The authors’ own recent hierarchical inference argued statistically that a large triple contribution is favored ([Stegmann et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15873)).\n\nThe new paper supplies the missing forward-model demonstration: one channel can reproduce the low-mass peak, mass ratios and two spin observables together. That is substantive, but “reveal formation in triples” remains stronger than the evidence warrants. Triple initial conditions, natal kicks, common-envelope evolution and stellar-spin transport are uncertain, while selection effects and mixtures of isolated-binary, cluster and triple channels can imitate parts of the same distributions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31611", "arxiv": "2609.31611", "title": "A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport", "authors": "Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A six-dimensional Boltzmann neutrino solver is integrated with Gmunu, using interaction-kernel factorization to reduce the cost of implicit collision calculations.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31611", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li 2609.31611 A six-dimensional Boltzmann neutrino solver is integrated with Gmunu, using interaction-kernel factorization to reduce the cost of implicit collision calculations. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Neutrinos Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. A Multidimensional General-Relativistic Boltzmann Solver for Neutrino Transport\n\n**Arthur Offermans, Harry Ho-Yin Ng, Patrick Chi-Kit Cheong, Anthony Mezzacappa, Tjonnie Guang Feng Li**  \n[arXiv:2609.31611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31611)\n\nThe full six-dimensional Boltzmann equation for neutrinos is incorporated into the general-relativistic MHD code `Gmunu`, using finite-volume discretization in spatial, energy and angular coordinates.\n\nA factorization based on the Legendre expansion of interaction kernels reduces the cost of the stiff implicit collision solve. In a one-dimensional core-collapse snapshot with 14 angular bins, it is approximately **300 times faster** than full-matrix LU decomposition. Tests show quadratic numerical convergence and roughly 1% energy conservation with 20 energy bins. Compared with M1 transport, free-streaming luminosities can differ by about 10%, although mean energies and fluid profiles at bounce agree reasonably well.\n\n##### History\n\nFully general-relativistic multidimensional Boltzmann solvers already exist, notably the discrete-ordinate implementation of [Akaho et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.10780), while three-dimensional supernova calculations with six-dimensional transport have been demonstrated in Newtonian or approximate-GR settings ([Iwakami et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.02091)). The novelty is therefore not the first multidimensional Boltzmann calculation, but the flexible integration with a GRMHD framework and the large reduction in the species- and energy-coupled collision cost.\n\nThe validation remains mostly one-dimensional and near core bounce. The decisive test will be whether the optimization makes long-duration multidimensional GRMHD simulations practical while retaining sufficient angular and energy resolution; the quoted 300-fold gain is relative to an especially expensive dense-matrix baseline.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30616", "arxiv": "2609.30616", "title": "Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110", "authors": "Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A homogeneous 43-burst DSA-110 sample links host dispersion, rotation measures and scattering to dense, magnetized host-galaxy environments.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30616", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110 Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor 2609.30616 A homogeneous 43-burst DSA-110 sample links host dispersion, rotation measures and scattering to dense, magnetized host-galaxy environments. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 5. Tracing the Host-Galaxy Environments of FRBs with a New Sample of Localized Sources Detected with the DSA-110\n\n**Frank M. Verdi, Vikram Ravi, Myles B. Sherman, Jakob T. Faber, Stella Koch Ocker, Kritti Sharma, Casey J. Law, Gregg Hallinan, Kaitlyn Shin, Liam Connor**  \n[arXiv:2609.30616](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30616)\n\nThe paper presents 24 localized DSA-110 FRBs with spectroscopic host redshifts, including four near or beyond \\(z=1\\), and combines them with earlier detections to form a homogeneous 43-burst sample.\n\nAfter subtracting Milky-Way and intergalactic contributions, host dispersion and rotation measures are significantly correlated. Scattered bursts also approximately follow the scattering–DM relation measured for Galactic pulsars. The joint evidence indicates that much of the excess DM, RM and temporal broadening originates in dense, magnetized host-galaxy plasma, plausibly H II regions associated with young magnetars.\n\n##### History\n\nThe first uniform DSA-110 sample contained 11 localized FRBs, while an earlier polarimetric analysis of ten DSA events already indicated appreciable host RM contributions ([Law et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03344); [Sherman et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06816)). The new 43-object sample turns those hints into a joint DM–RM–scattering environmental result and extends the redshift baseline beyond unity.\n\nThe conclusion favors young, core-collapse magnetars but does not uniquely identify them: compact binaries or older neutron stars passing through dense gas could produce similar propagation measures. Host inclination, uncertain halo/intergalactic DM subtraction and DSA’s fluence and scattering selection functions must be propagated before inferring the intrinsic progenitor fraction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-30376", "arxiv": "2609.30376", "title": "Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution", "authors": "Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Kinetic and fluid-PIC simulations identify gyro-phase bunching as the saturation mechanism of a cosmic-ray streaming instability in the studied shell-distribution regime.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-30376", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer 2609.30376 Kinetic and fluid-PIC simulations identify gyro-phase bunching as the saturation mechanism of a cosmic-ray streaming instability in the studied shell-distribution regime. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 6. Saturation Mechanism of Cosmic Ray Streaming Instabilities with a Shell Distribution\n\n**Mohamad Shalaby, Rouven Lemmerz, Christoph Pfrommer**  \n[arXiv:2609.30376](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30376)\n\nFully kinetic PIC and fluid-PIC simulations show that a gyroresonant cosmic-ray streaming instability saturates through **gyro-phase bunching around the excited Alfvén waves**. Simulations with and without Landau closures reach the same saturation amplitude and fully isotropize the cosmic rays in the wave frame.\n\nAt the simulated parameters,\n\n\\[\nv_A=0.01c,\\qquad n_{\\rm CR}/n_i=0.01,\n\\]\n\nnonlinear Landau damping is therefore not responsible for either saturation or isotropization. Increasing the domain size by a factor of ten leaves the saturation amplitude unchanged, supporting a local wave–particle mechanism.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier PIC and MHD-PIC studies established quasilinear growth, nonlinear pitch-angle scattering and eventual isotropization, but generally characterized saturation through wave growth, damping and redistribution of the CR anisotropy ([Holcomb and Spitkovsky](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01951); [Bai et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10219)). The new result identifies a more specific microscopic mechanism and explicitly falsifies nonlinear Landau damping as the controlling process in this regime.\n\nThe extrapolation caveat is unusually important: the adopted CR density is many orders of magnitude above typical diffuse-ISM values, and \\(v_A\\) is also comparatively large. The result challenges a common closure assumption in galaxy simulations but does not yet demonstrate that gyro-phase bunching controls saturation under realistic Galactic conditions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-31340", "arxiv": "2609.31340", "title": "Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry", "authors": "Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Active-neutrino flavor equilibration can interrupt delayed resonant sterile-neutrino production, causing the dark-matter abundance to saturate at high initial lepton asymmetry.", "path": "2026/09/28/index.html#paper-2609-31340", "reportDate": "28 September 2026", "searchText": "Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov 2609.31340 Active-neutrino flavor equilibration can interrupt delayed resonant sterile-neutrino production, causing the dark-matter abundance to saturate at high initial lepton asymmetry. 2026-09-28 28 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Natural Saturation of Sterile-Neutrino Dark-Matter Resonant Production by a High Lepton Flavor Asymmetry\n\n**Dmitry Gorbunov, Dmitry Kalashnikov**  \n[arXiv:2609.31340](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31340)\n\nAt very large primordial flavor asymmetry, the active–sterile resonance occurs later and at lower temperature. The authors show that this can overlap with the onset of active-neutrino flavor oscillations, which redistribute or erase the asymmetry driving resonant sterile-neutrino production.\n\nConsequently, the final abundance saturates: increasing the initial asymmetry no longer proportionally increases sterile-dark-matter production. The surviving resonant-production region spans approximately two orders of magnitude in the mixing parameter \\(\\sin^2 2\\theta\\).\n\n##### History\n\nThe Shi–Fuller mechanism has long relied on a lepton asymmetry to resonantly produce colder sterile dark matter. Recent work reopened much larger asymmetries consistent with BBN and used them to enlarge the viable production region ([Vogel and Abazajian](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18752)). The present paper adds a countervailing effect that was not included in that simple monotonic picture: sufficiently delayed production encounters active-flavor equilibration and ceases to benefit from a larger initial asymmetry.\n\nThe conclusion is sensitive to flavor-dependent initial conditions and the treatment of quantum kinetics through the QCD epoch. Its discussion of pion condensation and an associated gravitational-wave transition is more speculative and should be separated from the better-motivated abundance-saturation result."}, {"id": "paper-2609-29911", "arxiv": "2609.29911", "title": "Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In", "authors": "Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A flavor-covariant quantum-kinetic calculation finds strong in-medium suppression of sterile-neutrino production by electroweak gauge-boson decays in the Standard Model plasma.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29911", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler 2609.29911 A flavor-covariant quantum-kinetic calculation finds strong in-medium suppression of sterile-neutrino production by electroweak gauge-boson decays in the Standard Model plasma. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 1. Plasma Effects Suppress Mixing-Induced Collisional Freeze-In\n\n**Shao-Ping Li, Josef Pradler**  \n[arXiv:2609.29911](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29911)\n\nThe paper examines whether electroweak gauge-boson decays can produce keV–MeV sterile-neutrino dark matter through its small vacuum mixing with active neutrinos. A flavor-covariant Kadanoff–Baym calculation shows that the collision rate is controlled by the **in-medium**, rather than vacuum, mixing angle:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 2\\theta_{\\rm eff}=\n\\frac{m_N^4\\sin^2 2\\theta}\n{(m_N^2\\cos2\\theta-\\Pi_\\nu)^2+m_N^4\\sin^22\\theta}.\n\\]\n\nFor a 10-keV sterile neutrino, the predicted abundance is approximately \\(10^{20}\\) times smaller than in the semiclassical calculation; across the studied range the suppression is \\(10^{12}-10^{24}\\). Production from the Standard Model plasma therefore cannot supply all the dark matter.\n\n##### History\n\n[Datta, Roshan and Sil](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.02030) previously used vacuum mixing in a Boltzmann collision term and found viable gauge-boson freeze-in. Conversely, [Lello, Boyanovsky and Pisarski](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.07647) found strong medium suppression from finite-temperature Dirac equations. The new paper resolves that disagreement: the plasma rotates the propagation eigenstates, a multiplicative effect that adding thermal masses only to phase space misses. This is a substantial closure of a claimed production window, not a small rate correction. The conclusion applies to minimal Standard-Model-plasma production; resonant production from a lepton asymmetry, decays of additional heavy particles and other nonthermal channels remain viable. Narrow-width and quasiparticle approximations also warrant further checking near possible resonances.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28668", "arxiv": "2609.28668", "title": "Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields", "authors": "E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A voltage-sampling model connects AGN current-sheet acceleration with Auger composition measurements, favoring proton-poor injection in tested nearby-source scenarios.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28668", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas 2609.28668 A voltage-sampling model connects AGN current-sheet acceleration with Auger composition measurements, favoring proton-poor injection in tested nearby-source scenarios. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. Composition-dependent acceleration of UHE Cosmic Rays in AGN electric fields\n\n**E. Koutsoumpou, I. Contopoulos, A. Nathanail, A. Loules, D. Kazanas**  \n[arXiv:2609.28668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28668)\n\nThe authors connect particle acceleration in near-horizon current sheets of magnetically arrested AGN disks directly to Auger composition observables. Nuclei sample a log-normal distribution of effective voltage fractions \\(\\eta\\), acquire energy proportional to \\(Z\\eta\\mathcal V_{\\max}\\), and are propagated through photodisintegration, photopion, Bethe–Heitler and adiabatic losses.\n\nBayesian fits to the 2025 Auger deep-learning \\(X_{\\max}\\) moments favor a proton-poor injection at source distances of 10–30 Mpc. At 30 Mpc,\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\ln\\eta}=0.59^{+0.13}_{-0.10},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a factor \\(\\sim1.8\\) dispersion in sampled voltage. A template containing about 53% hydrogen is disfavored by \\(\\Delta\\chi^2=18-26\\); both tested templates fit poorly at 100 Mpc.\n\n##### History\n\nGRMHD simulations had already identified reconnection layers and large electric potentials in magnetically arrested disks ([Ripperda et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.15115); [Nathanail et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03689)), while Auger source fits had established that hard, relatively heavy injection can reproduce the spectrum and composition ([Auger combined fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07155)). The new contribution is the explicit mapping from stochastic voltage sampling to both the mean and width of the latest [Auger \\(X_{\\max}\\) distributions](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.111.022003). The preferred composition is not independently derived—the successful injection template is itself Auger-inspired—and the log-normal voltage distribution is phenomenological. A joint fit of spectrum, \\(X_{\\max}\\), anisotropy and source population would be needed before interpreting the distance preference as evidence for nearby AGN.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-29761", "arxiv": "2609.29761", "title": "Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars", "authors": "Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Neutrinos", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A large equation-of-state ensemble correlates kHz and MHz gravitational-wave signals with a delayed neutrino burst from phase transitions in proto-neutron stars.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29761", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla 2609.29761 A large equation-of-state ensemble correlates kHz and MHz gravitational-wave signals with a delayed neutrino burst from phase transitions in proto-neutron stars. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Multimessenger Neutrinos Compact objects #### ★★★ 3. Multi-messenger and multi-band signal from first-order phase transitions in proto-neutron stars\n\n**Christian Ecker, Mauro Giliberti, Luciano Rezzolla**  \n[arXiv:2609.29761](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29761)\n\nA first-order hadron–quark transition in a proto-neutron star should produce three correlated signals: a kHz gravitational-wave burst from global stellar contraction and oscillation, MHz acoustic gravitational waves from colliding quark bubbles, and a delayed second neutrino burst.\n\nThe authors evolve accreting stars through approximately \\(5\\times10^4\\) model-agnostic equations of state. A 5% measurement of the kHz mode would reduce the logarithmic uncertainty in the predicted MHz frequency interval by about 8.7%. Their weighted population gives a median delay of roughly 2.1 seconds between the ordinary collapse neutrino burst and the transition-driven second burst.\n\n##### History\n\nPhase-transition-induced kHz emission and second neutrino bursts were studied separately in collapse simulations ([Abdikamalov et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1700); [Sagert et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4225)), while bubble-collision MHz emission has recently been proposed for compact-star transitions ([Blas et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03171)). This is the first systematic attempt to correlate all three signals across a large equation-of-state ensemble, and apparently the first population estimate of the neutrino delay. The forecast is not yet a realistic detectability calculation: the stellar evolution uses cold equations of state, the kHz amplitude assumes maximally efficient mode excitation, and the MHz calculation adapts a simplified sound-shell treatment developed for cosmological transitions. MHz detection itself remains highly speculative.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28629", "arxiv": "2609.28629", "title": "Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies", "authors": "Y. Dong et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A systematic CHIME/FRB Outrigger search identifies dwarf hosts and investigates metallicity differences between repeating and nonrepeating fast radio bursts.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28629", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies Y. Dong et al. 2609.28629 A systematic CHIME/FRB Outrigger search identifies dwarf hosts and investigates metallicity differences between repeating and nonrepeating fast radio bursts. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 4. Probing the Metallicity Dependence of Fast Radio Burst Progenitors with CHIME/FRB Outrigger Dwarf Host Galaxies\n\n**Y. Dong et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.28629](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28629)\n\nAmong 78 accurately localized CHIME/FRB Outrigger events, the authors identify eight dwarf-galaxy candidates and spectroscopically confirm five new dwarf hosts. Combining these with the literature suggests a metallicity-dependent split:\n\n- nonrepeaters preferentially occupy relatively metal-rich dwarfs, generally \\(12+\\log({\\rm O/H})\\gtrsim8.35\\);\n- repeaters preferentially occupy lower-metallicity hosts;\n- nearly every repeater with a persistent radio source lies below approximately 8.15.\n\nNo correlation exceeding \\(3\\sigma\\) is found between metallicity and host dispersion, rotation measure or scattering.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier demographic studies established that FRB hosts span a wide galaxy population ([Gordon et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05465)), while the DSA sample suggested an overall preference for massive, metal-rich star-forming galaxies ([Sharma et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16964)). Individual nonrepeaters in dwarfs were already known ([Bhandari et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.16790)). The advance is a systematic, uniformly selected dwarf-host search with direct gas-phase metallicities, revealing that the global high-metallicity preference may conceal different repeater and nonrepeater channels. The evidence remains sample-limited—particularly for repeaters—and “nonrepeating” is an exposure-dependent classification. Strong-line metallicity calibrations, heterogeneous literature follow-up and optical host-selection effects can shift the proposed thresholds.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28651", "arxiv": "2609.28651", "title": "Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria", "authors": "Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Relativistic MHD simulations find diverse stable mixed magnetic-field configurations in neutron stars that retain memory of their initial current geometry.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28651", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu 2609.28651 Relativistic MHD simulations find diverse stable mixed magnetic-field configurations in neutron stars that retain memory of their initial current geometry. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 5. Dynamically Stable Magnetic Fields in Relativistic Neutron Stars: A Diverse Landscape of GRMHD Equilibria\n\n**Mai Li, Vasileios Paschalidis, Antonios Tsokaros, Maria Mutz, Koji Uryu**  \n[arXiv:2609.28651](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28651)\n\nFully relativistic MHD simulations evolve slowly rotating neutron stars with several self-consistent mixed poloidal–toroidal initial fields for tens of Alfvén times. The relaxed fields are neither unique nor universally dipolar: they retain memory of the initial current geometry and contain substantial higher multipoles.\n\nA more limited regularity survives—the angle-averaged toroidal-to-poloidal field-amplitude ratio is typically 20–40% through much of the stellar interior.\n\n##### History\n\nPurely poloidal and toroidal configurations were long known to be unstable, and previous calculations often suggested relaxation toward a broadly “twisted-torus” geometry ([Ciolfi and Rezzolla](https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3971); [Sur et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.11858)). By starting from multiple self-consistent current structures and decomposing the relaxed fields into vector spherical harmonics, the new simulations demonstrate that the twisted-torus picture is not a unique dynamical attractor. This materially changes the interpretation of magnetar internal-field models. Stability is established only in ideal GRMHD over dynamical times: resistive evolution, Hall drift, crust formation, rapid rotation and realistic stratification could erase or reorganize the retained multipoles.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-29162", "arxiv": "2609.29162", "title": "Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe", "authors": "Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Timing measurements from 26 binary pulsars constrain the three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter and the presence of nearby unseen objects.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-29162", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan 2609.29162 Timing measurements from 26 binary pulsars constrain the three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter and the presence of nearby unseen objects. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 6. Binary-pulsar Timing: A 3D Solar-System Accelerometer and Unknown-Source Probe\n\n**Zheng-Long Wang, Zi-Qing Xia, Bo Zhang, Yi-Zhong Fan**  \n[arXiv:2609.29162](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.29162)\n\nOrbital-period derivatives from 26 detached binary pulsars are combined to reconstruct the full three-dimensional acceleration of the Solar-System barycenter. No anomalous acceleration is detected. Directional 95%-confidence limits are \\(0.198\\), \\(0.262\\) and \\(0.693~\\mu{\\rm as\\,yr^{-1}}\\) over 50%, 75% and the full sampled sky.\n\nIncluding both the observer- and pulsar-side response excludes a \\(10\\,M_\\odot\\) unseen object within 0.39, 0.34 and 0.21 pc over the corresponding sky fractions.\n\n##### History\n\n[Zakamska and Tremaine](https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0506548) introduced astronomical-clock limits on peculiar Solar-System acceleration, and later timing plus VLBI of PSR J0437−4715 improved the single-direction constraint. The new network reconstruction is the first three-dimensional binary-pulsar measurement and improves historical pulsar limits by factors of 12–28; it is also tighter than a matched Gaia EDR3 comparison over most directions. The mass bounds improve on a rescaled INPOP19a benchmark beyond roughly 0.2 pc, but that comparison is not confidence-matched. Full timing covariances are unavailable for much of the heterogeneous sample, only six or seven pulsars dominate the precision, and the method tests quasi-static perturbers rather than rapid flybys.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28953", "arxiv": "2609.28953", "title": "Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114", "authors": "Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A GW250114 ringdown analysis measures a quadratic-mode amplitude-coupling coefficient and compares it with numerical-relativity and perturbative predictions.", "path": "2026/09/25/index.html#paper-2609-28953", "reportDate": "25 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114 Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu 2609.28953 A GW250114 ringdown analysis measures a quadratic-mode amplitude-coupling coefficient and compares it with numerical-relativity and perturbative predictions. 2026-09-25 25 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 7. Constraining the Quadratic-mode Amplitude Coupling in GW250114\n\n**Yuxin Yang, Changfu Shi, Yi-Ming Hu**  \n[arXiv:2609.28953](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28953)\n\nThe GW250114 ringdown is fitted with the linear \\(220\\) mode, its first overtone \\(221\\), and the quadratic \\(220\\times220\\) mode. The analysis directly infers the nonlinear amplitude-coupling coefficient, which second-order perturbation theory predicts should depend on the remnant spin.\n\nThe measurement agrees with both numerical-relativity fits and second-order black-hole perturbation calculations; none differs from the posterior by more than \\(1.3\\sigma\\). Present data cannot discriminate between the competing spin-dependent predictions.\n\n##### History\n\nEarlier analyses reported evidence that a quadratic mode improves the GW250114 ringdown fit ([Yang, Shi and Hu](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.16903)) and, using more restrictive merger-informed priors, claimed approximately \\(3\\sigma\\) evidence for several nonlinear modes ([Wang et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05734)). The new paper advances from asking whether a quadratic component is present to measuring its mode-specific coupling coefficient. Agreement with theory is encouraging but not yet a precision nonlinear-gravity test: the result remains sensitive to ringdown start time, the assumed mode set and correlations with the \\(221\\) overtone, and it cannot distinguish numerical-relativity from perturbative predictions."}, {"id": "paper-2609-28457", "arxiv": "2609.28457", "title": "Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons", "authors": "Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A quantum-kinetic parameter scan of the νMSM links baryogenesis and sterile-neutrino dark matter to experimentally testable heavy neutral leptons below approximately 50 GeV.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-28457", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov 2609.28457 A quantum-kinetic parameter scan of the νMSM links baryogenesis and sterile-neutrino dark matter to experimentally testable heavy neutral leptons below approximately 50 GeV. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 1. Sterile Neutrino Dark Matter Cries for GeV Heavy Neutral Leptons\n\n**Marco Drewes, Yannis Georis, Juraj Klarić, Mikhail Shaposhnikov, Inar Timiryasov**  \n[arXiv:2609.28457](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28457)\n\nThe authors perform the first modern parameter scan of the \\(\\nu\\)MSM that consistently evolves the quantum kinetics of its three right-handed neutrinos through baryogenesis, late-time lepton-asymmetry production and resonant sterile-neutrino dark-matter production.\n\nFor a benchmark \\(7\\)-keV dark-matter state, producing the required late lepton asymmetry confines the heavier quasi-degenerate pair to\n\n\\[\nM_{2,3}\\lesssim 50~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\n\nwith mixing angles above the minimal seesaw floor throughout the meson-decay range. A newly identified self-amplified MSW “ignition” mechanism reopens masses below the \\(B\\)-meson threshold. SHiP should cover the complete allowed region below approximately \\(2\\) GeV and a significant part up to \\(5\\) GeV; LHC and future lepton-collider searches cover higher masses.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original comprehensive \\(\\nu\\)MSM scans found regions simultaneously compatible with neutrino masses, baryogenesis and resonantly produced dark matter, but used kinetic treatments later shown to be incomplete. ([Canetti et al. 2013](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.3902)) Subsequent calculations improved thermal rates and resonance dynamics without producing a new global scan. ([Ghiglieri and Laine 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08814), [2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10766)) This paper closes that technical gap and turns the all-in-one \\(\\nu\\)MSM explanation into a bounded, experimentally testable target rather than a broadly open scenario. Important residual uncertainties arise below \\(M\\simeq500\\) MeV, where hadronic HNL decays become difficult to treat, and above \\(50\\) GeV, where the employed Fermi-theory description breaks down. The quantitative region also depends on the assumed sterile-dark-matter mass and its astrophysical constraints.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26965", "arxiv": "2609.26965", "title": "Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a 3+1 sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference", "authors": "Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An independent, trial-calibrated reconstruction finds a residual 2.7σ preference for 3+1 oscillations alongside at least 2.5σ tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26965", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a 3+1 sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad 2609.26965 An independent, trial-calibrated reconstruction finds a residual 2.7σ preference for 3+1 oscillations alongside at least 2.5σ tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★★ 2. Tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within a \\(3+1\\) sterile-neutrino framework using simulation-based inference\n\n**Julia P. Woodward, Austin Schneider, Joshua Villarreal, John Hardin, Janet Conrad**  \n[arXiv:2609.26965](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26965)\n\nA common simulation and statistical framework is constructed for MiniBooNE and the MicroBooNE BNB and NuMI samples. Unlike the published analyses, post-hoc data-driven flux-correction factors are omitted consistently from both experiments.\n\nTrial-calibrated simulation-based inference gives a residual preference for \\(3+1\\) oscillations of\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma\n\\]\n\nafter MicroBooNE is included. The parameter-goodness-of-fit test nevertheless finds at least \\(2.5\\sigma\\) tension between MiniBooNE and MicroBooNE within the minimal model.\n\n##### History\n\nMiniBooNE reported a \\(4.8\\sigma\\) electron-like excess and a roughly \\(4.6\\sigma\\) oscillation preference, whereas MicroBooNE’s inclusive electron-neutrino searches found no corresponding excess. ([MiniBooNE final sample](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16883), [MicroBooNE Wire-Cell search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13978)) A previous combined fit still admitted parts of \\(3+1\\) parameter space, but relied on asymptotic likelihood statistics and less complete MicroBooNE information. ([Previous combined fit](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01724)) The new result supplies the most carefully trial-calibrated direct consistency test to date and shows that neither “MicroBooNE completely eliminates MiniBooNE” nor “the combined data prefer sterile neutrinos” adequately summarizes the situation: the residual preference is modest, and the two datasets are themselves mutually uncomfortable in \\(3+1\\).\n\nThis is an independent reconstruction, not a collaboration likelihood. MicroBooNE has not released event-level Monte Carlo containing all true and reconstructed variables, forcing the authors to approximate detector efficiencies and smearing. The quoted tension therefore should not be treated as a final experimental combination.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26888", "arxiv": "2609.26888", "title": "The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass", "authors": "Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An Abelian Higgs realization of photon mass is studied across magnetic environments, distinguishing symmetry-restored, Meissner and vortex phases and their applicable constraints.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26888", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang 2609.26888 An Abelian Higgs realization of photon mass is studied across magnetic environments, distinguishing symmetry-restored, Meissner and vortex phases and their applicable constraints. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 3. The universe as a superconductor: How to search for a photon mass\n\n**Andrei Gruzinov, Anson Hook, Junwu Huang**  \n[arXiv:2609.26888](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26888)\n\nConventional photon-mass limits normally assume an explicit Proca mass. Here the mass instead arises through an Abelian Higgs field, making electromagnetism analogous to a superconductor with Meissner, vortex and symmetry-restored phases.\n\nBecause ambient magnetic fields can restore the \\(U(1)\\) symmetry, a photon may be massive in vacuum but effectively massless on Earth or inside galaxies. For a Type-I realization, the authors find that conventional limits can formally permit a vacuum mass as large as\n\n\\[\nm_\\gamma\\sim 0.3~{\\rm MeV}.\n\\]\n\nFor Type-II models, a generalized magnetohydrodynamic calculation makes Galactic-dynamo operation the principal constraint. The authors identify fast-radio-burst dispersion, frequency-dependent cosmic opacity, magnetically shielded laboratory volumes and space-based Coulomb-law tests as complementary probes.\n\n##### History\n\nIt has been known for two decades that vortices in a Higgs realization can invalidate magnetic-energy bounds that are extremely strong for Proca electromagnetism. ([Adelberger, Dvali and Gruzinov](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0306245); [photon-mass review](https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1003)) The new paper’s advance is a systematic three-parameter phase diagram, including symmetry restoration, vortex formation, plasma currents and Galactic dynamo evolution. It therefore clarifies which existing bounds apply in which physical environment and proposes searches targeted specifically at the remaining regions.\n\nThe \\(0.3\\)-MeV statement is not a generic relaxation of the photon-mass limit: it requires an extraordinarily light millicharged Higgs, extremely small couplings and a very flat potential. The paper excludes the most severe order-by-order tunings but still occupies an exotic theoretical regime. No established Proca bound is contradicted.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26897", "arxiv": "2609.26897", "title": "A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT", "authors": "Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A coherent search of Fermi-GBM data identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts and seven candidate source clusters, with Swift-BAT providing narrower localizations for three candidates.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26897", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav 2609.26897 A coherent search of Fermi-GBM data identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts and seven candidate source clusters, with Swift-BAT providing narrower localizations for three candidates. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Gamma rays X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 4. A coherent search for new Galactic magnetars using Fermi/GBM and follow-up Swift/BAT\n\n**Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav**  \n[arXiv:2609.26897](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26897)\n\nA coherent Poisson matched-filter search is applied to continuous Fermi/GBM data from 2013–2025. Archival Swift/BAT imaging and spatiotemporal clustering are then used to associate the broadly localized GBM events with individual sources.\n\nThe analysis identifies 1,720 magnetar-like bursts:\n\n- 1,170 are associated with 11 known magnetars;\n- 550 remain unassociated;\n- seven spatially distinct clusters are proposed as candidate new magnetars;\n- three candidates possess BAT localizations sufficiently narrow for targeted X-ray follow-up.\n\nThe associated sample extends below \\(10^{-8}\\ {\\rm erg\\,cm^{-2}}\\) in fluence.\n\n##### History\n\nThe recent independent 17-year GBM catalog associated 735 bursts over the overlapping observing span. ([Godwin et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02809)) The coherent search raises that number to 1,170 and combines GBM sensitivity with BAT’s arcminute localization, making the source-association methodology—not merely the longer exposure—the key improvement. The catalog and posterior samples are public.\n\nThe seven objects remain candidates. Four have localization regions too broad for efficient pointed observations, while even the BAT-localized candidates require persistent X-ray emission, pulsations or repeated localized activity for confirmation. The search completeness as a function of fluence is not established, so the low-fluence turnover cannot yet be used for an intrinsic luminosity-function measurement.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-27057", "arxiv": "2609.27057", "title": "Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole", "authors": "Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda", "tags": ["X rays", "Compact objects"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "XRISM spectroscopy and contemporaneous radio imaging constrain a truncated disk and compact jet base in the highly accreting radio galaxy 3C 120.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-27057", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda 2609.27057 XRISM spectroscopy and contemporaneous radio imaging constrain a truncated disk and compact jet base in the highly accreting radio galaxy 3C 120. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 X rays Compact objects #### ★★★ 5. Disk truncation triggers relativistic jet launching of a highly accreting supermassive black hole\n\n**Hirofumi Noda, Kazuhiro Hada, Hiroto Maruyama, Taishu Kayanoki, Kenji Toma, Taiki Kawamuro, Yoshiyuki Inoue, Tomohisa Kawashima, Soyeon Yeo, Ilje Cho, Keiichi Asada, Yasushi Fukazawa, Yoshihiro Ueda**  \n[arXiv:2609.27057](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27057)\n\nCoordinated XRISM/Resolve spectroscopy and GMVA, VLBA and EAVN imaging probe the accretion flow and jet of the broad-line radio galaxy 3C 120, which accretes at roughly \\(0.1\\!-\\!0.2\\) of Eddington.\n\nThe resolved relativistic Fe-\\(K\\alpha\\) profile gives\n\n\\[\nR_{\\rm in}=17.4^{+6.3}_{-6.5}\\,R_g,\n\\]\n\nindicating a truncated cold disk with a hot, geometrically thick inner flow. Extrapolation of the VLBI collimation profile places the jet base within approximately \\(10R_g\\). The resulting geometry can advect large-scale magnetic flux and support Blandford–Znajek jet production despite the high accretion rate.\n\n##### History\n\nLong-term monitoring previously showed that X-ray dips in 3C 120 precede the ejection of superluminal radio knots, establishing a phenomenological disk–jet connection. ([Chatterjee et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.2051)) CCD spectroscopy had also suggested disk truncation, but multiple reflection, absorption and jet-continuum models remained comparably acceptable. ([Lohfink et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.4937)) XRISM separates the broad disk line from narrower distant components, providing the most robust truncation-radius measurement for this source, while contemporaneous VLBI supplies an independent jet-scale constraint.\n\nThe observations establish compatible geometry, not direct causality: the VLBI size near the horizon is an extrapolation, and the data do not prove that the inner flow is magnetically arrested or that the jet is powered uniquely by Blandford–Znajek extraction.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-28460", "arxiv": "2609.28460", "title": "First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event", "authors": "Erica Hammerstein et al.", "tags": ["Compact objects"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The first pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event reveals an early fast outflow and a continuum whose far-UV contribution is missed by optical blackbody fits.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-28460", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event Erica Hammerstein et al. 2609.28460 The first pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event reveals an early fast outflow and a continuum whose far-UV contribution is missed by optical blackbody fits. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Compact objects #### ★★☆ 6. First pre-peak ultraviolet spectrum of a tidal disruption event\n\n**Erica Hammerstein et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.28460](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28460)\n\nHST/STIS obtained a UV spectrum of TDE 2025aarm approximately twenty days before its optical maximum—the first such pre-peak UV spectrum of a tidal disruption event.\n\nBroad far- and near-UV absorption features are blueshifted by approximately\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm out}\\simeq10^4~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\ndemonstrating that a fast outflow was already present well before peak light. The far-UV-to-infrared continuum is substantially bluer than a single blackbody. Fitting only near-UV and optical photometry would underestimate the inferred bolometric luminosity by roughly a factor of nine for this event.\n\n##### History\n\nBroad UV absorption and comparably fast outflows were already observed after or near maximum in iPTF15af and AT 2019qiz. ([iPTF15af](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07446), [AT 2019qiz](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.01593)) The new result is the pre-maximum timing, which places strong outflow formation during the early circularization or disk-assembly phase. The continuum also provides direct evidence that optical blackbody fits can miss considerable far-UV/EUV power.\n\nThe factor-of-nine correction is object-specific and depends on extrapolating across the unobserved EUV; it should not yet be applied to the optical TDE population. Only a sample of prompt UV spectra can determine whether this is typical.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26878", "arxiv": "2609.26878", "title": "Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments", "authors": "Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti", "tags": ["Compact objects", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cluster simulations jointly track compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions, spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse, linking their cosmic rates and observable populations.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-26878", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti 2609.26878 Cluster simulations jointly track compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions, spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse, linking their cosmic rates and observable populations. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Compact objects Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Compact-object mergers and micro-tidal disruption events in dense stellar environments\n\n**Konstantinos Kritos, Francesco Iacovelli, Rosalba Perna, Emanuele Berti**  \n[arXiv:2609.26878](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26878)\n\nUsing the rapid cluster code Rapster, the authors simulate \\(10^5\\) clusters for each of ten physical variations and jointly follow compact-object mergers, stellar disruptions by neutron stars or stellar-mass black holes, compact-object spin evolution and accretion-induced collapse.\n\nThe black-hole merger rate peaks at \\(z\\simeq2\\!-\\!3\\) at several tens of \\({\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). The integrated number of micro-TDEs exceeds the merger number by a factor of approximately \\(2\\!-\\!5\\). About 88–90% of disruptions in these simulations arise from single compact-object–star encounters. Accretion can spin up low-mass black holes and push neutron stars above their maximum mass, producing black holes in the \\(2.2\\!-\\!5\\,M_\\odot\\) lower mass gap.\n\n##### History\n\nThe original analytical estimate predicted a local globular-cluster micro-TDE rate of a few \\({\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). ([Perets et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07698)) Later direct cluster simulations obtained a similar local black-hole–star disruption rate near \\(3\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\). ([Kremer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06353)) The present globular-cluster value is about \\(0.04\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}}\\), roughly 25 times below the original analytical estimate, principally because mass segregation strongly suppresses neutron-star encounters. Its new contribution is a unified cosmic calculation linking disruption rates, merger observables, compact-object spins and accretion-induced collapse.\n\nThe channel fractions are not robust population predictions: primordial binaries and triples are omitted, even though other simulations find them responsible for most micro-TDEs. The authors correctly describe their disruption rates as conservative lower limits.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-27496", "arxiv": "2609.27496", "title": "Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos", "authors": "Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Cosmic rays", "X rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Geminga’s halo cutoff is modeled with either a maximum electron injection energy or energy-dependent diffusion that carries emission beyond the observation aperture.", "path": "2026/09/24/index.html#paper-2609-27496", "reportDate": "24 September 2026", "searchText": "Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang 2609.27496 Geminga’s halo cutoff is modeled with either a maximum electron injection energy or energy-dependent diffusion that carries emission beyond the observation aperture. 2026-09-24 24 September 2026 Gamma rays Cosmic rays X rays #### ★★☆ 8. Origin of the high-energy spectral cutoff in gamma-ray pulsar halos\n\n**Qi-Ling Chen, Ming-Ming Kang, Kun Fang, Hong-Bo Hu, Yi-Qing Guo, Chao-Wen Yang**  \n[arXiv:2609.27496](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27496)\n\nThe tens-of-TeV cutoff in Geminga’s gamma-ray halo is modeled under two physically different hypotheses:\n\n1. the PWN termination shock reaches a finite maximum electron energy;\n2. the diffusion coefficient rises sharply above a characteristic energy, allowing high-energy electrons and their inverse-Compton emission to escape beyond the HAWC analysis aperture.\n\nBoth scenarios reproduce the spectrum and radial profiles. They predict different correlations between cutoff energy and pulsar properties and different hard-X-ray cutoffs for the central PWN.\n\n##### History\n\nHAWC’s precision Geminga and Monogem analysis established the cutoff and strongly suppressed local diffusion but did not determine whether the spectral feature originates at injection or during propagation. ([HAWC measurement](https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ad738e)) The important point here is that the measured cutoff need not directly measure the PWN accelerator limit: energy-dependent aperture leakage can generate a similar spectrum. This is a useful reinterpretation rather than a new observational detection.\n\nThe broken diffusion coefficient is phenomenological and is not derived from a self-consistent turbulence calculation. Wide-field energy-resolved morphology and PWN hard-X-ray measurements are needed to discriminate the models."}, {"id": "paper-2609-25681", "arxiv": "2609.25681", "title": "Search for the ¹⁶O(ppp) → ¹³C π⁺π⁺e⁺ decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques", "authors": "Super-Kamiokande Collaboration", "tags": ["BSM", "Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Super-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating three-proton decay using a convolutional neural network and sets a 90%-confidence lifetime limit of 4.2 × 10³² years.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25681", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for the ¹⁶O(ppp) → ¹³C π⁺π⁺e⁺ decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques Super-Kamiokande Collaboration 2609.25681 Super-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating three-proton decay using a convolutional neural network and sets a 90%-confidence lifetime limit of 4.2 × 10³² years. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. Search for the \\(^{16}\\mathrm O(ppp)\\rightarrow{}^{13}\\mathrm C\\,\\pi^+\\pi^+e^+\\) decay mode in Super-Kamiokande using machine learning techniques\n\n**Super-Kamiokande Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.25681](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25681)\n\nSuper-Kamiokande searches for a baryon-number-violating \\(\\Delta B=3\\) decay of three bound protons. An attention-based convolutional neural network classifies the complex multi-ring topology—the first CNN-based nucleon-decay analysis by Super-K.\n\nIn \\(0.401\\) megaton-years of SK-I–V data, one event survives, consistent with an expected background of 1.33 events. The resulting 90%-confidence lower limit is\n\n\\[\n\\tau/ {\\rm Br}>4.2\\times10^{32}\\ {\\rm yr}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious direct searches by GERDA and the Majorana Demonstrator obtained limits of approximately \\(4.7\\times10^{25}\\) and \\(1.6\\times10^{26}\\) years, respectively. The new result therefore improves the direct bound by more than six orders of magnitude and, for the first time, reaches the \\(\\sim10^{33}\\)-year range motivated by some anomaly-free \\(Z_6\\) baryon-parity models. This is a new sensitivity regime rather than an incremental exposure update. Its interpretation remains specific to this nuclear final state; efficiency and background estimates depend on intranuclear pion transport, nuclear de-excitation, and atmospheric-neutrino simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25990", "arxiv": "2609.25990", "title": "Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421", "authors": "Nanda Rea et al.", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Coherent pulsations, bursts and broadband follow-up identify the Einstein Probe transient EP J223759.5+531421 as a new Galactic magnetar.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25990", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421 Nanda Rea et al. 2609.25990 Coherent pulsations, bursts and broadband follow-up identify the Einstein Probe transient EP J223759.5+531421 as a new Galactic magnetar. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★★ 2. Einstein Probe discovery of the magnetar EP J223759.5+531421\n\n**Nanda Rea et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.25990](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25990)\n\nEinstein Probe discovered a new Galactic X-ray transient on 28 June 2026. Follow-up by XMM-Newton, NuSTAR, SVOM and IXPE revealed short magnetar-like bursts and coherent pulsations with\n\n\\[\nP\\simeq6\\ {\\rm s},\\qquad\n\\dot P\\simeq2.8\\times10^{-12}\\ {\\rm s\\,s^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\nUnder vacuum dipole braking, these imply \\(B_{\\rm dip}\\simeq2.6\\times10^{14}\\) G and a characteristic age near 34 kyr. Its \\(0.5-30\\) keV luminosity declined from roughly \\(1.2\\times10^{35}\\) to \\(5.7\\times10^{34}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}}\\) during the first month, assuming a distance of 3.3 kpc. No radio or near-infrared counterpart was found.\n\n##### History\n\nThe period, period derivative, bursts, hard X-ray tail and thermal spectrum together elevate EP J223759.5+531421 from a candidate to a securely identified magnetar. It adds a new object to the population summarized in the [McGill magnetar catalogue](https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.4167), with unusually comprehensive observations immediately after discovery. Its position about 0.26 kpc below the Galactic plane is difficult to reconcile with the nominal characteristic age if it was born close to the midplane. That tension is suggestive but not yet anomalous physics: the adopted distance is uncertain, and magnetar torque variability makes \\(\\tau_c=P/(2\\dot P)\\) an unreliable chronological age.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25958", "arxiv": "2609.25958", "title": "IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421", "authors": "Roberto Taverna et al.", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IXPE measurements resolve strong phase- and energy-dependent polarization in EP J223759.5+531421, constraining its viewing geometry and surface-emission models.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25958", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421 Roberto Taverna et al. 2609.25958 IXPE measurements resolve strong phase- and energy-dependent polarization in EP J223759.5+531421, constraining its viewing geometry and surface-emission models. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★★ 3. IXPE observation of the new magnetar source EP J223759.5+531421\n\n**Roberto Taverna et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.25958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25958)\n\nIXPE began observing the newly discovered magnetar approximately eight days after its outburst. The phase-integrated polarization degree is about 7%, detected above \\(4\\sigma\\), but varies strongly with rotational phase and energy. It reaches approximately 63% in the \\(4-7.5\\) keV band near the secondary pulse.\n\nThe polarization angle swings through almost \\(180^\\circ\\). A rotating-vector fit gives approximate angles of \\(28^\\circ\\) between the line of sight and spin axis and \\(109^\\circ\\) between the magnetic and spin axes. The authors interpret the cooler component as emission from condensed surface regions and the hotter component as radiation processed through a magnetized atmosphere.\n\n##### History\n\nIXPE previously found much stronger energy-integrated polarization in 1RXS J170849.0−400910, rising from roughly 20% at low energies to about 80% above 6 keV ([previous IXPE result](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.12919)). The new observation extends this program to a magnetar caught immediately after discovery and provides unusually rapid phase-resolved constraints on its viewing geometry. The proposed mixture of condensed-surface and atmospheric emission confirms and refines interpretations already developed for 1RXS J170849 and 4U 0142+61, rather than introducing an entirely new emission mechanism. The 63% value occurs in a restricted phase-energy bin with limited counts, and the geometric inference depends on an idealized dipolar rotating-vector model.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25950", "arxiv": "2609.25950", "title": "Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations", "authors": "Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Inhomogeneous reheating from domain walls and first-order phase transitions is mapped into deuterium constraints on early-Universe explanations of pulsar-timing-array signals.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25950", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller 2609.25950 Inhomogeneous reheating from domain walls and first-order phase transitions is mapped into deuterium constraints on early-Universe explanations of pulsar-timing-array signals. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 4. Big Bang nucleosynthesis confronts domain walls and first-order phase transitions as pulsar-timing-array explanations\n\n**Fatemeh Elahi, Diego Fortini, Yann Gouttenoire, Nicholas Leister, Pedro Schwaller**  \n[arXiv:2609.25950](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25950)\n\nDomain-wall annihilation or a first-order phase transition can reheat the visible plasma inhomogeneously, producing baryon-to-photon fluctuations that survive until nucleosynthesis. Because deuterium production responds nonlinearly to these fluctuations, the spatially averaged abundance differs from homogeneous-BBN predictions.\n\nUsing PRIMAT nuclear rates, fluctuations with RMS amplitude\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\langle S_B^2\\rangle}\\simeq0.12-0.29\n\\]\n\ncan reduce the existing mild deuterium discrepancy, whereas larger amplitudes overproduce deuterium. PArthENoPE already agrees with the observed abundance without inhomogeneities and instead gives an approximate upper limit of 0.19. For fully visible reheating, the authors infer \\(\\alpha_{\\rm DW}\\lesssim0.25\\) for domain walls and exclude particularly strong, slow transitions in parts of pulsar-timing-array parameter space.\n\n##### History\n\nThe [NANOGrav 15-year analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16213) established a common-spectrum signal compatible with a stochastic gravitational-wave background, prompting many phase-transition and domain-wall interpretations. Earlier inhomogeneous-BBN studies established that deuterium is sensitive to baryon fluctuations, but this paper explicitly maps fluctuations from those two gravitational-wave sources into BBN and overlays the result on PTA posteriors. The constraint may be the strongest current BBN test of these particular early-Universe PTA explanations. Its main limitation is unusually consequential: switching between PRIMAT and PArthENoPE changes a nominally preferred fluctuation interval into only an upper bound. Visible branching fraction, diffusion, source morphology and thermalization assumptions also enter directly.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25217", "arxiv": "2609.25217", "title": "Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons", "authors": "Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections can obstruct a full two-Z density-matrix interpretation of Higgs four-lepton decays; a reduced angular construction remains well behaved.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25217", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli 2609.25217 Off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections can obstruct a full two-Z density-matrix interpretation of Higgs four-lepton decays; a reduced angular construction remains well behaved. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 5. Limitations of quantum tomography in Higgs-boson decays to four leptons\n\n**Morgan Del Gratta, Fabio Maltoni, Davide Pagani, Giovanni Pelliccioli**  \n[arXiv:2609.25217](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25217)\n\nThe paper examines whether the angular distribution in\n\n\\[\nH\\rightarrow ZZ^\\ast\\rightarrow4\\ell\n\\]\n\ncan consistently be interpreted as quantum-state tomography of two spin-1 particles beyond leading order.\n\nAt NLO, the conventionally reconstructed two-qutrit density matrix can acquire negative eigenvalues even after radiatively corrected decay analyzers are used. The failure originates from coherent \\(Z^\\ast\\) and \\(\\gamma^\\ast\\) contributions to the off-shell dilepton system, which do not share a universal production-times-decay factorization. A neutrino control channel restores positivity, while an SMEFT \\(HZ\\gamma\\) contribution can generate the same problem already at tree level. A reduced rank-two angular sector remains well behaved and can still supply a lower bound on concurrence.\n\n##### History\n\nThis is an important qualification to the recent ATLAS and CMS evidence for \\(ZZ^\\ast\\) entanglement ([CERN summary](https://home.cern/strong-evidence-for-quantum-entanglement-between-z-bosons-found-by-atlas-and-cms/), [ATLAS result](https://doi.org/10.1103/y1nh-1b82)). It does **not** show that the measured angular correlations or the entanglement conclusion are false. Instead, it demonstrates that interpreting the complete four-lepton distribution as a physical two-\\(Z\\) density matrix becomes theoretically ambiguous once off-shell photon interference and radiative corrections matter. The robust rank-two construction is therefore the paper’s practical advance. Detector acceptance and unfolding may introduce additional complications beyond the parton-level issue studied here.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25672", "arxiv": "2609.25672", "title": "A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample", "authors": "Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint probability statistic combines Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information to rank high-energy transient candidates across their overlapping 2013–2025 observations.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25672", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav 2609.25672 A joint probability statistic combines Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information to rank high-energy transient candidates across their overlapping 2013–2025 observations. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 6. A multi-mission detection framework for high-energy transients: application to a 13-year Fermi/GBM and Swift/BAT sample\n\n**Ariel Perera, Barak Zackay, Tejaswi Venumadhav**  \n[arXiv:2609.25672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25672)\n\nThe authors construct a joint probability statistic from Fermi-GBM and Swift-BAT trigger information and apply it to their overlapping 2013–2025 data. Instead of using one instrument merely to validate candidates from the other, the foreground and background distributions are modeled jointly.\n\nDepending on the assumed transient class and favorable spacecraft geometry, the accessible volume can increase by as much as 60% for short-GRB-like events. Candidates with high joint probability have an estimated contamination fraction near 4%.\n\n##### History\n\nPrevious GBM targeted searches and BAT/GBM coincidence studies recovered subthreshold events by conditioning on an external trigger—famously important for weak counterparts such as [GRB 170817A](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05833). The present work advances this into a statistically calibrated multi-instrument ranking rather than a post-hoc confirmation test. The quoted 60% volume gain is an optimistic upper envelope: the current implementation is still seeded by a GBM trigger threshold, and it does not fully marginalize over realistic field-of-view, orientation, energy-response and duty-cycle effects. A fully symmetric low-level search would be the decisive next step.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26574", "arxiv": "2609.26574", "title": "Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421", "authors": "Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak", "tags": ["X rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Polarized AM3 transport models reproduce the broad energy-dependent polarization of Mrk 421 with one helical emission region and pitch-angle-dependent cooling.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-26574", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421 Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak 2609.26574 Polarized AM3 transport models reproduce the broad energy-dependent polarization of Mrk 421 with one helical emission region and pitch-angle-dependent cooling. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 X rays #### ★★☆ 7. Self-consistent modeling of energy-dependent synchrotron polarization in blazars with application to Mrk 421\n\n**Frederike Apel, Motseothata Tisang, Victor Barbosa Martins, Xavier Rodrigues, Markus Böttcher, Anna Franckowiak**  \n[arXiv:2609.26574](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26574)\n\nThe AM3 transport framework is extended to follow synchrotron polarization, pitch-angle-dependent cooling and multiple magnetic-field structures self-consistently. A single-zone helical-field model reproduces the broad polarization behavior of Mrk 421 during May 2022: approximately 15% X-ray polarization versus about 3% in the optical and infrared.\n\nIn the shock-in-jet calculation, the X-ray polarization angle rotates by roughly \\(70^\\circ\\) over five hours while the optical angle stays nearly fixed. The result demonstrates that chromatic polarization need not imply spatially separated emission zones.\n\n##### History\n\nThe contemporaneous [MAGIC/IXPE campaign](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10732) interpreted the X-ray polarization exceeding the optical and radio values by factors of two to five as evidence for an energy-stratified region near a shock. This paper supplies an explicit counterexample: pitch-angle-dependent cooling in one co-spatial helical region can produce similar stratification. Time-dependent helical-field models existed previously, but the self-consistent polarized transport is a real methodological improvement. The fit is not unique, modest polarization residuals remain, and the magnetic geometry is prescribed rather than generated dynamically; the data therefore do not yet distinguish this scenario from multi-zone models.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-25202", "arxiv": "2609.25202", "title": "Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis", "authors": "Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A rotating axion-like field is proposed to connect baryogenesis with tentative CMB birefringence, predicting a positive rotation angle and a nonstandard pre-BBN expansion history.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-25202", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee 2609.25202 A rotating axion-like field is proposed to connect baryogenesis with tentative CMB birefringence, predicting a positive rotation angle and a nonstandard pre-BBN expansion history. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 BSM #### ★★☆ 8. Cosmic birefringence and axiogenesis\n\n**Raymond T. Co, Lawrence J. Hall, Keisuke Harigaya, Taegyu Lee**  \n[arXiv:2609.25202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25202)\n\nA rotating axion-like field is proposed as a common origin for the baryon asymmetry through axiogenesis and the tentative isotropic CMB-polarization rotation near \\(0.3^\\circ\\).\n\nThe minimal construction predicts a positive birefringence angle and an early matter-dominated era followed by kination, ending at temperatures of approximately \\(3-50\\) MeV. Producing the required conserved axion charge is difficult in a single-field realization, so the authors construct a multi-field clockwork model that exponentially enhances the angular momentum.\n\n##### History\n\nPlanck analyses previously reported a possible isotropic rotation of \\(\\beta=0.35\\pm0.14^\\circ\\) ([Minami and Komatsu](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.11254)), while [axiogenesis](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.02080) was developed independently as a baryogenesis mechanism driven by axion rotation. This paper’s new contribution is a single dynamical origin for both effects, with a sign prediction and a constrained pre-BBN expansion history. It should not yet be read as evidence for an axion: the birefringence measurement remains sensitive to polarization calibration and Galactic foregrounds, while the necessary charge requires an elaborate multi-field sector. The late kination phase also lies close enough to BBN to require careful thermal-history tests.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-26611", "arxiv": "2609.26611", "title": "Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays", "authors": "Mikhail Sekretov", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A speculative two-step mechanism transfers ultrahigh-energy cosmic-ray energy to dark matter for long-distance propagation, followed by reconversion into a baryon near the Milky Way.", "path": "2026/09/23/index.html#paper-2609-26611", "reportDate": "23 September 2026", "searchText": "Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays Mikhail Sekretov 2609.26611 A speculative two-step mechanism transfers ultrahigh-energy cosmic-ray energy to dark matter for long-distance propagation, followed by reconversion into a baryon near the Milky Way. 2026-09-23 23 September 2026 Cosmic rays Dark matter BSM #### ★☆☆ 9. Dark-matter relay for ultra-high-energy cosmic rays\n\n**Mikhail Sekretov**  \n[arXiv:2609.26611](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26611)\n\nThe paper proposes that an ultrahigh-energy nucleon near an active galaxy transfers energy to a light neutral dark-matter particle. The boosted particle then crosses cosmological distances without GZK attenuation and scatters back into a baryon near the Milky Way.\n\nFor a benchmark \\(m_\\chi\\sim25\\) MeV dark-photon model, the required high-energy \\(\\chi N\\) cross section is of order \\(10^{-25}\\ {\\rm cm^2}\\). The probability of reconversion is estimated at roughly \\(5\\times10^{-5}\\) through the local Galactic disk and potentially \\(10^{-2}\\) along dark-matter-rich Galactic-center directions.\n\n##### History\n\nCosmic-ray-accelerated and boosted dark matter have been studied previously, as have dark-matter interactions that cool AGN cosmic rays ([related recent study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11906)). The two-step “relay” mechanism appears to be new. It remains a feasibility argument rather than a predictive UHECR model: there is no end-to-end spectrum, composition or event-rate calculation, and the motivating BL Lac correlations are themselves statistically controversial. The large deep-inelastic cross section must also survive accelerator, cosmological and boosted-dark-matter constraints, while secondary gamma rays and neutrinos may provide stronger limits."}, {"id": "paper-2609-22638", "arxiv": "2609.22638", "title": "New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch", "authors": "Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Self-consistent MESA stellar evolution and red-giant-branch measurements from 27 globular clusters constrain electron-coupled and Higgs-mixed light scalars.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22638", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent 2609.22638 Self-consistent MESA stellar evolution and red-giant-branch measurements from 27 globular clusters constrain electron-coupled and Higgs-mixed light scalars. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 1. New bounds on light scalars from the tip of the red giant branch\n\n**Natnael Debru, Audrey Fung, Saniya Heeba, Hugo Schérer, Katelin Schutz, Aaron C. Vincent**  \n[arXiv:2609.22638](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22638)\n\nLight scalars coupled to electrons can be resonantly produced through longitudinal-plasmon conversion in degenerate red-giant cores. The resulting energy loss delays helium ignition and brightens the tip of the red giant branch.\n\nRather than evaluating the exotic emissivity at fixed benchmark core conditions, the authors incorporate it into MESA and evolve the stellar structure self-consistently. Comparing with the bolometric TRGB magnitudes of 27 Milky Way globular clusters gives\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{\\phi ee}\\equiv\\frac{g_e^2}{4\\pi}\n   <1.6\\times10^{-34}\n\\]\n\nfor sub-keV scalar masses. For a Higgs-mixed scalar this becomes\n\n\\[\n\\sin\\theta<2.2\\times10^{-11},\n\\]\n\nthe strongest reported bound over approximately \\(1\\,{\\rm eV}-10\\,{\\rm keV}\\).\n\n##### History\n\n[Hardy and Lasenby](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.05852) established that plasma mixing makes resonant longitudinal-plasmon conversion dominate low-mass scalar emission, but their red-giant estimate used representative core conditions rather than a full evolutionary calculation. Subsequent work concentrated largely on revised bremsstrahlung rates, including [Balaji et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01669) and [Yamamoto and Yoshioka](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.03123). The present calculation combines the resonant rate with stellar backreaction and a multi-cluster likelihood, strengthening the previous TRGB scalar bound by nearly two orders of magnitude. The remaining caveat is the dependence on stellar-evolution inputs and globular-cluster calibration, although the paper explicitly varies helium abundance, metallicity, mass loss and mixing length.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22480", "arxiv": "2609.22480", "title": "Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks", "authors": "Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Kinetic calculations and hybrid-PIC simulations show that resonant streaming can dominate magnetic amplification driven by maximum-energy cosmic rays upstream of shocks.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22480", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli 2609.22480 Kinetic calculations and hybrid-PIC simulations show that resonant streaming can dominate magnetic amplification driven by maximum-energy cosmic rays upstream of shocks. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. Importance of the Resonant Cosmic-Ray Streaming Instability Upstream of Collisionless Shocks\n\n**Bricker Ostler, Benedikt Schroer, Damiano Caprioli**  \n[arXiv:2609.22480](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22480)\n\nEscaping maximum-energy cosmic rays form a relativistic, forward-beamed population upstream of collisionless shocks. Solving the kinetic dispersion relation for cold and finite-width beams, the authors find that the resonant streaming mode can grow faster than the usually assumed non-resonant Bell mode.\n\nAt sufficiently slow shocks or large pitch-angle spread, the non-resonant mode can even become stable while the resonant mode survives. Relativistic hybrid-PIC simulations reproduce the linear result and yield nonlinear saturation around\n\n\\[\n\\delta B/B_0\\sim1.\n\\]\n\nBecause the growing wavelength is resonant with the escaping particles themselves, it directly supplies fluctuations capable of confining the highest-energy cosmic rays.\n\n##### History\n\nSince [Bell’s 2004 analysis](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2966.2004.08097.x), upstream magnetic amplification has generally been organized around the fast non-resonant current-driven mode. Later kinetic and hybrid studies showed simultaneous resonant and non-resonant activity, for example [Haggerty, Caprioli and Zweibel](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06346), but did not establish the resonant mode as the dominant instability specifically for the narrow, maximum-energy escaping population. This paper changes that ordering and shows that neglecting resonance can underestimate amplification at precisely the gyroradius required for confinement. Its nonlinear simulations treat an idealized homogeneous beam rather than a self-consistent shock precursor, so the impact on maximum SNR energy still needs to be demonstrated in shock simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22577", "arxiv": "2609.22577", "title": "Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube", "authors": "S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Neutrinos", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "HAWC separates point-like and diffuse Galactic-center emission; an IceCube search with the corresponding hadronic template finds no neutrino excess.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22577", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations 2609.22577 HAWC separates point-like and diffuse Galactic-center emission; an IceCube search with the corresponding hadronic template finds no neutrino excess. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Gamma rays Neutrinos Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. Resolving the Galactic Center Region’s Gamma-Ray Emission and Searching for its Neutrino Counterpart with HAWC and IceCube\n\n**S. Yun-Cárcamo, R. Babu, for the HAWC and IceCube collaborations**  \n[arXiv:2609.22577](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22577)\n\nUsing approximately 2860 days of HAWC data and improved event reconstruction, the analysis resolves the Galactic-center emission into a point-like component, HAWC J1745−2857, and Galactic diffuse emission. The point-source spectrum remains an unbroken power law beyond \\(100\\) TeV.\n\nThe diffuse contribution is approximately 35% of the point-source flux at the Galactic center. The resulting gamma-ray morphology is converted into a hadronic neutrino template and searched for using 12 years of IceCube muon-track data. No excess is found:\n\n\\[\np=0.49,\n\\]\n\nand the 90% upper limit is approximately twelve times the neutrino flux predicted from the nominal hadronic gamma-ray model.\n\n##### History\n\nHAWC’s earlier analysis detected \\(>100\\)-TeV Galactic-center emission but modeled it essentially as one point source. ([Previous HAWC result](https://doi.org/10.3847/2041-8213/ad772e)) The new analysis retains that hard, cutoff-free component while explicitly separating diffuse ridge emission. IceCube had already established diffuse Galactic-plane neutrinos at population level ([Science 380, adc9818](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adc9818)), but this is the first IceCube limit tied to HAWC’s two-component Galactic-center morphology. Numerically, however, the limit remains a factor of twelve above the transparent \\(pp\\) prediction: it neither confirms nor excludes the hadronic interpretation. The principal advance is therefore the spatially consistent multimessenger template, not a constraining neutrino result.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22581", "arxiv": "2609.22581", "title": "Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Lyα Forest", "authors": "Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "DESI Lyman-α forest information, combined with CMB and BAO data, constrains extra radiation and thermal sterile-neutrino relics with nonstandard temperatures.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22581", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Lyα Forest Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino 2609.22581 DESI Lyman-α forest information, combined with CMB and BAO data, constrains extra radiation and thermal sterile-neutrino relics with nonstandard temperatures. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 4. Hunting Thermal Relics in the DESI DR1 Ly\\(\\alpha\\) Forest\n\n**Emanuelly Silva, Artur Ladeira, Rafael C. Nunes, Eleonora Di Valentino**  \n[arXiv:2609.22581](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22581)\n\nThe authors combine Planck 2018, DESI DR2 BAO and DESI DR1 one-dimensional Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) forest power-spectrum information to constrain extra radiation and thermal sterile neutrinos whose temperature may differ from that of active neutrinos.\n\nThey validate DESI’s two-parameter \\(P_{\\rm 1D}\\) compression for this nonstandard model, finding residual cosmological dependence below 0.15%. No extra radiation or sterile component is preferred. The combined limits are\n\n\\[\nN_{\\rm eff}<3.41,\n\\qquad\nm_s^{\\rm eff}<0.061~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\n\nat 95% credibility. Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) information improves the \\(m_s^{\\rm eff}\\) limit by roughly a factor of seven relative to CMB alone.\n\n##### History\n\nThe DESI DR1 Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) cosmology analysis introduced the compressed small-scale-power likelihood but tested mostly standard cosmological extensions ([Chaves-Montero et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21432)). Different-temperature sterile relics had recently been constrained with CMB and large-scale structure without this Lyman-\\(\\alpha\\) information ([Ladeira et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21518)). The new contribution is both the validation of the compression for a colder sterile distribution and the resulting \\(0.061\\)-eV effective-mass bound. \\(m_s^{\\rm eff}\\) is an abundance-weighted cosmological parameter, not the physical sterile mass; a colder, dilute population can still contain substantially heavier particles. The limit also inherits the astrophysical and hydrodynamical assumptions used to compress the forest likelihood.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-22806", "arxiv": "2609.22806", "title": "Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects", "authors": "Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A supernova’s delayed 511-keV gamma-ray echo could be compared with its directly measured neutrino fluence to probe emission anisotropy and propagation effects.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-22806", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto 2609.22806 A supernova’s delayed 511-keV gamma-ray echo could be compared with its directly measured neutrino fluence to probe emission anisotropy and propagation effects. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 5. Gamma-Ray Echo as a Probe of Supernova Neutrino Emission Anisotropy and Long-Baseline Effects\n\n**Garv Chauhan, Cecilia Lunardini, Yago Porto**  \n[arXiv:2609.22806](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22806)\n\nElectron antineutrinos from a core-collapse supernova undergo inverse beta decay in the progenitor’s outer hydrogen envelope. The resulting positrons thermalize and produce a delayed 511-keV annihilation signal—the gamma-ray echo.\n\nThe authors compare the \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\) fluence inferred from this echo with the fluence measured directly at Earth. Because the envelope and Earth view the source along different effective directions and baselines, discrepancies can probe intrinsic neutrino-emission anisotropy or propagation effects such as invisible neutrino decay. For a nearby supernova, tens-of-percent effects could be tested with a 511-keV effective area of order\n\n\\[\nA_{\\rm eff}\\sim10^4~{\\rm cm^2}.\n\\]\n\n##### History\n\n[Lunardini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.13197) introduced the modern quantitative gamma-ray-echo calculation for an approximately isotropic neutrino burst. The new paper generalizes it to dipolar and quadrupolar emission and, more originally, formulates the stellar envelope and terrestrial detector as an astrophysical near–far experiment. There is no previous quantitative sensitivity study of this particular echo/direct-neutrino ratio. The limitation is instrumental: the required narrow-band MeV effective area exceeds that of current instruments, and intrinsic anisotropy is partly degenerate with exotic propagation. The latter would require time, energy or directional structure to disentangle.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24950", "arxiv": "2609.24950", "title": "Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An ATLAS multilepton search combining Run-2 and Run-3 data finds no significant excess and constrains a heavy vector resonance that couples preferentially to top quarks.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24950", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks ATLAS Collaboration 2609.24950 An ATLAS multilepton search combining Run-2 and Run-3 data finds no significant excess and constrains a heavy vector resonance that couples preferentially to top quarks. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 6. Search for a top-philic heavy resonance in association with top quarks\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.24950](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24950)\n\nATLAS searches for a color-singlet vector \\(Z'\\) that couples preferentially to top quarks, using the complete \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 sample plus \\(56~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) from Run 3.\n\nEvents with two same-sign leptons or at least three leptons are classified using a mass-parameterized transformer network. No significant excess is found. For a purely left-handed benchmark with \\(c_t=1\\),\n\n\\[\nm_{Z'}<1.15~{\\rm TeV}\n\\]\n\nis excluded at 95% confidence, compared with an expected reach of \\(1.25\\) TeV. For \\(c_t=3\\), the observed exclusion reaches \\(2.0\\) TeV.\n\n##### History\n\nATLAS’s earlier full-Run-2 single-lepton search constrained cross sections but could not exclude any mass for the \\(c_t=1\\) benchmark. ([Previous ATLAS search](https://doi.org/10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-12318-9)) The new multilepton analysis improves the cross-section sensitivity by approximately a factor of fifteen; the Run-3 addition alone supplies about 30% more sensitivity at \\(1.5\\) TeV. This is therefore a substantial experimental improvement, not merely a luminosity update. The observed reach is modestly weaker than expected, and the quoted mass exclusions apply to a simplified resonance with specified coupling and chirality rather than generic top-compositeness models.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24085", "arxiv": "2609.24085", "title": "Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts", "authors": "Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint polarization analysis of three repeating fast radio bursts finds no axion-induced oscillation and constrains ultralight axion-like dark matter.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24085", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen 2609.24085 A joint polarization analysis of three repeating fast radio bursts finds no axion-induced oscillation and constrains ultralight axion-like dark matter. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 7. Constraints on Axion-like Dark Matter from Cosmic Birefringence with a Polarization Array of Repeating Fast Radio Bursts\n\n**Xiaohui Liu, Zi-Yan Yuwen, Yun-Long Zhang, Zerui Liu, Shiqian Zhao, Shuai Feng, Wei-Yang Wang, Xuelei Chen**  \n[arXiv:2609.24085](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24085)\n\nA coherently oscillating axion field produces periodic rotations of linear polarization. The authors analyze three highly active repeating FRBs and jointly treat their independent source terms and correlated Earth term in a hierarchical Bayesian model that includes intrinsic burst-to-burst polarization-angle scatter.\n\nNo oscillation is found. Across\n\n\\[\n10^{-23}\\lesssim m_a/{\\rm eV}\\lesssim10^{-18},\n\\]\n\nthe polarization-array limits range from roughly \\(10^{-12.6}\\) to \\(10^{-7.9}\\ {\\rm GeV^{-1}}\\).\n\n##### History\n\nPulsar-polarization arrays already exploit the same correlated Earth term ([Liu, Lou and Ren](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10615); [PPTA limits](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02229)), while a single-FRB analysis was performed by [Wang et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.00473). This is the first multi-source repeating-FRB polarization array. Combining the three sources improves the low-mass limit by factors of about \\(2-10\\) relative to the best individual FRB, primarily by combining different observing baselines. At present, however, auto-correlations dominate; the shared Earth term adds little, and the bounds remain weaker than several pulsar and other astrophysical constraints. With only three sources and a maximum baseline of about 472 days, this is a proof of concept.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24735", "arxiv": "2609.24735", "title": "Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence", "authors": "Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Particle-in-cell simulations trace acceleration in stratified magnetorotational turbulence through reconnection injection and successive first- and second-order Fermi stages.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24735", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra 2609.24735 Particle-in-cell simulations trace acceleration in stratified magnetorotational turbulence through reconnection injection and successive first- and second-order Fermi stages. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★☆ 8. Particle Acceleration in Stratified 2D MRI Turbulence\n\n**Astor Sandoval, Mario Riquelme, Jorge Cuadra**  \n[arXiv:2609.24735](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24735)\n\nParticle-in-cell simulations identify three stages of acceleration in collisionless magnetorotational turbulence:\n\n1. reconnection electric fields inject particles during plasmoid splitting;\n2. converging separating plasmoids produce rapid first-order-Fermi acceleration;\n3. repeated plasmoid encounters produce slower second-order-Fermi acceleration, with a subdominant shear contribution.\n\nThis explains why the maximum energy scales as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\max}\\propto\\frac{\\omega_{c,0}}{\\Omega_0},\n\\]\n\nwhile the late stochastic phase increases energies by only a factor of a few.\n\n##### History\n\nThe authors’ earlier stratified simulations discovered the \\(E_{\\max}\\propto\\omega_c/\\Omega\\) scaling without isolating its physical origin ([Sandoval et al. 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12348)). Other kinetic MRI simulations established persistent nonthermal acceleration in unstratified boxes ([Bacchini et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01399)). The new work explains the scaling through rapid plasmoid-driven first-order acceleration rather than ordinary slow stochastic energization. The main caveat is substantial: the simulations remain two-dimensional and use a pair plasma. Three-dimensional topology, electron–ion energy partition and radiative losses may change both injection and saturation.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-24887", "arxiv": "2609.24887", "title": "A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes", "authors": "Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Dark vector-meson resonances could imprint absorption features in ultrahigh-energy neutrino spectra through interactions with the cosmic neutrino background.", "path": "2026/09/22/index.html#paper-2609-24887", "reportDate": "22 September 2026", "searchText": "A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni 2609.24887 Dark vector-meson resonances could imprint absorption features in ultrahigh-energy neutrino spectra through interactions with the cosmic neutrino background. 2026-09-22 22 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 9. A New Probe of Strongly-Interacting Dark Sectors using Neutrino Telescopes\n\n**Jose A. Macias Cruz, Christopher V. Cappiello, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Suchita Kulkarni**  \n[arXiv:2609.24887](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24887)\n\nHigh-energy cosmic neutrinos can resonantly annihilate on the cosmic neutrino background through dark vector mesons. The resulting conversion into dark pions produces an absorption feature whose position reflects the dark-hadron spectrum.\n\nUnlike the Standard-Model \\(\\rho\\) resonance, the dark vector can possess a much larger branching ratio into neutrinos, allowing an observable dip without requiring a large local relic-neutrino overdensity. Forecasts indicate sensitivity with future detectors, particularly IceCube-Gen2 Radio.\n\n##### History\n\nResonant C\\(\\nu\\)B absorption is an old idea, originally involving the \\(Z\\) resonance, and has been extensively adapted to light secret-neutrino mediators ([Ioka and Murase](https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.2279); [Esteban et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13568)). Standard QCD-vector-meson absorption was also considered, but its tiny neutrino branching ratio makes detection impractical without a large relic-neutrino overdensity. The new element is to use the resonance tower of a confining dark sector as a spectroscopic observable. This remains a forecast: sensitivity depends on the uncertain ultrahigh-energy neutrino flux, the absolute neutrino mass, detector energy resolution and the assumed dark-neutrino portal.\n\nThe 22 September selection includes **no new UHECR composition or anisotropy measurement and no new precision laboratory-neutrino oscillation result**."}, {"id": "paper-2609-21921", "arxiv": "2609.21921", "title": "Observation of the doubly charmed baryon Ωcc⁺", "authors": "LHCb Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "LHCb observes the doubly charmed strange baryon Ωcc⁺ in upgraded-detector data, with a global significance of 8.7σ.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21921", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Observation of the doubly charmed baryon Ωcc⁺ LHCb Collaboration 2609.21921 LHCb observes the doubly charmed strange baryon Ωcc⁺ in upgraded-detector data, with a global significance of 8.7σ. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Particle physics #### ★★★ 1. Observation of the doubly charmed baryon \\(\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\)\n\n**LHCb Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.21921](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21921)\n\nLHCb reports the first observation of the doubly charmed strange baryon \\(\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\), reconstructed through\n\n\\[\n\\Omega_{cc}^{+}\\rightarrow\\Omega_c^0\\pi^+,\n\\qquad\n\\Omega_c^0\\rightarrow pK^-K^-\\pi^+ .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(6.3~\\mathrm{fb^{-1}}\\) of 13.6-TeV proton-proton data collected with the upgraded detector in 2024, the analysis finds a peaking structure with a global significance of \\(8.7\\sigma\\). Its measured mass is\n\n\\[\nm_{\\Omega_{cc}^{+}}\n =3725.9\\pm1.0_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.2_{\\rm syst}\n \\pm0.4_{\\rm lifetime}\\pm0.6_{\\rm external}\n \\ {\\rm MeV}/c^2.\n\\]\n\nIts weak decay establishes that the state is a ground-state doubly charmed baryon rather than a short-lived excited resonance.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20949", "arxiv": "2609.20949", "title": "First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE", "authors": "MicroBooNE Collaboration", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "MicroBooNE measures electron-neutrino–argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables, testing nuclear-interaction and intranuclear-transport models.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20949", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE MicroBooNE Collaboration 2609.20949 MicroBooNE measures electron-neutrino–argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables, testing nuclear-interaction and intranuclear-transport models. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 2. First measurement of differential cross sections in kinematic-imbalance variables with electron-neutrino interactions using MicroBooNE\n\n**MicroBooNE Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.20949](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20949)\n\nMicroBooNE measures flux-integrated \\(\\nu_e\\)-argon cross sections in transverse kinematic-imbalance variables using its complete Booster Neutrino Beam exposure of approximately\n\n\\[\n1.3\\times10^{21}\\ {\\rm protons\\ on\\ target}.\n\\]\n\nThese observables separate simple quasielastic scattering from Fermi motion, multinucleon interactions and final-state rescattering more effectively than inclusive lepton kinematics. Comparisons with commonly used neutrino-event generators reveal discrepancies in parts of phase space expected to be relatively insensitive to final-state interactions, pointing to deficiencies in the primary nuclear-interaction model as well as in intranuclear transport.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20944", "arxiv": "2609.20944", "title": "Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies", "authors": "Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A study of dwarf-galaxy J-factor systematics quantifies how additional uncertainties and spatial extension can weaken stacked gamma-ray annihilation limits.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20944", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul 2609.20944 A study of dwarf-galaxy J-factor systematics quantifies how additional uncertainties and spatial extension can weaken stacked gamma-ray annihilation limits. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter Gamma rays #### ★★★ 3. Systematic uncertainties and their impact on gamma-ray searches for dark matter in dwarf galaxies\n\n**Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper, Arifa Khatee Zathul**  \n[arXiv:2609.20944](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20944)\n\nThe paper examines whether quoted dwarf-spheroidal \\(J\\)-factor uncertainties adequately describe uncertainties from stellar membership, unresolved binaries, equilibrium and spherical-symmetry assumptions, and the adopted stellar and dark-matter density profiles.\n\nThe spread among published determinations is substantially larger than many nominal statistical error bars. The authors argue that an additional uncertainty of at least\n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\log_{10}J\\sim0.5\n\\]\n\nis plausible. In simulated stacked Fermi-LAT analyses of 27 dwarfs, adding systematic uncertainties of \\(0.5\\) and \\(1.0\\) dex weakens median annihilation limits by factors of approximately \\(1.4\\) and \\(3.2\\), respectively. Treating spatially extended halos as point sources can further overstate the sensitivity.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21118", "arxiv": "2609.21118", "title": "Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection", "authors": "Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi", "tags": ["Multimessenger", "Gamma rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Analytic particle-orbit arguments and particle-in-cell simulations determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21118", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi 2609.21118 Analytic particle-orbit arguments and particle-in-cell simulations determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Multimessenger Gamma rays Neutrinos #### ★★★ 4. Energy partition in relativistic electron–positron–ion reconnection\n\n**Nilay Mancini, Luca Comisso, Lorenzo Sironi**  \n[arXiv:2609.21118](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21118)\n\nThe authors combine analytic particle-orbit arguments with particle-in-cell simulations to determine how magnetic-reconnection energy is divided between leptons and ions in mixed pair-ion plasmas.\n\nThey find that the mean lepton energy scales approximately with the effective electron magnetization, while the ion energy in the nonrelativistic-escape regime behaves as\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\gamma_i-1\\rangle\n \\propto \\mu^{1/3}\\left(\\sigma_e^\\ast\\right)^{2/3},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mu=m_i/m_e\\). Ion and lepton energies become comparable only when ion escape is itself ultrarelativistic. In pair-dominated systems, the ion energy fraction can consequently lie orders of magnitude below simple equipartition assumptions.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21947", "arxiv": "2609.21947", "title": "Test of lepton-flavor universality with B̄ → D(*)τ⁻ν̄τ decays at Belle II", "authors": "Belle II Collaboration", "tags": ["Particle physics", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Belle II simultaneously measures R(D) and R(D*) with hadronic tagging; the combined result agrees with the Standard Model at 1.5σ.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21947", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Test of lepton-flavor universality with B̄ → D(*)τ⁻ν̄τ decays at Belle II Belle II Collaboration 2609.21947 Belle II simultaneously measures R(D) and R(D*) with hadronic tagging; the combined result agrees with the Standard Model at 1.5σ. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Particle physics BSM #### ★★★ 5. Test of lepton-flavor universality with \\(\\bar B\\rightarrow D^{(*)}\\tau^-\\bar\\nu_\\tau\\) decays at Belle II\n\n**Belle II Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.21947](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21947)\n\nBelle II performs a simultaneous hadronic-tag measurement using \\(387\\) million \\(\\Upsilon(4S)\\) decays and obtains\n\n\\[\nR(D^\\ast)=0.242\\pm0.019_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.016_{\\rm syst},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nR(D)=0.439\\pm0.055_{\\rm stat}\\pm0.046_{\\rm syst}.\n\\]\n\nThe two measurements are negatively correlated. Their combination agrees with the Standard Model at \\(1.5\\sigma\\), while the updated global average differs by approximately \\(1.3\\sigma\\).\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20936", "arxiv": "2609.20936", "title": "AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology", "authors": "Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson", "tags": ["BSM", "Dark matter", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "AutoTherm automates ultrarelativistic thermal production rates, combining matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-20936", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson 2609.20936 AutoTherm automates ultrarelativistic thermal production rates, combining matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 BSM Dark matter Neutrinos #### ★★★ 6. AutoTherm: Automated thermal field-theory rates for cosmology\n\n**Killian Bouzoud, Jacopo Ghiglieri, Greg Jackson**  \n[arXiv:2609.20936](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20936)\n\nAutoTherm is a Wolfram/Python pipeline that converts particle-physics models into ultrarelativistic thermal production rates. It automates \\(2\\leftrightarrow2\\) matrix elements, thermal phase-space integration and hard-thermal-loop resummation for massless \\(t\\)-channel mediators.\n\nThree formally leading-order-equivalent prescriptions are provided to expose sensitivity to the hard-soft separation. Validation cases include right-handed neutrinos, gravitons, gravitinos, axions and dark photons; the comparisons reproduce most known results while identifying corrections or unexpectedly large scheme dependence in several cases.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21823", "arxiv": "2609.21823", "title": "LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Heaνen: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture", "authors": "Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Solar capture and neutrino limits constrain elastic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated 248-keV recoil candidate.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21823", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Heaνen: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha 2609.21823 Solar capture and neutrino limits constrain elastic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated 248-keV recoil candidate. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 7. LUX-ZEPLIN’s Stairway to Hea\\(\\nu\\)en: limits on elastic dark-matter scattering from solar capture\n\n**Debajit Bose, Akash Kumar Saha, Nirmal Raj, Tarak Nath Maity, Ranjan Laha**  \n[arXiv:2609.21823](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21823)\n\nThe authors reconstruct nonrelativistic effective-operator interpretations of LZ’s isolated \\(248\\)-keV recoil candidate and calculate the corresponding capture and annihilation of dark matter in the Sun.\n\nFor annihilation into \\(W^+W^-\\), \\(b\\bar b\\), \\(\\tau^+\\tau^-\\) or neutrino pairs, Super-Kamiokande and IceCube exclude substantial mass ranges in which elastic scattering could reproduce the LZ event. Direct annihilation to neutrinos generally provides the strongest constraint.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21991", "arxiv": "2609.21991", "title": "Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays", "authors": "Tousif Raza", "tags": ["BSM", "Cosmic rays", "Neutrinos"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Atmospheric charged-kaon decays provide a possible source of axion-like particles that decay to photons inside large underground neutrino detectors.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21991", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays Tousif Raza 2609.21991 Atmospheric charged-kaon decays provide a possible source of axion-like particles that decay to photons inside large underground neutrino detectors. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 BSM Cosmic rays Neutrinos #### ★★☆ 8. Searching for axions from atmospheric kaon decays\n\n**Tousif Raza**  \n[arXiv:2609.21991](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21991)\n\nCosmic-ray air showers produce charged kaons that could undergo\n\n\\[\nK^+\\rightarrow\\pi^+a.\n\\]\n\nA sufficiently long-lived axion-like particle can reach an underground detector before decaying to two photons. The paper uses MCEq with atmospheric-density and hadronic-interaction models to predict the angular and energy distributions, then derives constraints from published Super-Kamiokande and IceCube data for masses extending to approximately \\(350\\) MeV.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-21444", "arxiv": "2609.21444", "title": "The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event", "authors": "Ciaran A. J. O’Hare", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and FIRE/Latte are used to study the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate.", "path": "2026/09/21/index.html#paper-2609-21444", "reportDate": "21 September 2026", "searchText": "The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event Ciaran A. J. O’Hare 2609.21444 Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and FIRE/Latte are used to study the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate. 2026-09-21 21 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 9. The high-velocity dark-matter halo and the LZ high-recoil event\n\n**Ciaran A. J. O’Hare**  \n[arXiv:2609.21444](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21444)\n\nThis study uses 98 Milky-Way analogues from TNG50 and three high-resolution FIRE/Latte halos to determine whether realistic halo dynamics materially alter the high-speed dark-matter population required for the LZ recoil candidate.\n\nFor the event date, the inferred maximum laboratory-frame speed is\n\n\\[\nv_{\\max}=792.7^{+82.9}_{-95.6}\\ {\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nat 95% containment, close to the conventional Standard Halo Model estimate. Simulated halos generally co-rotate with their stellar disks, but their negatively skewed velocity distributions retain substantial counter-rotating high-speed tails. At thresholds above roughly \\(700~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}\\), annual modulation can approach unity in some halos."}, {"id": "paper-2609-19477", "arxiv": "2609.19477", "title": "Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events", "authors": "Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19477", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer 2609.19477 The authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. Lepto-hadronic modeling of blazars associated with well-reconstructed high-energy neutrino events\n\n**Leonard Pfeiffer, Margot Boughelilba, Simone Garrappa, Sara Buson, Massimiliano Lincetto, Annette Bremer**  \n[arXiv:2609.19477](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19477)\n\nThe authors examine six blazars that are the sole gamma-ray counterparts inside the reconstructed directions of well-localized IceCAT-1 neutrino events, also accounting for the improved localizations anticipated from IceCAT-2.\n\nMultifrequency spectral-energy distributions are fitted using compact one-zone lepto-hadronic models. The preferred emission regions lie near the broad-line region, where external photons can substantially enhance the photohadronic interaction rate. Most fitted gamma-ray spectra remain leptonic-dominated, with hadronic cascades subdominant; one object permits hadronically dominated gamma-ray emission.\n\nThe maximum predicted IceCube rate is approximately\n\n\\[\nR_\\nu\\sim5\\times10^{-2}\\ {\\rm yr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\nThis can be statistically compatible with one detected event for several sources, but it does not establish a physical association. The modeled objects would contribute negligibly to the diffuse astrophysical-neutrino flux.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19174", "arxiv": "2609.19174", "title": "Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event", "authors": "Mattia Di Mauro", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated 248-keV LZ recoil candidate to an independent solar-neutrino test.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19174", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event Mattia Di Mauro 2609.19174 This paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated 248-keV LZ recoil candidate to an independent solar-neutrino test. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Dark matter Neutrinos BSM #### ★★★ 2. Testing Higgs-Coupled Minimal Dark Matter with Solar Neutrinos after the LZ High-Recoil Event\n\n**Mattia Di Mauro**  \n[arXiv:2609.19174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19174)\n\nThis paper subjects Higgs-coupled minimal dark-matter interpretations of the isolated **248-keV LZ recoil candidate** to an independent solar-neutrino test.\n\nThe electroweak interaction responsible for endothermic xenon scattering also permits capture in the Sun. The calculation includes exact inelastic kinematics, realistic isotope mixtures, nuclear form factors, thermal nuclear motion near the capture threshold, orbital cooling through loop-induced elastic scattering, and an explicit test of capture–annihilation equilibrium.\n\nFor two thermal benchmarks within IceCube’s published mass grid, the predicted solar annihilation rates exceed the corresponding \\(W^+W^-\\) IceCube response by factors of approximately\n\n\\[\n2.2\\times10^3\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n91.\n\\]\n\nA 22-TeV benchmark remains strongly disfavored under a conservative extrapolation beyond IceCube’s published 10-TeV grid. The 48-TeV solution is closer to the sensitivity boundary, while the conclusions for 82 and 130 TeV become increasingly response-dependent.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19259", "arxiv": "2609.19259", "title": "On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources", "authors": "Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19259", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman 2609.19259 Cross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 3. On the Clustering Bias of Unresolved Gamma-Ray Sources\n\n**Bhashin A. Thakore, Marco Regis, Michela Negro, Stefano Camera, Daniel Gruen, Nicolao Fornengo, Aaron J. Roodman**  \n[arXiv:2609.19259](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19259)\n\nCross-correlations between the unresolved gamma-ray background and weak gravitational lensing depend not only on source luminosities and redshifts, but also on how strongly the unresolved sources trace the matter distribution.\n\nThe authors connect gamma-ray luminosity to host-halo mass and calculate the effective redshift-dependent linear bias. For a reference unresolved-blazar population, the cross-correlation is dominated around \\(z\\simeq1\\) by sources with\n\n\\[\n\\langle b\\rangle\\gtrsim2,\n\\qquad\n\\langle M_{\\rm halo}\\rangle\\sim10^{13}M_\\odot.\n\\]\n\nReplacing the blazar population with a misaligned-AGN model gives similar effective bias values. The inferred clustering is therefore comparatively insensitive to the detailed gamma-ray source prescription.\n\nThis is relevant when using UGRB–large-scale-structure correlations to constrain unresolved astrophysical populations or exotic components such as annihilating dark matter: treating the source bias too simplistically can shift the inferred component amplitudes.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-20787", "arxiv": "2609.20787", "title": "A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events", "authors": "Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby long-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-20787", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem 2609.20787 Using two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby long-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 4. A systematic search for long GRBs without supernovae: global properties and rate of events\n\n**Yu-Han Yang, Eleonora Troja, Nicolas Monsalves, Massine El Kabir, Narjes Shahamat Dehsorkh, Hira Waseem**  \n[arXiv:2609.20787](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20787)\n\nUsing two decades of Swift data, the authors systematically search for nearby,\n\n\\[\nz\\lesssim0.35,\n\\]\n\nlong-duration GRBs lacking an accompanying bright supernova. The selected population is heterogeneous: some events lie in star-forming or dusty environments, while others are associated with relatively quiescent galaxies.\n\nRequiring meaningful distance information and sufficiently deep supernova limits more than doubles the previous sample of credible SN-less long-GRB candidates. The inferred unbeamed volumetric rate is\n\n\\[\nR=0.5\\pm0.2\\ {\\rm Gpc^{-3}\\,yr^{-1}},\n\\]\n\ncomparable to the apparent rates of short GRBs and luminous conventional long GRBs.\n\nCompact-object mergers can reproduce this rate for plausible beaming corrections, but assigning the entire population to any single merger channel already restricts the allowed parameter space. The present sample cannot establish whether SN-less long GRBs form a distinct progenitor class.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19948", "arxiv": "2609.19948", "title": "Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in H → γγ", "authors": "ATLAS Collaboration", "tags": ["BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "ATLAS uses interference between resonant Higgs production and the continuum diphoton background to constrain the Higgs total width with an on-shell measurement.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19948", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in H → γγ ATLAS Collaboration 2609.19948 ATLAS uses interference between resonant Higgs production and the continuum diphoton background to constrain the Higgs total width with an on-shell measurement. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 BSM #### ★★★ 5. Constraints on the Higgs boson total width from on-shell signal-background interference in \\(H\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\)\n\n**ATLAS Collaboration**  \n[arXiv:2609.19948](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19948)\n\nATLAS introduces a direct on-shell approach to the Higgs total width. The method uses interference between the resonant\n\n\\[\npp\\rightarrow H\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\n\\]\n\namplitude and the continuum diphoton background. This interference slightly distorts the diphoton invariant-mass distribution in a width-dependent manner.\n\nUsing the full \\(140~{\\rm fb^{-1}}\\) Run-2 dataset, ATLAS obtains\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_H<307~{\\rm MeV}\n\\qquad(95\\%~{\\rm CL}),\n\\]\n\ncompared with an expected limit of \\(258\\) MeV and the Standard-Model prediction\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_H^{\\rm SM}\\simeq4.1~{\\rm MeV}.\n\\]\n\nThe numerical constraint is much weaker than indirect off-shell limits, but it does not require assumptions connecting the on-shell and off-shell Higgs couplings.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19271", "arxiv": "2609.19271", "title": "Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background", "authors": "Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components:", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19271", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen 2609.19271 The paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components: 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. Probing Scalar-Induced First-Order Phase Transitions with the Diffuse Supernova Neutrino Background\n\n**Sudipta Das, Shamik Niyogi, Manibrata Sen**  \n[arXiv:2609.19271](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19271)\n\nThe paper considers a light scalar coupled to neutrinos and thermalized inside proto-neutron stars. A scalar-driven first-order phase transition would generate two additional neutrino components:\n\n- a prompt contribution from thermally produced scalars;\n- a delayed, spectrally narrower component from coherent scalar excitations produced during the transition.\n\nAfter integrating over the cosmic core-collapse history, the authors calculate the resulting DSNB spectra for Super-Kamiokande, Hyper-Kamiokande and DUNE. The constraints depend strongly on neutrino mass ordering and absolute mass scale. Existing Super-K data already probe part of the parameter space for inverted ordering with quasi-degenerate masses.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19922", "arxiv": "2609.19922", "title": "Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers", "authors": "Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Binary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19922", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich 2609.19922 Binary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Neutrinos Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Investigating the influence of full-kinematics charged-current processes in binary neutron-star mergers\n\n**Ramon Jaeger, Henrique Gieg, Tim Dietrich**  \n[arXiv:2609.19922](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19922)\n\nBinary-neutron-star simulations commonly calculate charged-current neutrino reactions using an elastic approximation. This work compares that treatment with NuLib rates and a full-kinematics calculation within M1 neutrino transport.\n\nThe elastic approximation slightly overestimates dynamical ejecta and early neutrino luminosities while underproducing high-electron-fraction ejecta. However, NuLib and full-kinematics simulations agree within current numerical uncertainties. Their post-merger gravitational-wave spectra are indistinguishable, and most nucleosynthetic yields remain consistent.\n\nThe main exception occurs around\n\n\\[\nA\\simeq90-125,\n\\]\n\nwhere the elastic approximation predicts larger abundances. Differences also appear in remnant-disk properties and could become more consequential in longer secular simulations.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-19984", "arxiv": "2609.19984", "title": "A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz", "authors": "Aiswarya Sankar K. et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands.", "path": "2026/09/18/index.html#paper-2609-19984", "reportDate": "18 September 2026", "searchText": "A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz Aiswarya Sankar K. et al. 2609.19984 A multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands. 2026-09-18 18 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. A 32-day quasi-periodic modulation in the post-peak light curve of SN 2018bsz\n\n**Aiswarya Sankar K. et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.19984](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19984)\n\nA multiband reanalysis of the nearby hydrogen-poor superluminous supernova SN 2018bsz finds phase-coherent post-maximum residual variability across ten optical and near-infrared bands.\n\nThe joint rest-frame period is\n\n\\[\nP=31.61\\pm0.03~{\\rm days},\n\\]\n\nalthough only about three cycles are observed, so the signal is appropriately classified as quasi-periodic. Its amplitude rises toward shorter wavelengths. Tests using alternative detrending choices, comparison stars and the lunar cycle disfavor straightforward analysis or observing artifacts.\n\nPossible explanations include Lense–Thirring precession of a central engine, interaction with circumstellar material structured before the explosion, or interaction with a surviving binary companion. Current data do not discriminate among them.\n\n---\n\nAn additional particle-phenomenology study is **“SHiP as a (post-)discovery machine: identifying the diphoton signals’ origin”** by Climescu, Mikkelsen and Ovchynnikov. It finds that as few as **2–4 reconstructed diphoton decays** could distinguish pure hypercharge-coupled from pure \\(SU(2)_L\\)-coupled ALPs for masses between approximately \\(0.2\\) and \\(1\\) GeV. [arXiv:2609.19269](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.19269)\n\nThere is **no sufficiently important new UHECR composition, spectrum or anisotropy result**, and no new precision laboratory-neutrino-oscillation measurement in the 18 September batch."}, {"id": "paper-2609-18383", "arxiv": "2609.18383", "title": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly", "authors": "Hicham Benmansour et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18383", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly Hicham Benmansour et al. 2609.18383 The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM #### ★★★ 1. On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly\n\n**Hicham Benmansour et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18383](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18383)\n\nThe authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.\n\nCosmic-ray muons producing two-arm coincidences generate peaks whose locations are controlled by the detectors’ discrete azimuthal geometry:\n\n\\[\n\\theta_{e^+e^-}\\sim140^\\circ\n\\]\n\nin the \\(^{8}\\mathrm{Be}\\) energy window and approximately \\(120^\\circ\\) in the \\(^{4}\\mathrm{He}\\) window. These structures resemble the reported Atomki anomalies not only in angle, but also in their energy-window and pair-asymmetry dependence.\n\nFor the five-arm apparatus, normalization to representative running conditions gives a cosmic-to-internal-pair-conversion ratio above unity in the signal region. The calculation does not prove that cosmic rays explain the anomaly, because the conclusion ultimately depends on the actual beam-off data and event-selection procedure. It does, however, establish cosmic backgrounds as a potentially leading contribution that must be demonstrated experimentally to be under control.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18226", "arxiv": "2609.18226", "title": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave", "authors": "Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense…", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18226", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith 2609.18226 Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 2. Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave\n\n**Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith**  \n[arXiv:2609.18226](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18226)\n\nOrdinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense red-supergiant wind.\n\nUsing cosmic-ray distributions from kinetic-MHD simulations that reach proton energies near \\(3\\) PeV, the authors calculate the time-dependent hadronic gamma-ray signal, including \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) attenuation on the supernova photospheric radiation field and diffuse cosmic backgrounds.\n\nThe photospheric field suppresses the intrinsic \\(1\\!-\\!100\\) TeV flux by roughly one order of magnitude, but does not erase it. For\n\n\\[\n\\dot M_{\\rm RSG}\\sim10^{-3}\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nR_{\\rm CSM}\\sim3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm cm},\n\\]\n\nthey estimate that a 50-hour CTA observation could detect emission around \\(100\\) TeV from distances up to approximately \\(4\\) Mpc. Reducing the circumstellar extent to \\(2\\times10^{15}\\) cm lowers the horizon to roughly \\(3\\) Mpc.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18247", "arxiv": "2609.18247", "title": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae", "authors": "Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["BSM", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18247", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun 2609.18247 This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 BSM Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae\n\n**Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun**  \n[arXiv:2609.18247](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18247)\n\nThis paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as\n\n\\[\nX\\rightarrow e^+e^- .\n\\]\n\nIf the decay occurs in the circumstellar medium, the positrons slow down and annihilate before the outgoing shock arrives, generating a transient \\(511\\) keV line.\n\nUsing MeV-scale dark photons as a benchmark and circumstellar profiles motivated by SN 2023ixf and SN 2024ggi, the authors find that a Galactic explosion at \\(10\\) kpc could allow COSI—and a future AMEGO-like mission—to probe parameter space beyond existing supernova limits.\n\nThe same deposited energy can heat the circumstellar gas and destroy dust, producing a rapid infrared-to-optical/UV transition. A coincident \\(511\\) keV line and dust-sublimation signature would therefore be substantially harder to mimic than either signal alone.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17705", "arxiv": "2609.17705", "title": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB", "authors": "Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17705", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler 2609.17705 The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 4. Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB\n\n**Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler**  \n[arXiv:2609.17705](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17705)\n\nThe authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.\n\nThe technically important component is their treatment of atmospheric-neutrino backgrounds. Oscillations and propagation modify the flavor and energy distributions entering the DSNB window; approximating these backgrounds too crudely can bias both the discovery significance and the reconstructed DSNB flux.\n\nTheir conclusion is that additional Super-K exposure produces only modest sensitivity gains, whereas Hyper-K has the potential for a statistically significant DSNB detection after approximately ten years.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18353", "arxiv": "2609.18353", "title": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant?", "authors": "Josep Martí et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18353", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant? Josep Martí et al. 2609.18353 LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays #### ★★★ 5. The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant?\n\n**Josep Martí et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18353](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18353)\n\nLHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.\n\nThe proposed explanation is a past microquasar outburst injecting one population of relativistic protons. Energy-dependent diffusion then separates that population:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{lower-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{remain near dense gas}\\rightarrow\\text{GeV emission},\\\\\n\\text{higher-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{reach more distant gas}\\rightarrow\\text{TeV--PeV emission}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe same transport model accounts for the compact–extended morphological difference and the absence of a bright present-day radio or X-ray source.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17665", "arxiv": "2609.17665", "title": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers", "authors": "Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information…", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17665", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi 2609.17665 Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 6. Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers\n\n**Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17665](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17665)\n\nPrevious tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information in one hierarchical Bayesian likelihood.\n\nApplied to GWTC-5.0 and the Quaia AGN catalogue, the analysis does not exclude an AGN contribution. For unobscured AGN above three luminosity thresholds,\n\n\\[\nL_{\\rm bol}\\geq\n\\{10^{44.5},10^{45.5},10^{46.5}\\}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nthe corresponding 95% credible upper limits on the AGN-hosted merger fraction are approximately\n\n\\[\nf_{\\rm AGN}<\\{0.52,0.41,0.61\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe current bounds are therefore moderate, but the joint framework should become considerably more informative as GW localization, population statistics and AGN catalogues improve.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17661", "arxiv": "2609.17661", "title": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres", "authors": "Jens F. Mahlmann et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17661", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres Jens F. Mahlmann et al. 2609.17661 These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★☆ 7. 1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres\n\n**Jens F. Mahlmann et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17661](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17661)\n\nThese particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.\n\nDespite strong drag opposing both electron and positron flows, the circuit reorganizes into a single accelerating gap near the stellar surface in one hemisphere. The resulting outflow becomes heavily pair-loaded, while ions extracted from the surface help transfer momentum across the equatorial region through streaming instabilities.\n\nA notable prediction is that confinement of the gap to one hemisphere should produce asymmetric hard-X-ray emission that may be visible in phase-resolved spectra.\n\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17672", "arxiv": "2609.17672", "title": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner", "authors": "Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17672", "reportDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi 2609.17672 The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 8. WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner\n\n**Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17672)\n\nThe authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.\n\nIf the partner decays after WIMP freeze-out, it injects a nonthermal WIMP population. Obtaining the observed relic density then requires re-annihilation and an annihilation cross section potentially up to\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\sim10^3\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle_{\\rm canonical}.\n\\]\n\nThis creates a relic-density mechanism that remains WIMP-like but predicts much stronger present-day indirect-detection signals. A light-higgsino realization is exhibited in the NMSSM.\n\n\n---\n\nNo genuinely new experimental **UHECR composition/anisotropy measurement** or precision **neutrino-oscillation result** appears in the 17 September batch. The paper on lensing-induced neutrino decoherence, [arXiv:2609.18202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18202), presents a novel idea, but its observational utility depends critically on whether the relevant astrophysical neutrino wave packets retain sufficient coherence before encountering the proposed stochastic lensing structure."}, {"id": "paper-2609-17275", "arxiv": "2609.17275", "title": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey", "authors": "Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring…", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17275", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al. 2609.17275 Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey\n\n**Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17275](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17275)\n\nUsing an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the **first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz**. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring of the already-known repeater FRB 20180916B.\n\nThis matters physically because plasma scattering and free–free absorption should become much stronger toward low frequencies. The inferred event rate is nevertheless compatible with rates measured above 400 MHz, disfavoring explanations in which severe low-frequency suppression is generic across the FRB population. It also demonstrates that wide-field imaging searches can handle the tens-of-seconds dispersion delays that make conventional low-frequency transient searches computationally difficult.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17315", "arxiv": "2609.17315", "title": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky", "authors": "D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17315", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò 2609.17315 The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 2. Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky\n\n**D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò**  \n[arXiv:2609.17315](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17315)\n\nThe authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq0.34~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nand the knee near\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq3.3~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nreported by LHAASO. A decaying relic with\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\simeq5\\times10^7~{\\rm GeV}\n\\]\n\nprovides a good fit to the proton spectrum across all three hadronic-interaction-model reconstructions considered.\n\nThe same hadronic cascade inevitably produces photons, however. Those photons exceed the measured cosmic-ray photon fraction by roughly **two orders of magnitude** and the LHAASO diffuse Galactic gamma-ray emission by about **one order of magnitude**.\n\nThe result is a particularly clean example of a multimessenger consistency test:\n\n\\[\n\\text{fit to CR nucleons}\n\\quad\\not\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\text{viable model once }\\gamma\\text{-rays are included}.\n\\]\n\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16425", "arxiv": "2609.16425", "title": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter", "authors": "Stefano Profumo", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16425", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter Stefano Profumo 2609.16425 This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter #### ★★★ 3. Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter\n\n**Stefano Profumo**  \n[arXiv:2609.16425](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16425)\n\nThis reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.\n\nThe excess remains present under several instrumental and data-splitting checks, but its inferred morphology is unexpectedly broad: uniform surface brightness or strongly substructure-enhanced emission is preferred over a smooth NFW annihilation template or central point source. Accounting for the restricted energy and morphology scan gives a conditional global significance of only about\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nwith the maximum near \\(44.5\\) GeV. Because the three-cluster sample had already been selected, this is not an unconditional discovery significance.\n\nA dark-matter interpretation would require an extreme subhalo boost and a large \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) branching fraction, leading to an annihilation cross section already in tension with stronger Galactic-halo line searches. The favored explanation is therefore a statistical fluctuation, possibly assisted by diffuse-background or unresolved-source mismodelling.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16529", "arxiv": "2609.16529", "title": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection", "authors": "Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16529", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang 2609.16529 The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★★ 4. Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection\n\n**Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.16529](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16529)\n\nThe paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for \\(^{129}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) and \\(^{131}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.\n\nAfter performing the multipole expansion and imposing angular-momentum, parity and time-reversal selection rules, the authors find that nuclear excitation cannot always be treated as a small correction. Depending on the effective DM operator:\n\n- the inelastic rate can be comparable to the elastic rate;\n- for some operators it can dominate by as much as three orders of magnitude;\n- transitions to higher excited states can be as important as the first excited state.\n\nThe resulting response functions differ significantly from shell-model calculations in some channels, potentially changing the interpretation of xenon direct-detection limits and high-energy recoil events.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16139", "arxiv": "2609.16139", "title": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube", "authors": "Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16139", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki 2609.16139 The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 5. Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D \\(U(1)_{L_\\mu-L_\\tau}\\) in IceCube\n\n**Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki**  \n[arXiv:2609.16139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16139)\n\nThe authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.\n\nInstead of one \\(L_\\mu-L_\\tau\\) mediator, a compact fifth dimension generates a Kaluza–Klein tower. This produces multiple nearby \\(s\\)-channel resonances, supplemented by \\(t\\)- and \\(u\\)-channel scattering. Repeated interactions can consequently create broad, energy-dependent attenuation rather than the isolated absorption dip typical of a single mediator.\n\nThe mechanism can soften an injected single power law, but its interpretation is highly model-dependent: it depends on the compactification scale, coupling, mediator spectrum, neutrino masses and assumed unattenuated source population. “Hints” in the title should therefore not be read as statistical evidence for an extra dimension.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16547", "arxiv": "2609.16547", "title": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra", "authors": "Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16547", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam 2609.16547 For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 6. Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra\n\n**Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam**  \n[arXiv:2609.16547](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16547)\n\nFor two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with\n\n\\[\nE_\\gamma=\\Delta E,\n\\]\n\nproducing a narrow line in the spectra of background galaxies or quasars.\n\nThe authors stack **145,087 DESI Early Data Release spectra** whose sightlines pass through foreground GAMA galaxy groups. No absorption line is found, and they derive \\(2\\sigma\\) limits on the transition cross section.\n\nThe strongest sensitivity occurs for splittings around \\(3\\!-\\!5\\) eV and DM masses of a few MeV, including parameter space poorly covered by direct detection and conventional BBN/CMB limits. The full DESI sample could improve the sensitivity by more than an order of magnitude.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16826", "arxiv": "2609.16826", "title": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A", "authors": "Toshiki Sato et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon…", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16826", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A Toshiki Sato et al. 2609.16826 The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to \\(^{44}\\)Ti in Cassiopeia A\n\n**Toshiki Sato et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.16826](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16826)\n\nThe usual interpretation treats the \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon shell merger.\n\nThe low- and intermediate-velocity \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) components correlate more strongly with oxygen-layer tracers than with shocked iron-rich material. If that association is pre-supernova in origin, it could account for as much as\n\n\\[\nM_{^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}}\\sim7\\times10^{-5}M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nroughly half the observed inventory. Producing this amount requires stronger ingestion and convection than standard one-dimensional stellar-evolution models predict. XRISM measurements of stable Fe-group abundance ratios provide an independent restriction on how much \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) can come from explosive burning.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17507", "arxiv": "2609.17507", "title": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts", "authors": "Muskan Yadav et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17507", "reportDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts Muskan Yadav et al. 2609.17507 The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts\n\n**Muskan Yadav et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17507](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17507)\n\nThe paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.\n\nATCA observations of seven nearby candidates at \\(2.1\\) GHz, taken \\(520\\!-\\!6900\\) days after the bursts, yield no late-time counterparts, with \\(3\\sigma\\) limits of \\(33\\!-\\!72~\\mu{\\rm Jy}\\). These limits do not yet exclude energetic magnetar-driven ejecta when the circumburst density is low.\n\nCombining the radio nondetections with fallback-powered prompt-emission modelling sharply restricts the allowed magnetar spin, magnetic field and jet baryon loading. A magnetar engine remains possible, but only in a comparatively narrow region of parameter space.\n\n---\n\nThere is **no sufficiently important new neutrino-oscillation result or new UHECR anisotropy/composition measurement** in the 16 September batch. The main neutrino item is the extra-dimensional attenuation model above; it is theoretical and exploratory."}, {"id": "paper-2609-14955", "arxiv": "2609.14955", "title": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter", "authors": "Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14955", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang 2609.14955 Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter\n\n**Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang**\n[arXiv:2609.14955](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14955)\n\nUsing almost **17 years of Fermi-LAT data**, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over \\(105^\\circ<l<145^\\circ\\). All three components show a clear **pion bump**, so the inferred emissivity is directly tracing the underlying CR-proton population rather than relying on a purely phenomenological gamma-ray spectrum.\n\nThey obtain proton spectral indices approximately\n\\[\n\\Gamma_p\\simeq\n2.75\\quad{\\rm Local},\\qquad\n2.55\\quad{\\rm Perseus},\\qquad\n2.80\\quad{\\rm Outer},\n\\]\nwith CR energy densities above \\(10\\) GeV of order\n\\[\nu_{\\rm CR}\\sim0.2~{\\rm eV\\,cm^{-3}}\n\\]\nin all three arms. The important tension is that the outer-Galaxy CR population comes out **denser and harder than standard propagation models usually predict** as Galactocentric radius increases. Possible explanations include a larger CR halo, slower radial decline of the magnetic field, or local acceleration—especially in the Perseus arm.\n\n**Significance:** it is a clean observational handle on the radial dependence of Galactic CR transport, with obvious implications for diffuse gamma rays and Galactic-neutrino modelling.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely useful observational CR result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13363", "arxiv": "2609.13363", "title": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO", "authors": "Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13363", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe 2609.13363 They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO\n\n**Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe**\n[arXiv:2609.13363](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13363)\n\nThey start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the **NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result** and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to become individually resolvable PTA sources.\n\nTwo objects stand out: **3C 66B and Mrk 501**. In their population realization these systems have a good chance of rising above the unresolved stochastic background. The result is especially interesting because individual detections would discriminate between binary-evolution scenarios: strong environmental hardening changes the abundance of resolvable systems relative to a purely GW-driven inspiral population.\n\nFor the SKA era, after including confusion noise from the unresolved SMBH population, they forecast **tens of individually detectable binaries**, with 3C 66B particularly robust.\n\n**Significance:** this is genuine **multimessenger source-population physics**—connecting radio/AGN binary candidates to PTA gravitational waves rather than treating the GW background only statistically.\n\n**Classification:** **new multimessenger population forecast; conceptually strong.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14446", "arxiv": "2609.14446", "title": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance", "authors": "Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14446", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun 2609.14446 They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★★ Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance\n\n**Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun**\n[arXiv:2609.14446](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14446)\n\nThey perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full\n\\[\n6\\times6\n\\]\nneutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.\n\nFor **Majorana neutrinos**, a transition magnetic moment can generate\n\\[\n\\nu_\\alpha\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_\\beta ,\n\\]\nwhereas for Dirac neutrinos the flipped state is sterile. A useful technical result is that in normal ordering they find **no finite-density Majorana level crossing** for \\(1\\!-\\!20\\) MeV solar neutrinos. A Dirac resonance appears above about \\(12\\) MeV, but it involves only a small \\(\\nu_e\\) component, so the resonant disappearance signal is limited.\n\nThe more interesting signature is therefore **nonresonant Majorana conversion producing solar \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\)**. For a future 3-kton Jinping detector operating for \\(5\\!-\\!10\\) years they estimate sensitivity to\n\\[\nP(\\nu_e\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_e)\n\\sim(0.85-1.3)\\times10^{-5}.\n\\]\nAssuming optimistic transverse core fields \\(B_\\perp\\sim7\\!-\\!10\\) MG, this corresponds in their \\(\\mu_{12}\\)-only example to\n\\[\n|\\mu_{12}|\\sim(2.3-4.1)\\times10^{-13}\\mu_B .\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** it gives an explicit three-flavor treatment of the distinction between **Dirac disappearance and Majorana antineutrino appearance**, which is much more informative than a simple magnetic-moment bound.\n\n**Classification:** **good neutrino-phenomenology paper; projected rather than observed result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13821", "arxiv": "2609.13821", "title": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology", "authors": "Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13821", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott 2609.13821 This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations #### ★★★ Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology\n\n**Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott**\n[arXiv:2609.13821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13821)\n\nThis paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular **flavor**, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply evolve three independent flavor fluids.\n\nStarting from the neutrino **quantum kinetic equations**, they derive the Boltzmann hierarchy in the neutrino-mass basis and determine when a simpler combined-fluid approximation is valid.\n\nTheir practical result is neat. Existing CMB limits on an effective neutrino self-coupling should be weakened by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nwhen two flavors interact and by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\nwhen only one flavor interacts. The \\(\\nu_e\\)-only case is the one where the fluid approximation becomes somewhat less reliable.\n\n**Significance:** this is one of those papers that can quietly change how constraints are recast across a large class of neutrino-BSM models; the correction is not orders of magnitude, but it is conceptually important.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological improvement to cosmological neutrino constraints.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15970", "arxiv": "2609.15970", "title": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance", "authors": "Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15970", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek 2609.15970 This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history? 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance\n\n**Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek**\n[arXiv:2609.15970](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15970)\n\nThis asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu\n\\]\nbe evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?\n\nThey explicitly model late particle decays injecting photons and/or dark radiation, and then perform MCMC analyses using **Planck primary CMB, Planck+ACT lensing and DESI BAO**. Surprisingly, reducing the neutrino abundance does **not** substantially weaken the mass bound.\n\nFor degenerate masses they quote\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu <0.0691~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor the standard thermal history,\n\\[\n<0.0652~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor photon-only injection, and\n\\[\n<0.0710~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nwhen dark radiation is also produced. Even allowing the neutrino/photon temperature ratio to vary phenomenologically gives only\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu<0.0724~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nat 95% credibility.\n\nThe underlying reason is interesting: \\(T_\\nu\\) and \\(\\sum m_\\nu\\) come out **positively correlated** once the full cosmological dataset is used.\n\n**Significance:** the emerging tension between cosmological neutrino-mass bounds and laboratory oscillation mass splittings cannot apparently be dismissed simply by saying “perhaps the relic neutrino density is smaller.”\n\n**Classification:** **important robustness check.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14508", "arxiv": "2609.14508", "title": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets", "authors": "Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14508", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu 2609.14508 The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★☆ First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets\n\n**Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu**\n[arXiv:2609.14508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14508)\n\nThe problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the **jet cocoon** provides a less-beamed early counterpart.\n\nHydrodynamic simulations plus radiative post-processing give a mildly relativistic cocoon producing soft quasi-thermal X-rays with\n\\[\nL_X\\sim10^{46}-10^{48}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\qquad\nkT\\sim0.1-1~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\nlasting a few to roughly ten seconds.\n\nFor viewing angles around \\(10^\\circ\\), Einstein Probe could see the signal to \\(z\\sim0.3\\). For a GW170817-like nearby event, detectability may extend to roughly \\(45^\\circ\\). Their fiducial Einstein Probe rate is about\n\\[\n0.5~{\\rm yr}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** a genuinely useful possible **early X-ray trigger for off-axis GW mergers**, preceding the kilonova and conventional afterglow.\n\n**Classification:** **multimessenger prediction, not yet observational confirmation.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14551", "arxiv": "2609.14551", "title": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023", "authors": "Yuya Nakamura et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV…", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14551", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023 Yuya Nakamura et al. 2609.14551 GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays #### ★★☆ First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023\n\n**Yuya Nakamura et al.**\n[arXiv:2609.14551](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14551)\n\nGRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about\n\\[\n1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV},\n\\qquad\n0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\nmaking the difficult sub-GeV Galactic-center region less confusion-limited than Fermi in some respects.\n\nRather than looking at the familiar \\(\\sim2\\) GeV GCE peak, they deliberately examine\n\\[\n75-300~{\\rm MeV},\n\\]\nwhere predicted spectra from millisecond-pulsar and DM-annihilation interpretations diverge more strongly.\n\nThey see **no significant excess** and obtain a \\(2\\sigma\\) upper limit\n\\[\nE^2\\Phi_{\\gamma}<1.70\\times10^{-7}\n\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\nfor a \\(1^\\circ\\)-radius Galactic-center ROI. Current statistics still allow both the DM and MSP interpretations.\n\n**Significance:** the important part is not the reported limit itself, which is still weak, but the ability to obtain **high-angular-resolution sub-GeV imaging** precisely where the GCE models become spectrally distinguishable.\n\n**Classification:** **first instrumental result; interesting but not yet constraining enough to settle the GCE.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15985", "arxiv": "2609.15985", "title": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN", "authors": "Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The analysis constrains dark-matter masses and interactions compatible with the LZ high-recoil candidate with limited model assumptions.", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15985", "reportDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi 2609.15985 The analysis constrains dark-matter masses and interactions compatible with the LZ high-recoil candidate with limited model assumptions. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN\n\n**Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi**\n[arXiv:2609.15985](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15985)\n\nThere is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ \\(248\\) keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively **model-independent**.\n\nThey infer the posterior directly in the space of DM masses, mass splittings and effective nuclear interactions, retaining exact two-body kinematics. The single high-recoil event plus the lack of lower-energy events drives the solution into disconnected kinematic regimes.\n\nGeneric interactions favor a finely tuned **endothermic heavy-WIMP region**, while exothermic scattering permits masses ranging from sub-GeV to heavy DM with large splittings. Purely elastic interpretations survive only with momentum suppression and/or spin-dependent operators.\n\n**Significance:** the inference characterizes what the LZ candidate implies without committing to a specific \\(U(1)'\\), SUSY or freeze-in construction.\n\n**Classification:** **useful inference paper, but the underlying LZ anomaly is still weak; not evidence for DM.**\n\nAn additional methodological paper is **“A Unified Numerical Study of Axion Stars: From the Nonrelativistic Regime to General Relativity”** by Arabameri, Arias, Colipí-Marchant and Schiappacasse. They benchmark Schrödinger–Poisson, relativistic EFT and full Einstein–Klein–Gordon calculations against one another and show quantitatively where the usual nonrelativistic axion-star description breaks down. This is a reference and methodological study."}, {"id": "paper-2609-12055", "arxiv": "2609.12055", "title": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The…", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12055", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.12055 IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The… 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. **Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore**\n\n**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12055](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12055)\n\nIceCube has developed a dedicated **sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data**, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The motivation is especially strong for sources with soft spectra.\n\nThey perform three searches: X-ray-bright AGN selected with Swift/BAT, including **NGC 1068**; Galactic Fermi-LAT sources whose spectra look compatible with \\(\\pi^0\\) decay; and an all-sky point-source search. They also combine individual source \\(p\\)-values with a binomial test to look for weak population-level emission. No statistically significant sub-TeV signal appears in any of them.\n\nFor NGC 1068, assuming\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\Phi_\\nu}{dE}\\propto E^{-3.4},\n\\]\n\nthey obtain\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\Phi_{\\nu+\\bar\\nu}\\right|_{1\\,{\\rm TeV}}\n<\n9.5\\times10^{-11}\n\\ {\\rm TeV^{-1}\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nper flavor at 90% C.L., probing approximately **30–400 GeV**. Importantly, that upper limit is still about a factor of two *above* the extrapolation of IceCube's higher-energy NGC 1068 measurement, so it does **not yet rule out** a simple continuation of the measured soft component.\n\n**Significance:** this provides an observational input for multimessenger modelling of NGC 1068. It begins constraining what the neutrino spectrum can do below the existing IceCube measurement and therefore interfaces naturally with MeV–GeV electromagnetic constraints and source-opacity/cascade models.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12174", "arxiv": "2609.12174", "title": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures", "authors": "Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "An energy-weighted directional statistic searches for spectral variations in UHECR arrival maps, complementing conventional event-count anisotropy searches.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12174", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira 2609.12174 An energy-weighted directional statistic searches for spectral variations in UHECR arrival maps, complementing conventional event-count anisotropy searches. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Cosmic rays #### ★★★ 2. **Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures**\n\n**Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira**  \n[arXiv:2609.12174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12174)\n\nMost UHECR source searches ask whether some angular region contains **more particles than expected**,\n\n\\[\nN(\\hat n)>N_{\\rm iso}(\\hat n).\n\\]\n\nBut a real source contribution could alter the *local energy distribution* without producing an obvious flux excess, particularly after magnetic smearing.\n\nThe authors construct a directional functional \\(\\mu_E\\) of the energy spectrum specifically designed to separate\n\n\\[\n\\text{flux normalization}\n\\qquad\\text{from}\\qquad\n\\text{spectral shape}.\n\\]\n\nThey then generalize the **Li–Ma significance** formalism to such energy-weighted observables and test the statistic in Monte Carlo. Hard-spectrum patches that are essentially invisible in conventional event-count maps become detectable with \\(\\mu_E\\).\n\nThat idea is interesting because magnetic deflections can smear angular overdensities while still allowing a direction-dependent spectrum to survive.\n\n**Significance:** it is complementary to conventional UHECR anisotropy statistics and particularly relevant to the general question of how much source information survives GMF/EGMF smearing. Comparison with composition-sensitive and entropy/information-based approaches could help establish the statistic's complementary value.\n\n**Assessment:** **new methodology; potentially useful rather than yet experimentally decisive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12044", "arxiv": "2609.12044", "title": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes", "authors": "Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A joint spectral and morphological analysis of 20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data constrains primordial black holes through inverse-Compton hard X-rays.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12044", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque 2609.12044 A joint spectral and morphological analysis of 20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS data constrains primordial black holes through inverse-Compton hard X-rays. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter Multimessenger #### ★★★ 3. **Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes**\n\n**Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.12044](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12044)\n\nLow-mass PBHs emit \\(e^\\pm\\) through Hawking evaporation. Those electrons and positrons subsequently inverse-Compton scatter the Galactic radiation field,\n\n\\[\ne^\\pm+\\gamma_{\\rm ISRF}\\rightarrow e^\\pm+\\gamma ,\n\\]\n\ncreating diffuse hard X-rays.\n\nThe authors use **20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS observations** and, importantly, do not rely only on the energy spectrum. They perform a combined **spectral + morphological analysis**, modelling the conventional Galactic hard-X-ray sky with cosmic-ray-electron IC emission plus unresolved accreting white dwarfs and adding the PBH-induced IC morphology.\n\nThey obtain competitive constraints on the PBH DM fraction for monochromatic, extended and rotating-PBH mass functions. Their dominant theoretical uncertainty is not Hawking physics itself but the Galactic transport of the relatively low-energy \\(e^\\pm\\) responsible for the IC emission.\n\nThat is an important methodological point:\n\n\\[\n\\text{particle-physics injection}\n\\longrightarrow e^\\pm\\ {\\rm propagation}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm IC}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm diffuse\\ morphology}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** besides the PBH result itself, the spectral+morphological separation of signal from Galactic diffuse emission is highly relevant to any MeV/hard-X-ray multimessenger analysis.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new search strategy and observational constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12821", "arxiv": "2609.12821", "title": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter", "authors": "Yugen Lin", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12821", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter Yugen Lin 2609.12821 This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★★ 4. **Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter**\n\n**Yugen Lin**  \n[arXiv:2609.12821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12821)\n\nThis paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.\n\nBecause the proto-neutron-star core contains an enormous neutrino density, even extremely weak interactions can efficiently populate a new light sector. If the produced DM escapes, it adds a new cooling channel.\n\nUsing modern supernova simulation profiles, the paper derives bounds on effective \\(\\nu\\)-DM interactions for sub-GeV DM. The striking numerical claim is that for\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\lesssim {\\cal O}(100)\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\nthe SN cooling limits on the reference DM–neutrino cross section can improve existing indirect-detection limits by **more than ten orders of magnitude**.\n\nThe physics is essentially the usual SN energy-loss argument, but with the very large thermal neutrino bath acting as the production environment rather than nucleons/electrons.\n\n**Significance:** it connects supernova neutrino transport directly to light-dark-sector phenomenology and illustrates how dramatically SN1987A-like arguments can outperform terrestrial/indirect searches for genuinely neutrinophilic states.\n\n**Assessment:** **new BSM constraint; quite interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12942", "arxiv": "2609.12942", "title": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay", "authors": "Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12942", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer 2609.12942 Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM #### ★★☆ 5. **Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay**\n\n**Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer**  \n[arXiv:2609.12942](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12942)\n\nHere the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by **TRISTAN**.\n\nThe authors calculate the spectral distortions for both lepton-number-conserving and lepton-number-violating interactions and derive updated laboratory constraints. Ordinarily cosmology wins over beta-decay experiments, but they emphasize an important assumption behind that comparison: the particle and its interactions must exist in essentially the same form in the early Universe.\n\nThey therefore construct an explicit pseudo-Nambu–Goldstone realization with a nontrivial symmetry-breaking thermal history in which the usual cosmological constraints can be evaded. In that case,\n\n\\[\n{\\rm KATRIN/TRISTAN}\n\\]\n\ncan become the leading probe.\n\n**Significance:** good example of the often-overlooked distinction between a powerful cosmological constraint and a genuinely model-independent laboratory constraint.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting neutrino/ALP phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12542", "arxiv": "2609.12542", "title": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters", "authors": "Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12542", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao 2609.12542 This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 6. **Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters**\n\n**Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao**  \n[arXiv:2609.12542](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12542)\n\nThis attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.\n\nThey instead impose three predictive relations,\n\n\\[\nM_D=M_u,\\qquad M_D=M_d,\\qquad \\text{or}\\qquad M_D=M_\\ell,\n\\]\n\nso the seesaw equation allows the heavy Majorana matrix to be reconstructed from low-energy parameters.\n\nThey evolve the flavored asymmetries using **density-matrix Boltzmann equations**. Viable thermal leptogenesis occurs for normal ordering with nonzero Majorana phases in the down-quark-like and charged-lepton-like constructions. Successful points favor a close heavy-neutrino pair,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|M_i-M_j|}{M_i}<10^{-3},\n\\]\n\nalthough the authors stress that it is not the conventional resonant-leptogenesis limit.\n\nThe interesting experimental payoff is \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\): some successful leptogenesis regions predict\n\n\\[\nm_{\\beta\\beta}\n\\]\n\nwithin reach of future **sub-10-meV** experiments such as LEGEND-1000, nEXO and CUPID-1T.\n\n**Significance:** this is exactly the kind of seesaw construction where cosmological baryogenesis and measurable low-energy neutrino observables cease to be almost completely disconnected.\n\n**Assessment:** **good neutrino-pheno paper; model-dependent but predictive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12775", "arxiv": "2609.12775", "title": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype", "authors": "Javier De Miguel et al.", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A straightforward but clean experimental result.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12775", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype Javier De Miguel et al. 2609.12775 A straightforward but clean experimental result. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 7. **Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype**\n\n**Javier De Miguel et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12775)\n\nA straightforward but clean experimental result.\n\nThe DALI haloscope prototype analyzed **36 hours** of data around\n\n\\[\n\\nu\\simeq6.9~{\\rm GHz},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a dark-photon mass near\n\n\\[\nm_{A'}=28.54~\\mu{\\rm eV}.\n\\]\n\nNo statistically significant excess is observed, giving approximately\n\n\\[\n\\chi\\lesssim6.8\\times10^{-14},\n\\]\n\nwhich the authors report as the strongest laboratory exclusion in that narrow frequency range.\n\n**Significance:** not transformative, but a real experimental improvement in ultralight-vector DM parameter space rather than another forecast paper.\n\n**Assessment:** **incremental but solid experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12120", "arxiv": "2609.12120", "title": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A", "authors": "Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Cosmogenic-neutrino propagation calculations test Lorentz-invariance violation in interpretations of KM3-230213A.", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12120", "reportDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos 2609.12120 Cosmogenic-neutrino propagation calculations test Lorentz-invariance violation in interpretations of KM3-230213A. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM #### ★★☆ 8. **Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A**\n\n**Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos**  \n[arXiv:2609.12120](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12120)\n\nThey generate standard cosmogenic neutrino spectra using **CRPropa 3.2**, then introduce superluminal neutrino LIV producing neutrino splitting. The basic spectral effect is\n\n\\[\n{\\rm LIV}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{suppression at the highest energies},\\\\\n\\text{pile-up/enhancement at PeV--EeV energies}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing KM3-230213A as a benchmark, they find sensitivity to dimensionful LIV coefficients roughly in the range\n\n\\[\n10^{-24}\\lesssim\n\\delta_{\\nu,1}/{\\rm eV^{-1}}\n\\lesssim10^{-22}.\n\\]\n\nLarger values can generate too many neutrinos in energy intervals already constrained by IceCube and Auger.\n\nThe important caveat is explicitly acknowledged by the authors: **one KM3NeT event does not permit a formal LIV constraint**, and the inferred sensitivity depends strongly on the assumed UHECR source population.\n\n**Significance:** the study connects UHECR propagation to cosmogenic-neutrino phenomenology, but its physical conclusion remains exploratory rather than a formal bound.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting/speculative phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10931", "arxiv": "2609.10931", "title": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T00:36:30Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10931", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.10931 IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos #### ★★★ 1. IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event\n\n**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.10931](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10931)\n\nIceCube uses **15 years of data** to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around \\(200\\) PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event, and a transient occurring at an arbitrary time. They find **no evidence for neutrino emission in any search** and derive point-source flux limits. The important conclusion is that if KM3-230213A came from an astrophysical point source, the simplest interpretation in which that source produces an \\(E^{-2}\\)-like flux at the normalization inferred from the single KM3NeT event is now **strongly constrained**, both for steady and transient scenarios.\n\n**Significance:** this significantly sharpens the astrophysical interpretation problem surrounding the KM3NeT event. The combination of one extraordinarily energetic KM3NeT neutrino and a long IceCube null exposure is becoming an interesting consistency test rather than merely an “extreme-energy neutrino” story.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new experimental constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11833", "arxiv": "2609.11833", "title": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations", "authors": "Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T17:25:40Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11833", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper 2609.11833 The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter #### ★★★ 2. Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations\n\n**Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper**  \n[arXiv:2609.11833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11833)\n\nThe recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events expected from conventional elastic WIMPs. Nguyen, Linden and Hooper point out an independent astroparticle constraint: such particles can be captured in the Sun, annihilate there, and generate **high-energy solar neutrinos**. IceCube sees no corresponding excess.\n\nTheir conclusion is that IceCube solar-neutrino observations **strongly constrain a substantial part of the inelastic-DM parameter space relevant to the LZ event**. This tests the recoil interpretation through an independent messenger.\n\nFor perspective, LZ itself reports only **2.6σ local significance**, approximately a \\(0.5\\%\\) background probability, and explicitly does **not** claim a DM discovery.\n\n**Significance:** a multimessenger connection between direct detection, solar capture and neutrino-telescope observations.\n\n**Classification:** **new phenomenological constraint on interpretations of the LZ candidate.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10668", "arxiv": "2609.10668", "title": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos", "authors": "Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10668", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari 2609.10668 The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM #### ★★★ 3. The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos\n\n**Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari**  \n[arXiv:2609.10668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10668)\n\nThe setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size\n\n\\[\nR \\sim 0.1-10~\\mu{\\rm m},\n\\]\n\nwith Majorana fermions propagating in the bulk and masses in roughly the \\(1-10\\) keV regime. The authors explicitly diagonalize the resulting neutrino/Kaluza–Klein system and then confront it with three rather different observables:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Daya Bay oscillations},\\qquad\n\\text{KATRIN } \\beta\\text{-decay},\\qquad\n\\text{KamLAND-Zen } 0\\nu\\beta\\beta .\n\\]\n\nThe technically interesting point is the neutrinoless-double-beta-decay calculation. They derive a closed expression for the effective Majorana mass including the KK tower and find that the tower can **screen the \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) amplitude** once the characteristic nuclear momentum exceeds the compactification and Majorana scales. Thus adding a tower of Majorana states does *not* simply add positive contributions to \\(m_{\\beta\\beta}\\); destructive structure emerges from the complete spectrum.\n\nWithin their preferred Dark-Dimension parameter window, **KATRIN becomes the strongest terrestrial constraint**, pushing the relevant Yukawa coupling to approximately\n\n\\[\ny\\lesssim {\\cal O}(10^{-3}),\n\\]\n\nwhile oscillation experiments and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) probe complementary regions of the Majorana-mass parameter space.\n\n**Significance:** it is an unusually clean example where oscillations, direct kinematic neutrino-mass measurements and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) constrain the *same explicit BSM neutrino construction*. The KK screening of \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) is a distinctive feature of the calculation.\n\n**Classification:** **new neutrino/BSM phenomenology; high-interest theory paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10784", "arxiv": "2609.10784", "title": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data", "authors": "Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T19:37:43Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10784", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri 2609.10784 The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM #### ★★☆ 4. Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data\n\n**Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri**  \n[arXiv:2609.10784](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10784)\n\nThe authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom **CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field**. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated *along* magnetic-field structures rather than deflected in the ordinary \\(q\\,\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B\\) sense, their trajectories develop what the authors call **Galactic magnetic funnels**, producing strongly anisotropic arrival directions. They then compare the predicted directional/energy distributions with Pierre Auger data.\n\nThe data generally favor **zero monopole contribution**, and the profile-likelihood analysis yields a \\(90\\%\\) C.L. integral flux limit below roughly\n\n\\[\n2.1\\times10^{-23}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** beyond monopole phenomenology, the study develops methods for modelling a highly anisotropic exotic charged population propagating through the GMF.\n\n**Classification:** **new simulation + Auger constraint; niche but technically interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10690", "arxiv": "2609.10690", "title": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials", "authors": "Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:05Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10690", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer 2609.10690 The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 5. EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials\n\n**Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer**  \n[arXiv:2609.10690](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10690)\n\nThe issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of **Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation** ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in nonrelativistic EFT and by explicitly regularizing the potential at short distance.\n\nFor a pseudoscalar mediator coupled to massive Dirac DM, an important result is that a **weakly coupled UV-complete theory need not generate Sommerfeld enhancement at all**. Conversely, derivative/higher-dimensional interactions can produce enhancement and bound states at weak coupling when there is a sufficiently large hierarchy between the EFT cutoff and \\(m_\\chi\\). In that case the enhancement retains genuine UV sensitivity.\n\n**Significance:** it is a useful warning against blindly inserting a singular potential into the Schrödinger equation and interpreting the resulting large enhancement as robust DM phenomenology.\n\n**Classification:** **conceptual/methodological advance rather than a new observational result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10674", "arxiv": "2609.10674", "title": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter", "authors": "Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10674", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster 2609.10674 Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★★☆ 6. Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter\n\n**Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster**  \n[arXiv:2609.10674](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10674)\n\nDifferent JWST searches for the infrared line from \\(a\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\) had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly **an order of magnitude**. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible non-parametric inference, concluding that some of the strongest previously claimed sensitivities are simply not achievable with the datasets used.\n\nTheir robust analysis nevertheless excludes QCD-axion DM over approximately\n\n\\[\nm_a\\simeq 0.5-2.5\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\nand gives complementary ALP constraints at \\(0.1-0.5\\) eV. Importantly, they argue that the present analyses are approaching a **systematics floor**, so simply accumulating more exposure will not give the naive statistical improvement unless JWST instrumental modelling and reduction pipelines improve.\n\n**Significance:** not “new physics discovered,” but exactly the kind of systematic reassessment that determines whether a spectacular exclusion plot is actually trustworthy.\n\n**Classification:** **important re-analysis/systematics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10694", "arxiv": "2609.10694", "title": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions", "authors": "Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:06Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10694", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger 2609.10694 Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 7. Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions\n\n**Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger**  \n[arXiv:2609.10694](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10694)\n\nUsing **3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to \\(\\sim30\\) s after collapse**, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm kick}\\sim10^2-10^3~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nsubstantially larger than the weak BH kicks often assumed.\n\nThree effects contribute:\n\n\\[\n\\text{asymmetric electromagnetic outflow},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{magnetic-flux eruptions},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{gravitational pull from asymmetric ejecta}.\n\\]\n\nFaster-spinning BHs tend to receive larger and more spin-aligned kicks. Particularly strong asymmetries occur when the system undergoes a delayed transition into a **magnetically arrested disk (MAD)** state.\n\nThe astrophysical connection is that the strong coherent magnetic flux required to generate these kicks is also precisely the ingredient invoked for relativistic **GRB jets and jet-driven hypernovae**. Thus strong BH kicks might be an intrinsic consequence of some collapsar engines rather than an independent phenomenon.\n\nConsequences include BH retention in clusters, binary disruption, spin-orbit misalignment and formation channels of high-spin BHs in or near the pair-instability mass gap.\n\n**Significance:** this connects GRB-engine MHD directly to the compact-object populations measured by gravitational waves.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new numerical result; strong high-energy-astrophysics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10736", "arxiv": "2609.10736", "title": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis", "authors": "D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:31:56Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10736", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen 2609.10736 This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure. 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 8. Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis\n\n**D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen**  \n[arXiv:2609.10736](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10736)\n\nTeglon takes a three-dimensional GW localization posterior and combines it with a **3D galaxy-completeness model**, producing a revised probability map informed by the known galaxy distribution. It can then ingest an arbitrary telescope footprint and continuously interpolate between pure galaxy targeting and ordinary sky tiling.\n\nThe clever extension is integration with **Redback**, allowing pixel-by-pixel detection efficiencies to be computed for transient models. The pipeline evaluates more than\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{source\\ is\\ here}),\n\\]\n\nbut effectively\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{detect}\\mid\\mathrm{source\\ here},\\,\n\\mathrm{instrument},\\,t,\\,\\mathrm{transient\\ model}).\n\\]\n\nThis supports an assessment of the detection efficiency of a follow-up campaign.\n\nThey report particularly substantial gains for telescopes with fields of view \\(\\lesssim1~{\\rm deg}^2\\), and Teglon has already been used through O3/O4 and is being prepared for O5.\n\n**Significance:** the tool quantitatively connects GW posterior volumes to gamma-ray, X-ray and optical counterpart searches.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological/tool paper, not a new astrophysical result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11885", "arxiv": "2609.11885", "title": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes", "authors": "Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T17:49:31Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11885", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni 2609.11885 Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★☆ 9. Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes\n\n**Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni**  \n[arXiv:2609.11885](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11885)\n\nInstead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population\n\n\\[\np(z,m_1,m_2)\n\\]\n\nwith a **normalizing flow embedded directly inside the hierarchical GW likelihood**. Event posterior samples propagate individual-event uncertainty, while injection campaigns encode detector selection effects.\n\nRestricting the inference to the region sufficiently sampled by injections can itself create an apparent correlation between mass and redshift. They therefore introduce a mutual-information diagnostic comparing the inferred distribution with a redshift-independent reference subject to the *same support restriction*.\n\nApplied to **GWTC-5.0**, the reconstruction independently recovers population features near\n\n\\[\nm_1\\sim10\\,M_\\odot,\\qquad 35\\,M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nbut finds **no significant evidence that the intrinsic primary-mass distribution evolves with redshift**.\n\n**Significance:** mainly methodology—the warning about spurious correlations induced by selection/support boundaries is applicable well beyond gravitational-wave inference.\n\n**Classification:** **strong methodological work; result itself is broadly consistent with existing LVK population conclusions.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10671", "arxiv": "2609.10671", "title": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter", "authors": "Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an…", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T18:00:00Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-10671", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou 2609.10671 They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Dark matter #### ★☆☆ 10. Ultralight Axial Dark Matter\n\n**Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou**  \n[arXiv:2609.10671](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10671)\n\nThey explore ultralight DM as an **axial vector field** rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an analogue of the axion-photon coupling, though its consistent realization requires a photon mass, and discuss signatures involving polarimetry, superconducting cavities, electric fields and the CMB. The massive axial vector description is dual to a massive two-form/Kalb–Ramond field.\n\n**Significance:** an expansion of the ultralight-DM model space with unusual experimental signatures.\n\n**Classification:** **new theoretical framework/model-building.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11511", "arxiv": "2609.11511", "title": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly", "authors": "Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH…", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-10T13:18:16Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-11", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/11/index.html#paper-2609-11511", "reportDate": "11 September 2026", "searchText": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan 2609.11511 The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH… 2026-09-11 11 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter Multimessenger #### ★☆☆ 11. Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly\n\n**Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan**  \n[arXiv:2609.11511](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11511)\n\nThe proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the **terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole**. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH lifetime and initial mass required to evaporate at the present epoch, while the final \\(z\\simeq0\\) phase becomes essentially Schwarzschild. They argue that this can produce a large local \\(\\sim220\\) PeV neutrino signal while avoiding excessive diffuse early-time emission.\n\nThis theoretical interpretation is **highly model-dependent**, and the IceCube point-source search further constrains source interpretations of KM3-230213A.\n\n**Significance:** the paper explores an exotic origin for the KM3NeT event, with viability dependent on the underlying PBH and source assumptions.\n\n**Classification:** **speculative interpretation.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10176", "arxiv": "2609.10176", "title": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT", "authors": "L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Fermi-LAT observations reveal three extended gamma-ray components near Westerlund 1 and Kes 41, with interpretations involving local cosmic rays or diffuse-background modelling.", "primary_category": "astro-ph.HE", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T13:44:10Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-10", "original_report_date": "2026-09-12", "path": "2026/09/10/index.html#paper-2609-10176", "reportDate": "10 September 2026", "searchText": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu 2609.10176 Fermi-LAT observations reveal three extended gamma-ray components near Westerlund 1 and Kes 41, with interpretations involving local cosmic rays or diffuse-background modelling. 2026-09-10 10 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger #### ★★★ 1. Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT\n\n**L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu**  \n[arXiv:2609.10176](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10176)\n\nUsing **more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above \\(0.8\\) GeV**, the authors identify **three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components** in the Westerlund 1/Kes 41 region. One is associated with the natal cloud of Kes 41 and is statistically preferred over the previously used point-source description. Two others overlap neutral gas within roughly \\(100\\) pc of the edge of the Westerlund 1 superbubble.\n\nThere is an important caveat: they cannot yet cleanly distinguish **real locally injected CR populations** from residual mismodelling of the Galactic interstellar emission. If the components are physical, the Kes 41 emission requires \\(\\lesssim5\\%\\) of the SNR energy budget in accelerated particles, while the two Wd1 components correspond to a gamma-ray luminosity of roughly \\(10^{-4}\\) of the cluster-wind mechanical power.\n\n**Significance:** this is precisely the sort of intermediate-scale CR-transport phenomenology that can alter how one interprets TeV–PeV emission and neutrino expectations from stellar-cluster environments.\n\n**Classification:** **new observational result; interpretation not yet unique.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10636", "arxiv": "2609.10636", "title": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon", "authors": "XinXin Qi, Hao Sun", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A light-mediator inelastic-scalar model tests whether nonthermal solar capture can reconcile the LZ recoil candidate with solar-neutrino constraints.", "primary_category": "hep-ph", "submitted_utc": "2026-09-09T10:43:05Z", "arxiv_listing_date": "2026-09-10", "original_report_date": "2026-09-13", "path": "2026/09/10/index.html#paper-2609-10636", "reportDate": "10 September 2026", "searchText": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon XinXin Qi, Hao Sun 2609.10636 A light-mediator inelastic-scalar model tests whether nonthermal solar capture can reconcile the LZ recoil candidate with solar-neutrino constraints. 2026-09-10 10 September 2026 Multimessenger #### ★★★ 2. Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon\n\n**XinXin Qi, Hao Sun**  \n[arXiv:2609.10636](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10636)\n\nThis light-mediator scalar model tests how nonthermal solar capture affects the interpretation of the LZ recoil candidate.\n\nThe authors construct a concrete anomaly-free\n\n\\[\nU(1)_{B-3L_\\tau}\n\\]\n\nmodel containing an \\(840\\) GeV complex scalar DM particle undergoing an endothermic transition with splitting\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\simeq 320~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\n\nmediated by a \\(160\\) MeV vector. Their benchmark gives about \\(0.96\\) expected accepted events in their reconstruction of the high-energy LZ search, i.e. roughly the scale required to interpret the single \\(248\\) keV LZ recoil candidate.\n\nThe calculation follows the evolution of DM after solar capture.\n\nInstead of assuming rapid thermalization into a compact central DM distribution, they evolve the captured particles in energy, angular momentum and internal state, including thermal nuclear motion, finite scattering rates and excited-state decay. Because the scattering is endothermic, the particles eventually reach orbital configurations for which further up-scattering is kinematically forbidden.\n\nThey find a nonthermal population that effectively **stalls around**\n\n\\[\nr_{\\rm eff}\\simeq0.194-0.198\\,R_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nrather than thermalizing in the solar core. The resulting annihilation rate is only\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\rm ann}\\sim1.3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nwhich in their benchmark is more than three orders of magnitude below their IceCube-based neutrino proxy.\n\n> Solar-neutrino constraints on *electroweak* inelastic DM cannot automatically be carried over to every inelastic-DM realization.\n\nThis paper provides an explicit light-mediator scalar counterexample.\n\n**Significance:** the lesson is broader than the LZ anomaly. Whenever one maps DM capture to a neutrino signal, the usual capture–thermalization–annihilation equilibrium assumption can fail badly if the scattering has nontrivial kinematic thresholds.\n\n**Classification:** **new model-specific phenomenology; particularly valuable as a counterpoint to broad solar-neutrino exclusion arguments.**\n\n---"}];
